      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPIIK</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPIIK</p>
        <p>WHAT HE WANTED.—MamMs : "NO ; you've bad enough cake." The Four-Year•Old " mamma, please can't I have too much?" THE difference between a wit and a humourist is that a wit says things and a humourist writes them. "HE says his wife has witching ways." "I believe it. I hear she has a habit of riding over htri with the broom•stick."</p>
        <p>_ WIG: "Jones says that he can read a woman like a book." Wag: "Yea; but he can't shut her up like one." Moxiikk: "I am surprised, my dear, that you stiffer a man to kiss you!" Daughter: "But, mamma, I don't call it suffering." OCTWkIGIntn.—He "Your life with me would 131 a path oi rose." She: " Yes ; but with Jack Wheeler it would be a cycle path." Emma: "And, Charlie, dear, would you have really ehot yourself if I had refused you?" Charlie: "Inded I would. I had already sent to four gusasmiths for price lists of revolvers."</p>
        <p>A LITERARY CONFERENCE. Sweet Sixteen: "Isn't the hero grand?" Sweet Seventeen: "Isn't he? Why, he is so noble that I was posi- tively jealous of the heroine." HOW HE MANAGED Ir.—Willis "KU Dr. fikinner a good-paying practice?" Wallace: "Oh, yes. His patients are never out of danger until they're paid up." GETTING UP SOCIALLY.---" She is certainly rising in the social scale." "Is she ?" " Oh, yes, indeed 1 She is snubbed by a better class of people each succeeding year." AN IMPORTANT DIFTERENCE.—Sopwith : " Loin- bardo says there is no difference between genius and madness. ' Waggle: "Pardon me; madness gets three square meals a day." MAUD (earnestly) : "I want to ask you a ques. Von, George." George (also earnestly) : "What is it, dearest ? " Maud (still earnestly) : "If you had never met me, would you have loved me Just the same?"</p>
        <p>His OviNioN.—She "What have you to say of a girl who ino.rriea a man to reform him?" He: "I think she's spoiling a mighty good wife for some other fellow."</p>
        <p>As TO A CLIENT.—" Ha is the most obstinate man I ever saw," said the first lawyer. "Is he?" said his friend. "Yes. He hates to take advice even when he pays for it." WORSE YET—We should at once cease to cry out about mothers spoiling their sons, and, be thankful that so few boys, comparatively, are brought up by their grandmothers.</p>
        <p>- - - THE STATUS.— She: "Just look at Mr. and Mrs. Young! Their honeymoan is evidently over." He: Yes. I suppose matrimony has resolved itself into a search for a modus vivendi."</p>
        <p>"MY cheeks are all on fire." "I thought I emelt burning paint I" . .</p>
        <p>ELIZABETH (vishing town friends): "My I Here's her visitin' card." Elizabeth's Husband : " What does it say?" Eiizabeth : "It says she's at home Thursdays. I wonder where she stops the rest of the time?</p>
        <p>NOTHING Seems to arouse our scorn 80 much as X person who appears to be well satisfied with him- self.</p>
        <p>_ I; that open door in China is not allowed to remain open, somebody's &amp;gess may be caught in the jamb. Tut Vimoit : "Busy, old man ?" Modern Author: "Buty ! Why, I'm writing so much that I don't have time to think." _ _ _</p>
        <p>A REASONABLE QUERY.—"That fellow takes things that) do not belong to'him." "Ah ! Ama- teur photographer or thief?" YES, INDEED.—LittIe Elmer: "Paps, what is a genius?" Professor Broadhead: "A genius, my eon, is a man who knows whenhe has said enough."</p>
        <p>AIIArEUR PALISIIST : "The life line indicates how long you will live." Sceptical Friend: "Yes? Isn't it a wonder the life insurance companies pay no attention to it ?"</p>
        <p>OFFICIAL RECEIVER (at a meeting of creditors) : "What have you eome here for?" Professional Beggar: 'To put in my claim. Mr. Jones used to give me twopence every week." PIONEERS.—She : "And Chillbse civilisation is very old. is it not?" He: "Very. China was the first nation in which women wore shoes which were too small for them." _</p>
        <p>ACUTE SYMPTOMS.—" Are you sure you love that ?" "Well, I can't work in the morning until I get a letter from her, and after I get it I can'S work."</p>
        <p>" PRESIDE:CT KRUGER is worth £5,000,000, and yet his wife does the cooking," said the lodger. "Great Scott !" exclaimed the landlord. 'ls there a servant-girl famine in the Transvaal, too?" _ _</p>
        <p>HALVES AND QUARTERS.—QtIerieIIS : "Let's See ; the married men all have better halves, don't they ? " Cyn icus ? "Yes." Querieus : "Then what do the bachelors have?" Cynicus : "Better quarters !"</p>
        <p>BESIDE THE LAKE. I thought she gave me cause to doubt, But soon I blessed my fates: For though my rival took her out, 'Twas I put on her skates. THEIR. NARROW ESCAPE.—Mrs. M'Gorry : "01 never was o frightened in ahl me loife ! Sure the car-r.r missed me be less than six inches !" M'Gorry : "Av yez had gone a step farther the children wud hov hod a stepmother." In 1991.—First Incubated Chicken: "Do you know it is said that our ancestors were originally hatched out by ordinary hens?" Second Incu- bated Chicken: "Nonsense ! I don't believe in any such absurd Darwinian theory !" No MORE DISSIMULATION.—Young Bridegroom: "Darling, I think I should like to take your little brother with us to N:agara Falls." Bride: "How kind that would be cf you, Harry !" Young Bridegroom: " Yes ; I should like to push him over into the biggest of them." NOT A VIOLENT CASE.—Mrs. Peck: "Henry, what would you do if I were to die suddenly ? " Henry: "Pray dou't talk of such a thing. I think it would almost drive me crazy." Mrs. Peck: "Do you think you would marry again?" Henry: "Oh, no. I don't think I would ba as crazy as that."</p>
        <p>A BURGLAR who had entered a minister's house at midnight was disturbed by the awakening of the occupant of the room he was in. Drawing his knife, he said: "If you stir, you are a dead man. I'm hunting for money." "Let me get up and strike a light," said the minister, "and I'll hunt with you.' "GOOD morning, uncle. How are yeu to-day V' " Ah, nephew, I am well. How are you?" "First rate. What do you think brought me here?" "Oh, the old story, I suppose "I'll bet you fi,,e shillings you can't guess?" "I'll take lb! You came here to borrow some money." "You've lost I Pay me the five shillings! I only wanted to inquire how aunt is to-day !" RETALIATION —" Well," said Miss.Fosdick,with a satisfied ci,, "I flatter myself that I have revenged myself upon that supercilious Miss Thorndyke Van Rensselaer." "How did you do it? Didn't you Invite her to your tea?" asked Miss Keedick. "Oh, yes, I invited her, but I spelt her name wrongly on the envelope, and also on the list of guests that I gave to -.the society papers."</p>
      </div>
