        <p>BREAKING IT GENTLY.—Judge Lynch's court having attended to Ole case of one Bilious Pete in the usual characteristic and conclusive manner. Alkali Ike was selected to break the news as gently as possible to the bereaved widow. "Howdy do, mom ?" he saluted, when the lady had come to the door in response to his knock. "I've just dropped around to sorer tell you that—er—er—your hus- band ain't a-goin' to live very long." " What makes you think he ain't?" returned the lady, in considerable surprise. " B'cuz he's dead now.' "How in the world did you ever come to promise to be his wife if you didn't love him?" "Welt, he went down on his knees and said he loved the ground I walked on." "Of course. They all do that if they think it's necessary." "Still I said no. Then he said he would die unless I promised to be his." " I hope you didn't accept him witq the idea of saving his life." 's No. I told him he was only wasting his time pleading with me, and advised him to go and try to find some other girl that he could love. Then he arose, and cried: Ah, you are as beautiful as an angel, but you ars heartless.'" " Well?" "Well, after that I didn't have the heart to say no." .e..rteza ta skoeurity 5 fiL rem krop.... the toettbr6., 'very' mamas,'</p>
