      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>ILLUSTRATED FASHIONS,</head>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED FASHIONS,</p>
        <p>The summer sale season is now in full swing, and this year bargains are real and numerous. There are very few of the beat shops which are not now announcing goods which are to be " sacrificed " or "greatly reduced," and the bargain hunters are following their favourite pursuit. There are some people to whom these periodical sales are a real boon, as they provide them with a chance of buying good articles at a small cost, but there are again others who seem to have a mania for acquiring bargains whether they have any use for the same or not. It is very foolish, however, to set forth on a visit to a sale without having made a note previously of what is really wanted, and how c-1 money can be safely spent. It is generally sa e plan to avoid investing in articles which may "come in," as that is only wasting money. Of course odd lengths of coloured silks or dainty— lace or embroidery are pretty safe purchases, as they can be used for blouses, collars, fronts or neck, or waist arrangements. Always avoid sternly, however attractive and cheap it may be, any article which is likely to date itself by following closely in the footsteps of the particular fashion of the summer. One can often pick up alpaca costumes of a plain style, linen gowns, neat skirts of soft voiles or cashmere cut fully and gracefully for wear with blouses during the next few months, or, in some instances, with bodice material in- cluded.</p>
        <p>Summer sales are now fixed for so early a date that (especially with such continued wet weather as prevailed early this summer) the buying of cotton frocks can be left till their commencement, Quantities of made-up robes, that is with the skirt ready shaped and finished</p>
