      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>BIJAI3IEIt TOILETTE.</head>
        <p>A BIJAI3IEIt TOILETTE.</p>
        <p>to be mounted into a band, together with material for the bodice, are offered by most of the big shops, and the summer toilette sketched is one of these_ robes made of pale green muslin trimmed with Valenciennes lace edging and lace insertion. The dress is made over a separate lining of green of the same tone. This colour is always cool and pleasant to wear and to behold, and is becoming to most people. It is only those with a very sallow complexion who must eschew its charms. Of course, although .1 cannot refrain from singing the praises of +these robes, yet anyone who can make her own dresses would do far better to buy the material by the yard. The quality is better and the variety of patterns to select from infinitely greater. Twelve yards are ample for a simple cotton or muslin gown, and this amount of material will allow of a full and slightly trained skirt, with two or three little cross-cut frills at the hem, and a full gathered blouse bodice. Swiss embroidery or linen lace goes best with the fashionable soft-surfaced linen gowns, and just now one can secure short lengths suitable for a yoke piece very cheaply. Pastel coloured linens are very smart for the country or the seaside. Stiff, hard linens arm hardly worth the making up, so quickly do they crush and get out of order. The pretty hat sketched is made of a rustic straw trimmed with tea roses and a little fine lace. It has a wide, shady brim which protects the eyes and is most becoming. The prettiest hats for seaside or country wear are of</p>
