      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>PHETTY HAT.</head>
        <p>A PHETTY HAT.</p>
        <p>several orders. First among them is the Panama, which is becoming almost objection- ably übiquitous. All sorts and conditions of women appear in this kind of hat, and it is few of them, indeed, who possess the art of wearing a studiously plain hat such as the Panama to advantap e. Its charms, alas ! become too often more obvious in the breach than in the observ- ance. It is difficult to distinguish between the Panama which costs two guineas and that valued at only half-a-guinea. Very pretty and less oommon are the Tuscan or Yeddah hats, which lend themselves readily to becoming curves. These are sometimes trimmed solely by a swathing of coloured tulle or chiffon arranged round the low crown and tied loosely below the chin or at one side.</p>
        <p>The blouse whose charms are illustrated is formed of creamy cashmere with a wide collar finished with pale pink coloured embroidery and a little vest of guipure lace. The style is one which would look well for a boating excur- sion or picnic, and the soft pliability of the</p>
        <p>BLOUSE. cashmere prevents it from crushing easily as does a muslin. There is a distinct tendency this summer towards the use of soft woollen materials such as sere or cashmere, but amongst the , prettiest of the more diaphanous fabrics is crystalline, a silky wavy material which does not crush or soil easily and yet. looks soft and filmy. It makes up into the most lovely gowns trimmed with coarse grass lawn lace, or deep coloured Irish guipure. Black and white seem to have caught the popular fancy, and in Paris especially are much in vogue. There is always something very smart in the combination of these two contrast- ing colours which keeps them ever snore or less in the front of the smartestwitodes. Coque/rev.</p>
