      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Once more our thoughts are turned to the Coronation, but it is rather in a chastened spirit that the great event will be regarded by the eeneral public, and rejoicing will naturally be tempered with devout thankfulness for his Majesty's restoration to health. Instead of making it the occasion for show and revelry, the nation will be more anxious to learn that the ceremony is not like ly to overtax the King's powers of endurance. The service in the Abbey will probably not occupy more than an hour and a half, but there is some apprehension that it will entail an undue strain on his Majesty, in spite of Public confidence in the doctors. Everyone however, will devoutly hope that this will not be so. The chiet concern of the nation is, that the Coronation should in no way retard his Majesty's complete restoration to health and that, after the experiences of the past few weeks, it should be held of more sacred and historic eiesnificanee, than made the Occasion for feastingel and junketings. The fact that Mr Balfour is the first bachelor Premier siace Pitt, has aroused a good deal of comment, and it , is even suggested that this state of things may have a Ratio's! importance. Everyone knowe that the late Mrs Gladstone was a source of strength and support to her husband, net only for the love and devotion she gave to his personal comfort, but also for the great interest she ' took in his political career. Lord Selisbury was equally fortunate in the wifely help of the late Marchioness', but very little is popularly known about Lady Beaconsfield, the wife of his predecessor in office. The story that she was originally a factory girl in bare feet when Mr Wyndham Lewis married and educated her,is generally discredited, though her paternal parentage is obscure. It was, as the wealthy widow of Mr Lewis that she married Mr Disraeli whose great attachment to his wife was often commonly remarked. She however died in 1874, without ever coming prominently before the public as did the late Mrs Gladstone, and Lady Salisbury. Mr A. J. Balfour has no love for foreign travel, and seldom figures amongst those who spend their holidays abroad or stay at their villas on the Mediterranean. The Prime Minister is usually satisfied with the beauties and attractions of his native land, and it is not unlikely that this preference will be shared by two Englishwomen, who were recently in Switzerland. On the evidence ef a child who had lost bee watch they war* arrested in the dining room of their hotel, were subjected to the indignity of being searched, and were then locked up for twenty-four hours before being released on bail. Finally they were dismissed without compensation or apology. for the outrage inflicted upon them,—fer this is the only terra to apply te such official stupidity and brutality en the part of the pollee. Probably these two ladies will give Switzerland a wide berth in future, and the Swiss people cannel be surprised if other holiday-makers from this country, do the same. In choosing and packing clothes fit the country or the seaside, it is desirable to bear in mind the fact that holi- day wear is hard wear. A good Pine finished blue serge skirt—made to clear the ground—will stand anything in the way of dust, mud, rain, or seawater, and is indis- pensable to the girl or woman who intends to hove "a good time." A neat little coat to match should be in- vested In, and to the list of dainty linen shirts to wear with such a costume, a couple of flannelette blouses should be added for wear on cooler days. A thoroughly well-fitting, yet easy pair of boots, is another very neces- sary item in the holiday outfit, also a good plain straw hat, which in some measure shades; the eyes, and which boasts little in the way of trinaming,save a bow of ribbon or a silk scarf. Regarding toilettes of the dressy descrip- tion, each holiday-maker is the best judge of what she will require in this direction, but those of us who desire to make the most of perhaps cur one and bard-earr holiday in the year, are well advised to burden outsets,' s with as little baggage of this kind as possible. White is the favourite wear both for morning and afternoon toilettes, among fashionable women, and for yachting or the seaside, serge, alpaca, mohair, and we urine flannel, are the fabrics most employed. Not Infrequently rid cravats, belts, shoos, undershirts, en-' tout-eas, or sunshades, and headgear, are worn with these white gowrie, the effect in the generality of cases being particularly erred. The " military overcoat," and the" Napoleonic clerk," in thick white cloth, go with such costumes for click wear, the former for day use when necessary, the latter being preferred for evening, when entirely white toilettes are again the vogue.. How to protect the face and hair from dust when motoring is a protlern apparently difficult of solution. First One description of veiling is recommended to us, then another, but the result is always—failure. Tr(' very latest idea lea kind elf hood which fits closely reund the face, and ties under the chin. In protecting one's hair, it is of course a success, but this headgear does nothing for the face, as regards dust or shade, and more- over it in appallingiy unbecoming. So far we can do nothing better than remain faithful to our small closely- fitting Melee, and thick gauze veil, which, when tied behind, should be arreneed in thick folds over the hair, and well secured, the ends being drawn to the front and arranged in a bow under the chin. Not only horses, but fashionable dogs also are being attired in sun bonnets. Those who have much to do with horses are almost unanimous in their opinion that sun bonnets cause a great deal more discomfort than they prevent. Nature has a way of its own in provid- ing for such matters, and as the horse came originally from the sun-baked plains of Tartary, it is pretty cer- tain that the animal was never intended to wear arti- ficial headgear. It is the same with dogs, only in their case a sense of the ridiculous is combined with senti- mental kindness, for nothing looks more funny than the fashionable woman with her toy net in a sunhat, for all the world looking like "Toby " of the old-fashioned Punch and Judy show. In London the common house fly has not been the pest that it usually is in the summer time. Whether this is due to the cold spring, or other ciuses, it has not yet invaded our kitchens and dwelling rooms in such num- bers as we expect at this time of the year. Probably he is only waiting for more genial conditions. Still we have had sufficiently hot days to bring out the mosquito, though even that creature does not seem so flourishing as in recent summers.</p>
        <p>Although the protest comes a little late, yet it is well that a lady's paper ehould have printed a condemnation of l'ahr aiming "as a fashionable amusement. The idea of making an exhibition of people's poverty is repulsive, and it tips a further disadvantage which is perhaps not in the minds of the ladies who visit the slums. Since this form of entertainment first came into vogue, a new class has arisen which may be described as the profes- sional poor. Their homes present a ghastly picture of penury and want, but the situation in many instances has been specially created with a view to the charitable donations of ladies who may visit the locality for the purpose of seeing " bow the poor live."</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>[ALL RIGHTS RESHRVHD.</head>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESHRVHD.</p>
        <p>TWO PARIAHS:</p>
        <p>A TALE,</p>
        <p>BY FERDINAND VON SAAR.</p>
        <p>PART I.</p>
        <p>One of the most remarkable railway construc- tions is that leading across the Semmering, a part of the Vorich Alps, which constitute the boundary between the Archduchy of Austria and green Styria. Whoever in days gone by (now the impression is no more the same) first travelled by that railway, winding along yawning precipices and steep rocks, the train thundering over dizzy viaducts, or on a sudden, rushing with shrill whistle into the night of a seemingly endless tunnel, has felt his admira- tion raised to that sort of pathetic awe which we always feel on seeing realised what, until now, we had considered to be impossible. And when the coupled coaches, having once more reached even ground, are sweeping softly over smiling pastures, proud to belong to a century creating such wonder- works, the traveller will lean back on his cushions, and with his eyes half closed, his dreams may carry him on to the time of a future generation, which, even after having excavated the Suez and pierced the Mont Cenis, will not consider itself excluded from still bolder aspirations. But there is a King, a government, which but few think of. I mean the thousands and thou- sands of men, who bending under their hardships, exposed to all sorts of dangers, have been mining rocks, moving blocks, bridging over abysses, and in fact creating that highly prized road, by means of which, any of my gentle readers, who, like myself, live in the boisterous, dust-covered metro- polis on the banks of the Danube, may be carried with the changes of their thought even to the h ores of the blue Adriatic. Of two such poor beings, who, themselves ex- cluded from the blessings of progress, yet ever faithfully helped in the great labour of the civili- sation of the world, I am now about to tell the story. Not forsooth with the intention of painting in sharp lines the hardihood'of those contrasts to Society who are erecting our cathedrals and palaces, , our universities and museums, nor, on the other hand, with that of hinting at the part which, in the course of events, the fifth state may yet be called upon to act—an enterprise which the author must leave for the Socialist—but only to set down a simple picture, taken from the lives of many of those workers who, existing over-burdened by physical labour, fighting, as it were, for their piece of daily bread, are wearing away, unknown and unnoticed, until at last they die off in some lost and miserable corner of this earth.</p>
        <p>_ It is my intention to show that sorrow and rapture are moving every human heart, and. that the great tragedy of the world is being played in its nl-qthnrnble recesses.</p>
        <p>Toe rail wu.v over the Semmering was finished; the noise caused- by the working, the thundering of the blasts, were hushed; and the numerous and restless throng of men aTsembled there, from far-off Bohemia, and the lowlands of Hungary and Moravia —the strong Gurst and the open-handed Frank— had removed_ southward, there to continue their day's troublesome task. The deer, frightened away deep into the woods, were corning back, ven- turing out, as if driven by curiosity, on the gigantic mountain-path, which yet unscarred by the pres- sure of iron wheels was lying in the peaceful recesses of the Alps, like some forlorn vestige of human energy. Here and there at a distance of about four miles from each other stood a few of those spacious huts which had been inhabited by whole tribes of those nomads of human labour. They were now sheltering some of those who had remained, and some too, who, having come later, were now here for the purpose of finishing the construction up to the heights. In several places there still remained rails to be laid out, tracks to be gravelled, posts to be erected, and signal boxes to gravelled, finished under the eaves of which graceful swallows, in long files, spending their days on the electric wires, had meanwhile been building their nests.</p>
        <p>On the threshold of one of these huts, which lay somewhat removed from the railway, with its back against the rugged rock, there sat one Sunday afternoon a young woman. She was barefooted ; her head was wrapped in a coarse handkerchief, and her face seemed faded from even that brownish Qolour which the scalding rays of a hot sun cause pale faces to assume. Deep lines furrowed her forehead, and round her mouth there was a forlorn expression which probably made her look older than she really was and at the same time strangely accentuated the gi;lishness of her figure. The sun was setting, dark and silent shadows were spread- ing over summits and slopes,. But in the meadows, and over the tops of the woods which wound along one side of the landscape, the sun's clear rays were still glittering and sparkling to the satisfaction of a whole world of butterflies, bees, and dragon-flies. The lonely woman paid no attention, however, to the magnificence of summer spreading before her eyes. She never looked up from a man's worsted jacket upon the mending of which she was wholly engrossed. Her task appeared to be a very trying one. Her rough, red hands, which plied the needle but reluctantly and with evident awkwardness seemed to all appearances only used to dealing with hoe and shovel. The girl was roused by approach- ing footsteps. She raised her head and saw a man coming from the railway side, towards the cottage. His appearance presented a pitiful sight. His figure was small and mean, he wore a worn-out soldier's frock at orce too long and too broad for him. It hung loosely from his body, whilst a faded blue soldier's cap lay over his matted hair. His gait was tottering, though he was leaning on a thick stick, and the small threadbare canvas sack he carried flaw,° over his shoulder did not appear to be overfilled. Looking out of a pair of harassed. colourless eyes, he awkwardly, and in an em- barrassed way, approached the girl, asking in an unsteady voice: "Is this number 7 ? " "It is?" she answered, in that particularly hard. sounding German, such as is spoken in South Bohemia. "What do you want?" "They sent me here to work." Saying this he showed the scrap of paper he held in his hand. She continued contemplating his strange appearance, and his worn looking hag- gard face with its untrimmed beard. "The foreman is not at home," she said at last, "he went down to Scholtervien with the Others to get something to drink. But sit down, and wait for him, if you are tired." And glancing once more athis forlorn appearance, she bethought herself of her interrupted work, and once more took up her needle and thread. The new arrival said nothing, but dragging himself a few steps onward, he sank down in the grass with evident signs of exhaustion. There he lay whilst the sun sank lower and lower, spending as it were his last rays of gold. Unbroken silence reigned around; only in the light azure of the evening sky a vulture, piercing the air with a long, protracted shriek, hovered in the sky. All of a sudden the wild chorus of men's vdces became audible. The woman started.</p>
        <p>"Good God 1 here they are already," she said to herself, "and I have not finished the jacket yet." The songs drew nearer and nearer, and a few minutes after, a troop of wild looking fellows approached, amongst whom, in somewhat better dress, was a man of gigantic proportions who over- topped all the others. He was about fifty. His broad tawny face was swollen red. with drinking, and his straw hat, hanging low down over his short neck, revealed untidy greyish hair. He had slipped off his coat which he wore thrown over his left shoulder and held by his right hand. A good strong hand this was, as one caught a sight of it under the loose sleeve of his shirt. He carried a huge basket, piled up with all sorts of victuals. Two of the others had sacks of potatoes on their backs. "Hallo, Tertschka," cried the man with the basket, in a hoarse voice, " lst's have a light to carry the potatoes down to the cellar." Standing before her, his eyes chanced to fall on the jacket, which she was anxiously hiding. He asked teevishly. "Well, is it ready ? " 'Not quite, ',she answered timidly. " What ! not/ ready yet?" he roared out, his face crimsoning with wrath. "Did I not tell you I wanted it to-morrow?"</p>
        <p>"I have been hurrying on with it the whole afternoon. But I cannot do it so quickly as one who has been taught sewing." The quiet, firm voice with which these words were uttered excited him still more.</p>
        <p>_ "You always have something to retort," he scres,med. "But mind, if I don't get the jacket by to-morrow morning, I'll" . . . . . . , and putting his basket down, he rushed upon her, as if instantly to put his threats into execution. She drew back. At the same time his eyes fell on the man in uniform, who had timidly approached. "Who's that," he asked angrily, allowing his lifted hand to sink down.</p>
        <p>" lie's been sent here to work," she replied, speaking with difficulty. The foreman, for he it was, planted the full weight of his body before the tiny man, and looked him over from head to foot. To work, this fellow, who is scarcely able to keep on his feet l" "I have walked a long way," said the other timidly, "I have come from Ottrenthal." "That's a queer kind of work," said the foreman sneeringly, examining by the light of the lamp the slip of paper, given to him with a trembling hand. "Your name is Kilber," said he, looking up after a pause. "Yes, George Kilber." "How did you come by that coat." "I am on half pay." "You have been in the army ?" "For seven years. But they sent e home, because I couldn't get rid of the fever I got at the siege of Venice." "So you have the fever to boot. They accept all sorts of people—crippled most of them, good for nothing but breaking stones; and then they wonder why things don't get on better. But beware, if you do not get through two loads of gravel a day, I'll drive you off. There's .no hos- pital here.' Having delivered this speech, he picked up his basket, and, followed by the others, entered the cottage' unfastening an iron-framed door at the back. This door led down to a cave, hewn into the rock, some few steps deep. It served as a kind of cellar. Tertschka lighted them down with a torch of pine wood she had snatched off the hearth. Then the provisions were stowed away. This aecomplished, the -foreman re-fastened the door, and retired to a sort of recess, or partition let into the wall. The men went on chattering among themselves, taking no notice whatever of their new comrade, and stretched themselves for the night upon a heap of old straw which lay along the stone wall. George continued standing near the entrance,, shy and embarrassed, until at last Tertschka came up to him. "Go to sleep," she said, pointing out a place left by the others on the straw. Following her advice, he tried to occupy as little space as possi- ble. He put his satchel under his head, and, covering himself with the coat he had taken off, sighed deeply and fell asleep. Tertschka kindled • a .tiny oil-lamp, and crouching near the hearth, busied herself with sewing. At last she dropped her needle and examined the jacket through , and through. Satisfied with her work, she blew out the smoking lamp, and without undressing lay down near the hearth. Outside it was a beautiful summer's night, and through the hole of the roof, into the dark space, the silence of which was only broken by the deep breathings of the sleepers, there looked down the ever watchful stars.</p>
        <p>Morning was scarce dawning when life returned to the cottage. George awoke from his sleep. He saw his comrades one after another leave their straw beds, take down from the wall, against which they were leaning, all sorts of tools, and with them cross the threshold. He, too, had risen slipped into his clothes, and was standing not knowing what to do, when Tertschka came to him, a heavy hammer with a long handle to it on her shoulder.</p>
        <p>"The foreman is still asleep," she said, "but I know what your work will be. Take -our hammer; if you like you may go with me to work."</p>
        <p>He did what she bade him, and stepped out at her side into the freshness of the morning. Out of doors it was cool and quiet, the low notes of a bird were heard here and there, and the soft dew was resting on the meadows. They walked silently on as far as the railway, then along and up to a lonely quarry, where some labourers had already assembled. The rest, provided with barrows and shovels, had scattered along the railway. Tertschka and George passed on, walking until they reached a sort of level trench higher up. "This is my place, 'Nile said, sitting down among some rubble and Oinarry stones. "I do not like to work with those men yonder. They are wild and ill-natured. But you may stay here with me if you choose." He did not answer, but quietly sat down near her.</p>
        <p>"You see, these must be knocked to small pieces. This," she continued, pointing to a littlE pile of heaped-up gravel, is my last week's work."</p>
        <p>He took a large piece of limestone, and struck upoil it with his hammer. It made no impression. "Harder!" said Tertschka, and striking it her- self, the stone burst into pieces.</p>
        <p>He looked at her with. astonishment, and once more tried his strength, this time with bettex success; but without saying more, they applied themselves to their work.</p>
        <p>The place where they were sitting offered a. magnificent view over undulations of the far- stretching mountainous landscape. Below the rail. road, and on the same level with the ruins of the old castle of Lituni, which, like some vulture's nest, was clinging to a woodgrown cliff, deep below in a narrow pass, there stretched the borough of Scholtevrein. Behind it rose the Sonnevendstein, and out of the green pastures at its foot there was was seen a little glittering chapel surrounded with trees. This was called Our Lady's Guard." But these two, intent only on their task, had no eyes for this glorious view; on they went hammering and breaking stones. The fiun rose higher and higher, and at length shone down scorchingly on their heads. George's strokes grew fewer and fewer, and at last he allowed thebammer to drop from his hand. He lifted his cap and wiped the sweat; which was trickling down his face in big drops, from his brow. Tertschka stAapped too.</p>
        <p>"Are you tired already?" she asked, looking compassionately at him. "God knows I am," he said, in a hollow voice. "I never felt till now how the fever has reduced me."</p>
        <p>"Why did you come here weak as you are?" - "What could I do? Go a-begging, perhaps? That I cannot do. And as to a trade, I haven't learnt any. I lost my father and mother when I was a child. They made me look after the poultry, and later, until I was eighteen I had to care for the cows in the village. As I was always ailing, no one would take me into his service. But the recruiting sergeant did accept me. He forced the white coat upon me. And now they have sent me away ill and penniless. For a short time the parish provided for me, but then they said I was to come up here for work. And so I did," he added, with a bitter smile, taking up the hammer again. She bent her head iii silence. "But you can't keep at it?" she said, in a whisper. "I hope to, now that I have food. I have gone through much misery—l have had nothing to eat since yesterday morning." She said nothing. But drawing from her apron a piece of coarse bread she bore wrapped up in it, she broke it into two unequal parts, the larger of which she gave to her companion. " Eat, ' she said.</p>
        <p>He cast a stealthy glance at what was offered to him. "It's your bread, though," he said, in a low voice, which implied that he refused it.</p>
        <p>"No matter, I have enough left." And as he did not seem willing to take it, she laid it clown near him.</p>
        <p>"You must be thirsty, too," she continued. "I'm going to fetch you a mouthful of water. There's a little spring up yonder." She rose, and, picking up a half-broken pitcher lying amongst the stones, went up as far as the fir trees, where a little stream of water was forcing itself out from under the dark moss. She filled the ewer, drank, and filled it again. On returning she found the bread lying in the same place. But George took the water, thanking her warmly for it after having drunk. "There's no need to thank me for what I did with all my heart. But do eat," she insisted, "It's no shame to take it." At last he took the bread, though with some hesitation. "I'm sure you have gone through misery—that's why you are so good," he said, taking a morsel without looking at her. "I have, indeed, and even to this day I often feel what hunger is." It was as if the bread was sticking to his throat. "Really," he said, "is one so poorly paid in this place?" "I don't get paid at all now." " What ! You don't get any wages?" "No, the foreman keeps them himself." "What do you mean ? " "He's my stepfather." " Your stepfather ? " he repeated, mechanically, struck with astonishment.</p>
        <p>"Yes, my own father died from the effects of a bad accident when I was only a child. He was buried by an avalanche. After that my mother married the foreman, who at that time was a pond. digger like my father, and they went to Bohemia.' "SO you are from Bohemia? That's why you speak in the way you do, and have so queer a name. Ter—? I can't pronounce it."</p>
        <p>" Tertschka," she interposed, "it's Theresa in German."</p>
        <p>"Ilere thei_sallAiou Thesi.' But if your ate,-</p>
        <p>tattier is keeping back your wages, tie must, or course, provide for you." "I don't starve, but that's all. You can't imagine how wicked he is. He thinks of nothing but his own pleasure, and there's scarcely a nay when he's not drunk. He grudges the others the very water they drink, because they don't pay him for it. And he would sooner see all round him starve than give them-anything. I am, therefore, compelled to be content with what is left over. He keeps back my wages' as I told you before, besides forty florins in silver my mother left me. But this is not the worst. He is a wild and angey man, and often beats me. You heard yesterday how he bullied me on account of that jacket of his." "I did." "And he always did the same thing to my poor dead mother. She died of consumption, which I am sure she got from a blow on the chest he gave her once in a 'drunken fit." _ _</p>
        <p>She stopped short, lost, as it seemed, in het sorrowful recollections.</p>
        <p>George said after a while: "If your stepfather illtreats you in this way, why do you stay with him?"</p>
        <p>"Because I know very well that he would not allow me to go away," she said, after a pause. "He just wants a poor creature like me to plague and torture, without fear of punishment. In his heart he's a coward though, with all his raging. And where should I go, tool" she added, with a sigh. Baying which, she began working again. George, somewhat refreshed as he was now feeling, followed her example, and soon they were engaged once more in their hard employment. Hour after hour wore away, and the mid-day sun was shining over them. Perfect silence reigned all around, the quiet was only broken by the mono- tonous fall of the hammer and the cry of the wood- pecker. At length the shrill note of a bell was heard..</p>
        <p>"What's that?" asked George, seeing the others put down their tools and make for the cottage. "The foreman is ringing for dinner?" answered Tertschka.</p>
        <p>"For dinner !" he faintly repeated, "and what do they give us here for dinner?" "Gruel and potatoes. There are sure to be potatoes to-day, they brought some up with them yesterday." "It's long since I ate any meat," he said, medi- tatively. 'But who prepares the dinner ? " "The foreman himself, for he mistrusts any of us. Besides, he likes cooking. As for it taking up his time, he doesn't mind, he lets things go any- how. Now and then, however, he does look into things, swearing and shouting at everyone, although, to be sure, mostly at those who are too timid to reply. But take my advice, do not eat any meat to-day. It would harm you, and you will have the fever. The foreman has no con- science, he buys spoilt meat from the butcher's down in Scholtevrein. Having ordered everyone to buy from himself the victuals they want, he is getting rid of whatever he has—pretty dearly, too, so that he gets a sinful profit on "No fear of the meat," said George, bitterly; "having got no money, I can't afford to buy any., ' "As to that, the foreman would certainly give you credit until Saturday, when the wages are paid. But beware if he once chalks you up I Not only does he reckon everything at twice its worth, but he will force you to go and play cards with him, in order to get both your body and soul into his clutches.. Then you won't see a farthing of your wage any more; you are his, as the evil soul is the devil's.'</p>
        <p>George was listening anxiously. "What am I to do to be ablc to live till Saturday?" he said, dejectedly. "If lam not to take anything of his upon credit, I must starve." Tertschka was: busying herself with the hem of her skirt, undoigi part of it. At last she drew out a twisted slip of paper, and unfolded it. It proved to be one of those fragments of paper money circu- lating in Austria at that period, in order to supply the want of coin. She handed it to George. "Take it," she said, "It will last you till Saturday. If you take care, you will be able to give it me back from your wages in small portions, every weeks" He stared silently at the paper in her hand. Surprise, emotion, joy passed in strange mingling over his face. It was as if he had been struck deaf and dumb. He did not move.</p>
        <p>_ "It's all I have," she frankly said. "Our Engineer gave it me last week when he was her. He had forgotten one of his instruments in thb cottage here, and sent me to fetch it for him. It will be doing me a favour to take it. lam always afraid of losing it--that was why I sewed it into the hem of my frock. If the foreman knew of it, he would long since have taken it." With these words she laid it in his hands. "Now let's go to our dinner, and don't forget what I said to you about the meat; be satisfied with potatoes for to-day. The meat is generally bad, but yesterday they brought fresh Potatoes. And you might take a glass of brandy, that would do you good." He rose and followed her in silence then suddenly stopped, and looking deep into her soft brown eyes, he said: "There never was anyone so good to me before as yourself. How am Ito make up to you for it?" 'Nonsense, aren't we to help one another in this world? And aren't you good, too? I knew you were at once when you came up to the hut, yester- day,* (To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>ARTILLERY. ON DARTMOOR.</head>
        <p>ARTILLERY. ON DARTMOOR.</p>
        <p>"When the Duke of Connaught some time ago announced his intention of visiting the Summer Practice Camp of the Royal Artillery, under the shadow of Ye 3 Tor, Dartmoor's second highest peak, the officers busied themeelves in making the best possible arrangements for the aecommodation of his Royal Highnees. Special furniture was brought up from the sleepy little town of Okehampton, 1,500 feet below in the valley, over which a ruined Norman castle, arrayed in its mantle of ivy. stands as a spectral sentinel. It was conjectured that this officer of Royal blood would be at least as particular about hie temperary quarters as any other general. At last, when all the ipreparations were com- plete, the Duke arrived and after a long and tiring day on the moorland watching the guns belch forth destruction at imaginary foes, he was shown the arrangements that had been made for him, with many apologies for their scanty character. The excuses were, however, quickly cut short by the Royal vi, itor : 'Oh ¶1 am not going to sleep in any building, thank you. I shall sleep under canvas as the men do, so that I may know what it is really like up here.' Accordingly,while the officers turned into their comfort- able quarters, the Duke lay down in an ordinary bell-i tent,just as the 500 re 600 gunners do every night. The ,1 next morning, when he was asked bow he had fared, he replied cheerily : ' Well, I turned in with a racking toothache, and woke this morning to find it bad quite gone, tharks to the splendid rir up here.' And the Duke added that in future he should recommend all his friends who were out of sorts to try the effect of tent life in the invigorating air of the western highlands. This camp has now superseded Shoeburyness as the practice-ground of the artillery, owing to the long and irregular ranges obtainable. It is delightfully situated on the northern margin of Dartmoor. F^r twenty-five years oa mps for artillerymen have been held every summer on a comparatively small scale on one portion of these uplands, but now Okehampton has become one of the great permanent military establish- ments of the country. Old wooden structures have given place to well-built substantial dayrooms for the men ; an excellent recreation-room, with reading-room, and refreshment bar, has just been completed ; a large church finds a place, and even the guardroom being enlarged—almost doubled. Many thousand pounds are now being spent by the War Jffiee in order to render the camp a more pleasant place of temporary residerce for the hundreds of cfficere and men who ia future will be sent either into Devonshire or to Salisbury Plain to practice with their guns. While the men con- tinue to sleep in tent, they have comfortable rooms in which they have their meals, and increased provision has recently been made to eneble them to spend their hours of leisure pleasurably, without over-running the little town in the valley below, which has few atnuee- meets to offer to a soldier. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The batteries. from all parts of England, visit Oke- hampton in turn, and the arrival and departure of the units lend to the railway station quite fi warlike appear- ance. Formerly the batteries travelled by road, being billeted at night at public-bousrs, but this mode of iemoval was so slow and expensive that it has been abandoned. Now each battery, however far it may have to travel, brings with it by rail its own horses and aunt; and complete equipment, of every kind, so that nnt only are the men tested as gunners at the admirable irregular ranger, but the bOr3eB are trained to Cover the difficult country swiftly and with unfalter- ing step, and the harness and weapons, as they are rattled over great boulders and through ravines and streams hidden away in the folds of the moorland, are put to a trial that could not be provided any where else. In the course of a season from twenty to thirtyl and sometimes even more, batteries pay flying vtaits of a fortnight each. Three batteries at a time are thus always using the ranges. There is also one unfortunate unit which is seleeted at the beginning of summer to remain as permanent fatigue party to keep the camp in good condition, speed the parting guests, and welcome those arriving, and to carry out all the weary, heavy work in connection with the care of the targets."—Fro an important article on "flew our Gunners are Trained," in the August " Windsor Magazine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>CRICKETERS' CLASSIC. 'Bowlers of the Past.</head>
        <p>THE CRICKETERS' CLASSIC. 'Bowlers of the Past.</p>
        <p>Though the volume is dedicated to the celebrated batsman, it seems to the reader that Nyren's sympathies were generally on the side of the bowler. The exploits of both are narrated with great relish, but his descrip- tion of the various methods of attack displays perhaps a larger share of simple pride, It is with the bowlers that he begins his description of the famous playere,and hints on the art and practice of bowling occupy a good part of the " Tutor " proper. The following piece of advice reads curiously in these days of billiard-table wickets a—" In a match, when running to bowl, he should fix his eye on a certain spot where be is desirous the ball should pitch ; there will be no difficulty in selecting an. object for the purpose of a guide : either a difference in the colour of the grass, or a slight unevenness in the ground will answer bie purpose . . ." So, toe, in the remarks upon pitching the neckets—a responsibility which rested in those days upon the bowlers of the team, who were wont to inspect the ground carefully before the commence- ment of a match, and place the wickets with a single eye to their own interests. " The chief art is," says the Tutor," to select a situation that will suit your own style of bowling, and at the same tine prove dine* vantageous to your 'adversaries ; as these two poiets, however, can rarely be accomplisbed, you oan at all events pitch the wickets in such a manner as to benefit yourself." Bowlers did not always display equal 'fore- thought in this important part of their business. That well-known trundler, "Lumpy " (whose *reel name was Stevens)—there is something delightfully re- . dolent of the primitive Hampshire village in these rustle nicknames—committed a grave error in this. He was intrinsically a fine bowler, but lacked subtlety of intell( et, and inconsequence" would invariably choose the ground where the ball would shoot,instead of select- ing' a rising spot to bowl against," with the object of securing catches. It le to be feared that Lumpy was inclined to selfiehness, for itl contrast to his method we have put'before us the shining example of David Harris, who was easeful not only to bowl for °etches (and be ceetent to share his glory with the field) but would also consider his partner when selecting a suitable pitch—an effort of altruism which, we gather.it never occurred to Lumpy to attempt. It is of David Harris that Nye= pronounces one of his finest panegyrics. Here is a portion of it, not always very intelligible to the modern cricketer :—" First of all, be stood erect like a soldier at drill ; then, with a graceful curve of the arm, he raised the ball to his forehead, and drawing back his right foot, started off with his left. The calm look and general air of the man were uncommonly striking, and from this series of preparations he never deviated. . . His mode of delivering the ball was very singular. He would bring it from under the arm by a twist, and nearly as high as his armpit, and with this action push t, as it were, from him. How it was that the balls acquired the velocity they did by this mode of delivery I never could comprehend." The reader will also, I inmeh3e, find it a difficult matter. _ . .</p>
        <p>One of the pleasantest tales, to any thinking, in this armoury of good things, is that of the great match (tingle wicket) between that celebrated bowler " Lumpy," mentioned above, and a certain country- man. Five pounds a side depended upon the result, and the rustic!bore the palm. There is no more racy passage in the bock than this from which I make a short excerpt : —The confident old bowler made the countryman go in first, for be thought to settle his business in a twink ; but the fellow having an arm as long as a hop-pole, reached in at Lumpy's balls, bowl what length he might ; and slashed and thrashed sway in the most ludicrous style, bitting his balls all ever the - field, and alwAys up in the air; and he made an immense number of runs from this! prince of bowlers before be could get him out—and, egad ! he beat him! for Lumpy was never much of a bat, and the fellow bowled fast and straight and all along the ground, The" prince of bowlers "was none too pleased with the event of this encounter, and swore that he would sever play another single wicket match. But the batsmen are still awaiting their turn, and here is the name of John Small, the elder. shining amente them "in all the lustre of a star of the first magnitude." John Small had many talents, among which it may be accounted to his credit that he was the inventor of that useful practice, the stealing of short rune. So correct, too, wad his judgment of the game, that old Nyren would appeal to him when there was a question of a point of law. He was also a " remarkably well-made and well-knit man, of honest expression, and as active as a hare. He was a goud fiddler and taught himself the double-bass, which proved bis salvation upon one occasion. For when a vicious bull made at him on his way to a musical party, our hem "with the charactertatic coolness and presence of mind of a good cricketer, began playing upon the bass. to the admiration and perfect satisfaction of the mis- chievous beast." As illustrating at once Small's fito simplicity and independence of spirit, it is related that when the Duke of Dorset sent him a new violin as a present, Small sent back in return two new bats and ballis.—Tram "'The Cricketers' Classie ' in the " Corte kill.Jiageutine " for August.</p>
      </div>
