      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>CHARGE AGAINST MAJOR LEWIS.</head>
        <p>THE CHARGE AGAINST MAJOR LEWIS.</p>
        <p>Trial at Liverpool Assizes. --- Three Years' Penal Servitude.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assiz tie Saturday, befnra the Lord Chief Justice, Richard Lowie (54), fernierly a Lancashire meeietrate, town councillor for Widnes', and a Volunteer nffieer. pleaded eunty te having on Merch 26, 1893, at Widnes, beine then a clerk to Henry Thomas Clare-hr, the rec=iver and manager of John Hutchinson, deceased, embezzled the eum of £47 15; received be him for or or' account of his employer ; and alsn to having fraudulently nee&amp; false entries in the books of his employer. Mr F. E. Smith (instructed by Masers Aaiun, Motto's and Paxton, of Tavernen', acting as aeento of the public Proeecuter) appeared for the prosecution, and Me k Tobin (inetructed by Mr H L Riley, ef St. Helens) appeared en priaenee's behalf. Mr Smith, addressing the Court, alluded fleet of all to the estate at Widnes, and slid that it had nriginelly helneeed to Mr Jean Hutchin- son and for many reare until ' recently had bten administered be trustees end the trustees appoin- ted the prieener, who had been in their emplnt- ment armee 1864. After hoidieg various offices for the trustees he was finally appointed benk-kesper, a pos time which he held when the eaeate passed out of the hands of the trustee.; in 1893, whew a receiver and maaager was arpointed by the Court of Chancery. The reoeiver appointed was Henry Thomas Clanchr,a retired captain of the Royal Navy, whn retained the services of the ac- cused until September, 1901. Eunice all that time there were excessive defalcations in the accounts of the estates and these were discovered quite accidentally by the auditor, who happened to notice some erasure. The aggregete amount of the defalcanions was £3,776. The -plan adopted by the prisoner was, when he received cheques to enter these hi the cash books for smaller amounts than they represented and from whatever moneys came into his hand's he retained the difference between the SITIJUSES entered and the value of the ohequee. I the prooeedings in the Court of Chancery an 'attempt was made to make C4ptain Clanchy answer- able for these leakeses. and therefore the case came into the hands of the public prosecutor. Mr Smith said, in conclueion, that it was onle proper for him to act, that the prienner had elven such assistance as he could in the investigaeien, and several items had been traned by his aid which might have caused the gree'est difficulty otherwise. Mr Tobin, addressing the Court on prisoner's behalf, said that for mane rears he had been well known in "ffidneF. He had held an appointment as magistrate for the county of Lancsabire and foe rniv,v years he had been a member of the Town Council of Widmer. He had interested himself in ma great many public works and had taken a most active part in connection with the Velure- Leer', in which beheld a commission as major. How this man, who had held thee offices and who had hold the respect of his fe low townsmen, came to be in that pneition he would relate al shortly as possible. The estate at Widnes, which was of many, many acres, had gradually tams built open, end the prisoner, ever since be was a lad, for between 30 and 40 years, had been in the service of the truetece of that estate. At first he had been an °Hiner:- clerk and then he had been gradually promoted until ho beneme cashier and book- keeper at a allay; of £3OO a Year. Tois salary was awarded him some 20 years ego and he had been in receipt of the same remuneration ever since. The moneys of the estate passed through his hands and these amounted to between £lB,OOO and £20,000 oer ;mum. Now, what was the cause of the trouble ? It was not euggeeted that the prisoner hau any extravagant tastes at all. It was not suggested that he had been gernedinte nor that he bed been nneculatine Upon the Stock Exchange, nor was it eugeested that he had spent the money upon vice or immorality. Oa the other hand he had lived in a stnall house with his wife and children and had kept only one servant, and there was nothiag extravagant in his life so far as he (counsel) was instructed. There were two causee of his downfall. The leerier clean wag that these appointmeete that he had held, as a town councillor, as a macistrate. Wan officer ef the volunteemend as a gentleman taking an active interest in public duties, had necessitated cer- tain expenditure which he would not otherwise have incurred and expenses for which his quite small income was totally inadequate. He (counsel) understood, how- ever, that the larger and real cause was that about 10 years ago prisener bought some land, some 50 odd acres, the price of which was £lO,OOO 136 paid off £l,OOO in cash and the balance remained on mortgage. It became necessary to develop thie land and to make it attractive and this necessitated the spending of money that was not possible out of an eternal ismotue amount- en to £3OO, It was necessary both to develope the land and to pay the intereat on the mortgage. The whole of t ebareemoney of the excused was locked up and be had o lm available for these purpoeee. Large sums OF money 'welts/ Sassing through his hand,' end so ha began to take a little at first, confidently believing that the Property would tern out well: At that time it was be- lieved the. Widnes would contioul to improve rapidly and become a lart, town These hopes were fels'fieel and the town went doer rathera d othan eimprovedyhehaS rve and many the chemical e works Were accused had taken money and l; amount on mertgege from £9,000 to £7,000. Last year the auditor discovered that there wore discrepencies,•nd the prisoner at once confessed everything, verbally upon possible and .s s at stye! the first day and in writing the next d set to work and rendered every Re (counsel) would point out that prisoner's faithful Work for the estate in all other respects for all these years showed his intellietnce and his desire to do what he could, and he would ale° eernestly ask his Lordship to remember that the defeleetioae were diecovered in eptemb.r last year and the months that had elapsed Since had cnntained nothing but suspenee for the pri- soner. What punishment should be pet or; him ? Of con:se it might be held that the position and educa- tion of the prisoner made the offence wireo and that therefore the e)unishment should be greater, nut on the other bend it should be lege by reason of the agony the prisoner must have suffered siace neptembn last year. Ten months aim he was a comparatively robust man • now he was merely a shadow of his former self. 'Since September last it was public knowledge that he bad done wrong and he had as time went on been stripped of his honoere. It had been ten months of acute agony, and he (counsel) begged his Lerdehip most earnestly to take Such a view of these things as would give the prisoner a chance in the nottton distant future to go aid work and earn some Money so that he might make what reetitntiontre could.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>"HAVE YOU TRIED</head>
        <p>"HAVE YOU TRIED</p>
        <p>the cambridge Lemonade ? It is a perfect boon this hot weather. Yours lovingly, Kitty." nig sentenc,o occurred at the end of a letter received by a lady, who forwarded it to Cbivers and Sons, Ltd. Histen, Cambridge, as an indication of the popularity of the new beverage. Made from Sugar and Lemons, Quarantred free from added aeid. "It bears all," says another correspondent. Azdt your grocer. One bottle Makes 2 gallons 511, or send f3d for sample to Chivers and Sons, Ltd., Hiptcn, Cambridge, The First English, Prilit Growers' Jam Factory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <p>The Judge : How many children has the defendant ? Mr Tobin : There are four, throe sons and a daugteter. The daughter is married. The Judge : I understand that it is ten years since be purchased this land P— That is so, your Lor.iship. The Judge : He involved no one else in immorality beside himself ?—No, my Lord. His Lordship reserved his judgment until after the luncheon adj ,urninent, some three hours later. When the C mrt re-assembled he announcvd that he had given the ease his most exious consideration and he had considered care- fully the features of prisoner's career set forth ire the I able an eloquent address by Mr. Tobin. But for :-bese he would have had to pass a mach more severe sentence, as be was abliged Le remeneleer the terrible example if a man of prisoner's position, who had gained public res.- Dent, should be allowed to go unpunished for his f aults. For a considerable time yrisonor had taken money by I fraud and deceit, he had concealed the fact in his accounts, and they were only revealed at a later stage when they were found out. He (the judge) could not shut his eyes to the fact that be had embezzled a very considerable sum and had falsified his books to conceal the fact. After an allusion td prisoner's adue.ation, he said he had considered what had been said upon that and other points, and he boliev,rl tba'. prisoner was sincerely sorry for his cri ne,and he had therefore modi- fied the sentence that he would otherwise have passed. The prisoner would be sentenced to three yzar,' pares servitude.</p>
        <p>Prisoner left the dock in a dazed state. ..--...--..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WARRINGTON VOLUNTEERS UNDER CANVAS. NOTES.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON VOLUN- TEERS UNDER CANVAS. NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A VOLUNTEER].</p>
        <p>Have you ever 'l3Leri under cantata's at Salisbury ? I not, you have missed a fair old treat. I went with the South Lancashire Volunteers (last Satuiday week for a fortnight. Starting from Central Station at 7.50 p.m.,we reached Tid worth Station at somewhere about 5 o'clock on Sunday morning and then had a four mile march over a very heavy road into camp, feeling very tired. Needless to say, church parade was cancelled, and the men, and the officers as well, spent the time in having a quiet (?) snooze— it least, most of them. The camp is very prettily situated. The battalion, as perhaps everyone knows, is under the command oft) Inel J C Ridgway, V.D , who is ably seeandel by Major F M Reome, Maj or Sudeten, Maj et. E ielsten, and a good .staff of officers. The " feeding " department is attended to ba Q lartermaSter aid En, Lieutenaat Potter and Q me-ernes:et Sergeant Wi'coaks, who attend tithe wants off the " bier man " wit ft tin- mendable abil ta. Of course, the s.l tiers hove language of their oa n and talk glib'y of"aplit pea" (Pea) and other nicknames for various articles, some of which are better, some worse. Pleaty of amusements are pro- vided in camp for man who are too tired—or lazy—to go out of camp, wootbali is indulged in by some, and others frequent the canteen until " Lights out " when they make for their tents with feet that are none too steady I know because ore out of our tent came in on Monday night. Of course, he canght his foot in the tent rope and fell on the top of poor me ! Others, want- ing to keep sober, go into one of the Soldiers' Institute. (Church of England and ‘Vesleyan) Here, one can get i anything to eat, drink, or smoke, while letters cin be written and magazines are supplied. Concerts are held every night,the music being provided by the men them- selves, and several of them are very good "warblers," especially some of our conoractea of the Rival Welsh Fusiliers. The audience is alternatively listening, to sentimental songs with rapt attention and then rolling about with laughter at a hurnorou I ditty. Even mando- line and whistling solos are rendered with good effect: There is plenty of work to do. Reveille is blown at 5 0 a.m., and you are awakened by the bugle band kicking up a deuce of a row all round each tent. Then there is a struggle. " Where's my boots ?" and " Who's got my pants ?" and otbar such remarks are heard on every side. At last, everyone is ready and "Fell in' is sounded. Then we see the officers coming, and the colour-sergeants and sergearsts start knocking Us into something like order. "Comoany, number ! fours ! right ! quick march is given, and off we go on to the parade ground to do a little skirmishing. After about so hour, we come back and have breakfast. Then out we go again for four hours before dinner, sometimes for a route march and sometimes indulging in a sham fight. After dinner, there is another hour OP two of bard work. Everyone is free alter tea, except the guards and picquets, until " Last post " when all must return except those who are lucky enough to get passes However, we are all enjoying ourselves immensely and I suppose the fortnight will pass all too quickly for most of us. I must now go and clean up for to-morrow so " Good-bye." Sudden Death at Warrington. The death of Mrs Elizabeth Shaw, wife of Mr W J Shaw,of the Greghound Inn,Dallam-lane,occurred on Monday, under painfully sudden cir, umstences• The deceased lady who 57 years of age, went out for a walk on Sunday with a friend, and when near Orford Barracks she was seized with an attack of , paralysis. She never regained consciousness, des- pite the attention of Dr. Peacocke. Lancashire Trades and Labour Councils.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Federation of the Lancashire Trades and Labour Coan,.3ils was held at the Association Hall, Manchester, on Saturday. The report which was submitted by the secretary (Mr G D Kelley), dealt in an exhaustive manner with the steps that had been taken by the Federation in general, an various branches in particular, on such questions as education, housing reform, the tax upon foodstuffs, old-age pensions,cheap trains for working men, and the amendment of the patent lair&amp; The report and statement of accounts were adopted. The Corley Council forwarded a resolu- tion suggesting that such sums as were accumulated by the Post office, from the savings of the poorer elaves should be utilizad for the construction of work men's dwellings. After some debate the resolution was referred hack in order that it might be redrafted. The financial difficulty in which the Federation, in common with the respective coun- cils, has found itself for some years, was met by a resolution, which was adopted, to increase the contribution by ls. for every 1,090 members in affiliation. The next business was the election of officers, and the vote resulted in Mr g Byrne (A.ahton) being electsd president, lkfr W Carr (Cheq- and Mr G D Kelley, secretary. ter), vice-president,</p>
        <p>£7OO roe AN ARM—At Birmingham, Mrs Kratith. Park Avenue, Haadsworth, was awarded £7OO damages for the loss of an arm. Sho was a prssengsr on the cable tram route of the city tramway company, and while alighting the car jerked forward, and she was thrown down, her left arm getting under the when. The late Cardinal Lliochowski left moat of his for- tune of £40,000 to his nephew, •Count Ledochowski, who threw up his commission in the Austrian Army as a protest against the practice of duelling.</p>
        <p>--</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>rITE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, AUGUST 1, 1902.</head>
        <p>rITE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, AUGUST 1, 1902.</p>
        <p>THE INDIAN TROOPS. Voyage up the Manchester Ship</p>
        <p>Canal.</p>
        <p>The Scene at Latchford Locks.</p>
        <p>The Indian troops who came to England in June to be present at the Coronation of the King sail -q1 up the Ship Canal from Liverpool on Wednesday upon a brief visit to Manchester. There are 600 of them, and they represent India in most of its great divisions They include Native Mountain 9.rtillery,Royal ingineer4. twelve detachments of cavalrN (from Madras, Bombay and Bengal), fifteen contingents of infantry, nine- teen se-Aions of the Imperial S-urvice Troops. and fifty Indian Volunteers Colonel D4WSOII, GB., is in command, and with him there are four teen Euro- pean officers. The native officers number 34, and the non-commissioned offi rers are all Indians. In accordance with the strict requirements of caste, toe troops have brought w,th them their own cooks, to the number of thirteen and they are ac- companied also by four barbers. They transhipped from the transport Hardinge to the steamers Claughton and Mersey at half-pact nine on Wednesday morning. In a few minutes the decks of the two small steamers were crowded with brilliantly uniformed Sikhs and Goorkhas, Cavalry Guides and Lancers, Viceroy's Bodyguards and Bombay Grenadiers—to mention but a few of many names. .• _</p>
        <p>As soon as the members of the contingent were aboard, supplemented by a boat load of additional passengers in the shape of a plrty from 11. anch e‘ter which inetided Mr Jahn Bythell, chairman of the Canal Company. and other officials, as well as a number of Press representatives, the Mersey and Claughton fortne I procession an I beaded up river towards the Canal entrance at Lytham. The river was rather rough, a little too rough indeed for some of the visitors, who, though excellent as soldiers, are by no means good sailors, and two or three were so far affected as to become a little poorly. But it was not more than a temporary trouble.</p>
        <p>Eastham wis duly reathed. The entrance was calmly and quickly effected, and safe upon the bosom of more peaceful waters the troops were able to enjoy the journey. It proved to be alike enjoyable and interesting to the Indians themselves, to their fellow passengers, and to the spectators on the shore. These latter were very numerous, gathering along the banks of the Canal in crowds almost as large as those which a year ago witnessed the passage of the:flotilla of destroyers. They were equally enthusiastic. They cheered frantically, and waved their hats, and shouted all manner of greetings to the gallant visitors from India's coral strand. And the soldiers very readily responded They waved their hands and saluted, replied to the friendly challengings in words of their own lantuage, not interpretable by the hearers except se far as the tones and gestures bespoke their sincerity and heartiness. The spoken greetings from shore were various. One—near Latchford —was a hearty " God bless you," and another—from some cheering boys—was, " Got any cocoa-nuts?" The particular Indian to whom the inquiry was addressed, not understanding it,rnerely smiled and nodded hi a head amiably. "Then throw us one," called the boy. At two or three paints on the way up flowers were thrown into the steamers from the shore, and were eagerly picked up. In the middle of the (Icy tMe troops, who had dined at nine o'clock on the Hardinge, were served with bananas, oranges, soda-water, and cigarettes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>RUNCORN'S WE LOOM E.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN'S WE LOOM E.</p>
        <p>The reception of the troops at Runcorn at noon, was most enthusiastic. For nearly two miles the banks of the canal were lined by thousands of spectators, who lustily cheered and waved flags and handkerchiefs as the steamers passed in quick succession. The scene was most picturesque, and amateur photographers were much in evidence snapping pictures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SCENE AT LATCEIFORD.</head>
        <p>THE SCENE AT LATCEIFORD.</p>
        <p>A lar7e concourse of Warrington people eagerly awaited the arrival of the troops at Latchford. For some distance the canal bank was lined with specta- tors and a large crowd had assembled at the locks. It was a quarter past one when the troops arrived at the locks and they were greeted with tremendous I cheering. To the surprise of many of the onlookers, 11 the troops returned the salutation with a hearty 1" Hip, hip, hurray." Whilst the vessels were being raised in the large lock the spectators had an opportunity of seeing the troops to ad van- ;age. The fine physique of the men, as well as the picturesque uniform., excited a great deal of comment not to say admiration, 1 Several of the brave Goorkhas were pointed out to 1 the interested assembly by a British officer. Some of the soldiers seemed to take a great interest in the working of the lock, and one who understood English entered into conversation with the specta- tors. Many amateur photographers were on the scene, anxious to secure a snap—hot as a memento of the event. At length, the lock gates were opened, and to the accompaniment of the loud cheers the steamers resumed the voyage to Manchester. There could he no doubt about the fact that the visitors were greatly impressed with the Canal and its accessories. The locks through which they passed gave some ot them momentary fears as they found themselves shut in down below the stone- work or gradually rising on the inrush as the upper gates were opened. But the swing bridges opening to let them pass afforded a subject of never-ending wonder.</p>
        <p>"See much good thing in London," ejaculat 3d one stalwart voteran wits bemeialled breast, " see much good thing in Liverpool, but water bridge most.gool thing in England."</p>
        <p>Thi; was in reference to the swing aqueduct at Barton, the working of which seemed to strike the Orientals as a matter of no small mystery The natives were all in good humour and most desirous of giving pleasure to others. Even to the question as to whether they did not find the weather rather cold, many of them would reply " Cold ? No cold, sahib." though the way in which they occasionally shivered in the chilly breeze of this inclement July rather gave them away. But no all are, as people might be tempted to suppose. unused to hard weather. A portly, hand- some subadhar of Bellochis, who speaks English fluently, informed an inquirer—' We are used to it in our country. It is colder in the hills sometimes than the weather you have here." But he admitted it was bad for the Dekkanis and other natives of the Dlain. .om 3 of whom would be a long time before they got over the results of their unwonted climatic experiences.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>TROOPS ON BOARD.</head>
        <p>THE TROOPS ON BOARD.</p>
        <p>The troops are representative of all branches of the native Indian army—Bengal lancers in yellow, scarlet, or blue, according to their regiments, stal- wart Sikhs in khaki, writh brightly-coloured turbans; [Undo() infantry from Bombay, Madras, and Bengal, in glaringly British red coats and blue trousers ; Imperial service troops from Kashmir and Hyderabad, and every great native State; fierce Beloochis, Pathans, Afridis, and Afghans, in the nuave-like costume of Jacob's riflds ; and the gallant little Goorkhas in their rifle green, round forage caps stuck jauntingly over their brown heads and swagger canes in their hands.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>ARRIVAL AT I‘IANCHESTER.</head>
        <p>ARRIVAL AT I‘IANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>Punctually at 4.30 the s s. Mersey arrived at No 1 Pomona Dook, and the Claughton was only a short distance in the rear. A la.ge crowd of persons had assembled on the quay, and afforded them a most bea,ty reception.</p>
        <p>The troops were received and welcomed by the Lord Mayor. A nrocession was formed for Belle Vue Gardens, an 1 on their way through the main streets of the city the troops were warmly cheered by the people. To the evening the Lord Mayor entertained the European officers of the continent at dinner. A nrework display was given at Belle Tee, and was watched with great interest by the troops.</p>
        <p>Reservists' Return from South Africa.‘</p>
        <p>Rain was -Tailing very heavily at ten o'clock on Tuesday night, when eighty reservists of the Ist South Lancashire Regiment and twenty reservists of the Royal Winter Regiment,in charge of Captain Norman,of the Borderers,arrived st Central Station, Warrington, from South Africa. The time of the men's arrival was not generally known, and very few spectators had assembled. When they reached Orford Barracks the soldiers were drenched. They were at once supplied with dry clothing and a hot supper, and made comfortable for the night. On Wednesday they proceeded to their homes. The Ist South Lancashires remain in South Africa for the present, but they are booked for India direct.</p>
        <p>A considerable number of nuns an sistsrs of mercy are arriving in Eoglani via Dover from France, as a result of tits closing of the religious schools and Convoints.</p>
      </div>
