      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>LAKE WINDERMERE.</head>
        <p>LAKE WINDERMERE.</p>
        <p>The Jurisdiction Question.</p>
        <p>'rhe much vexed question of the jurisdiction over Lake Windermere will again came before the Lancashire County Council at the next meeting. A conference has been held between a speoial sub-committee, the ripa- rian owners, and owners of land adjoining the lake, and representatives of the Ulverston Hoard e Guardians and Rural District Council. It was stated that the general opinion was held that owing to the increasing population of the districts akut- ting on the leo, a 1 to the number of ex- cursionists in the summer months toe better government of the lake and district, ss regards the regulation of the navigation, eff 3ctive police control,a rid the prevention of pollution, was a matter of prcasine importance. The present control of the lake (being so much divided among different local authorities) wss thought to be very unsatisfactory, and to be growing more complicated.° wing to some of the local authorities In the county of Westrt orland asserting jurisdiction over the whole water area of the lake, portions of which have been hitherto deemed to be within the county of Lan- eashire. Ls an outcome of the conference the Lancashire County Council is recernmended to authorise the special sub-committee to invite representatives of the Westmor.. land County Council to a conference on the whole sub- ject at an early date.</p>
        <p>"Mentioned " by Lord Kitchener. The" London gazette" on Tuesday night con- tained Lord Kitchener's final despatch, dated Cape Town, June 23rd, which covers the period between the signature of the terms of peace at Pretoria and his departure from Cape Town. Amongst the -names of officers, non-commissioned officers, and men brought to notice are the following :—Prince of Wales's Volunceers, Soufh Lancashire Regiment : Lieutenant Colonel R H Hell, D SO, Brevet Lieu- tenant-Colonel H P Shekleton ; Captains H De L Walters, C R S Woods, T Upperton, and B It Goren; 2972 Company Sergeant-Major L Duffy, 248 S Quartermaster-Sergeant T Foster, 3120 Colour. sergeant J Nolan, 4495 Sergeant J Murphy. 3rd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment (Militia) : Major H M Hall, Lance-sergeant Sumners, Lance- Sergeant Holden, Lance-Sergeant Noonan.</p>
        <p>Funeral of Mr Frederick M. Tonge.</p>
        <p>_ The remains of the late Mr Frederick M fonge,of Marsh-street, Warrington, who died early in the week at the Bowdon Consumption Hospital, were interred in the Sankey and Penketh Cemetery on Saturday: The deceased was a wiredrawer, employed for the last few years at Messrs Rylands Brothers and as a mark of respect to his memory about 75 of his fellow employes from Messrs Rylands, the Firth Company, the Vfhitecross Com- pany, and Messrs Houghton's (where he served his apprenticeship), preceded the hearse. The funeral I service was conducted by the Rev J R Jones, vicar of Sankey, and a strong contingent of tho Apollo I Male Voice Choir, under the cDnductorship of Mr Thomas Brough, sang the hymn " rerfect in Christ." A lsrge concourss of friends ware present to pay their last tribute of respect to the deceased, and altogether the service was of a very impressive character. Several beautiful floral emblems were forwarded by sympathizing friends. The deceased, who ,was the son of Mr Matthew Tongs, of Sankey Bridges, and was in his 37th year, leaves a wife and two children to mourn his loss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <p>..-....-....-...-...</p>
        <p>An Italian named Marinelli well known as a swimmer in Italy, has announced his intention to swim the Channel from Calais to Dover in August next.</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>NOTES ON UAIVIES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON UAIVIES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>There was very little cricket in Warrington and dis- trict on Saturday, owing to the wretched weather con- ditions. The first eleven met Earlestown at Earlestown, but after a quarter of an hour's play rain fell so per- sistently that the match was abandoned. P. B. Hawtin and .1 Gibbon were the only batsmen to go to the wicket and they had compiled 4 and 13 respectively when play ceased. ,The second eleven's encounter with Earlestown 2nd wag also of short duration, Earlestown having placed 78 for the loss of 5 wickets,to their credit when stumps were drawn</p>
        <p>Fairfield and Grappenhall 2nd played their engage- ment to a conclusion, Fairfield being victorious by 14 runs. H Whittle (14), GH W , Steel (13) and H Nield (11) were the chief contributors for the wieners, C Smails (29) and A L Kaye (12) doing capital work for Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Mills visited Platt Bridge, and their match with the Prinptive Methodists ended in a draw' The visitors were all dismissed for 48. and the home team had compiled 33 for the loss of seven wickets when play ceased. Hawthorn batted in creditable style for the Wire Mills, his innings realizing 18, and Walker bowled well.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday Warrington Co-operative entertained Bury Co-operative at Arpley, and the visitors were beaten, after an exciting finish, by two runs. Kay (18) and Johnson (12) were the top scorers for Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Rugby Football. _</head>
        <p>Rugby Football. _</p>
        <p>At a well-attended—meeting of the members of the Widnes Football Club, held at the Grosvenor Hotel, on Monday, presided over by Mr. W. J. Clarke,the question of the charges for admission during the coming Northern , Union season was thoroughly discussed. It was pro- posed that the stand be lOs 6d, membership 7s 6d, and season tickets, to view all matches on the popular side, with the exception of 'cup.ties, ss.This was unanimously agreed to. It was furthd decided to increase the sub- scription for ladies' tickets from 2s ad ti ss, and was carried by a large majerity. Pessimistic ideas are prevalent respecting the club's success this coming season, as practically no move is being made to secure a few "cracks." Thig will have to be done if success is to be assured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>It the London County Grounds, Herne-hill on Mon- day. Mrs Andrews made an effort to establish a one hour ladies' cycle record. She covered her first mile in 2min. 18 4-ssec , five miles in 12min. 20 1-ssec., ten miles in 24min. 52 3-ssec., fifteen miles in 37min. 34 3.55ec., and twenty miles in 50min. 39 3-ssec. With eighteen seconds to go she touched her pacer's back wheel and fell. Her total distance was 24 miles 280 yards. Miss M Harwood was reported to have covered 24 miles 780 yards in an hour at Putney last October.</p>
        <p>On S unday evening Edward Forster, aged 19, of 23 College-street, St Helens, met with a rather serious accident at Bold He ran into the wall surrounding the garden of Mr Ellison, of Willmere Ledge. His left ear was very badly torn, and Mr Ellison,on coming out, found the unfortunate cyclist in an unconscious state. Fortunately. Mr Ellison, who is secretary of a Liverp )ol branch of the St. John Ambulaace Association, was able to render the lad efficient first aid. Afterwards Forster was conveyed to the St. Helens hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Licensing Changes.</p>
        <p>At the Warringcun Police Court on Monday, temporary authority was granted to John Howard to sell at an off •lice in BAweey-road, in lieu of John Rigby.—William 04borne was granted temporary authority to selllat the" Gown. Angel" Inn, in lieu of John Howard, and Peter H Lake to sell at an off-licence in Froghall-lane in lieu of Samuel I; Smallwood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>Ship Canal Cotton Season . .</head>
        <p>The Ship Canal Cotton Season . .</p>
        <p>- • The Manchester Ship Canal cotton season is vir- tually at an end. It has been a disappointing ene, for it shows a decrease of 11,157 bales as compared with the previous season, though there has been en increase of 7,372 bales in comparison with 1899- 1900 In regard ta the Egyptian skipment there is an increase of over 20,000 bale c,m`pa,ed with las season, bat a decrease of 10,000 bales compare with the record 1899-1900</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Funeral of the Late Mr..,S._Chandley, J P.</head>
        <p>Funeral of the Late Mr..,S._Chandley, J P.</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Mr Silvanus Chew)lev, J P., Okville, Latchford, took piece at the War- riugton Cemetery at neon on Monday. and a large number of townspeople assembled to pay a last tribute of respect to the deceased gentleman. The cortege was headed by a detachment of police under the direction of Chief Inspector Phillips. A nuuaber of present and pest employees of the firm of Messrs S Chandley and Son, of which deceased was the head, attended and acted as bearers. The chief mourners were Messrs Arthur, %film, Charles and S Bertram Chaadley (sons), Miss Dorothy Cbandley, Mrs Kirkpatrick, and Mr. liesketh ' (daughters). Mr E T Milner (eon-in-law), Mr George Kirkpatrick (son-in-law). and Mr Hy. Baxter (nephew). The members of the Town Council present included Alder- men. Fairclough. Sutton and Shaw Green, Councillors Rmethurst,Forshaw,Blmps3n,Pierpoint, Barton. and Dunesu with the Town Clark (Mr J Lyon Whittle), Dr J Guest Gornall (medical officer of health) and Mr. Frederick Taylor (secretary of the Gas Department). There were also present Dr Fox, Messrs F Monks, J W Hayward, T Birch all, Garnett, John Fairclough. R T Faircluugh, :Charles White, and S Wright (justices of the peace) G F F White (magistrates' clerk), R G Heashall, David Garnett,E Clarej Hesketh,and F ClThompson and Dr Joseph. The officiating clergyman was the Rev R Greenall. Rector of Grappsnhall, and the Rev E Farrar, curate of Grappenhall was present. There were no flower', by request. The Mayor (Mr. J C Parr), who was prevented from attending the ob- sequies owing to an engagement in Manchester, received letters exprersing regret at their ability to attend from Mr William Owen and Alderman II Roberts, The latter gentleman wrote : "lam very sorry that an important appointment prevents me from paying a last token of respect to the memory of the late llr Chaudley."</p>
        <p>Archdeacon Wynne Jones, vim of TilLlangollen, was cycling at Llangollen, when a dog ran into his machine, throwing the rider violently against the wall. The rev. gentleman had one shoulder dislocated,and was rendered uneonseious.but is reported to be progressiag favJUrably.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>EVENTS OF THE WEEK</head>
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEEK</p>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED.</p>
        <p>It is fourteen years since the Blue Riband oi marksmanship was won by a Londoner, but the King's Prizeman at Risley this year is Lieutenant E. D. Johnson, of the London Rifle Brigade, and he is much to be complimented on his success for the conditions under which the shooting took place were the worst ever known in the history of the contest. There were moments during the afternoon when competitors had to make an allowance at 1,000 yards of 30 to 40 feet for wind, and Lieut.</p>
        <p>Johnson himself</p>
        <p>informed a newp- paper rep.resenta-</p>
        <p>iiie that in firing</p>
        <p>his last round he</p>
        <p>not only</p>
        <p>three feet-of wind</p>
        <p>provided for "on his rifle," but he</p>
        <p>actually aimed at the left edge of _</p>
        <p>the target ;n the left of that upon which he hoped</p>
        <p>to make the</p>
        <p>bull's-eye : The</p>
        <p>chief e;eht of the</p>
        <p>National</p>
        <p>Association Meet-</p>
        <p>ing has now been won by England en twenty: six</p>
        <p>occasions, Scot- land fourteen, Wales, Guernsey, and Canada once each. The King's Prizeman is only twenty-six years of age. and is a chartered accountant. He was born at Leicester and educated at King's College School. He joined the Cadet Corps of the London Rifle Brigade at an early age, commenced shooting at fourteen, and has been connected with the L.R.B. ever since. He is the champion shot of the City of London Rifle Associa- tion, but has not hitherto achieved any note- worthy success at Bisley. The Lieutenant has the distinction of being not only the best shot in the Kingdom, but a man who was rejected by the military authorities for service in South Africa! According to one of his friends, Johnson offered himself as a trooper in the C.LV. and was refused. Nothing daunted, however, he went to South Africa on his own account, and joined the Rough Riders, with whom he took part in many engage- ments, and was mentioned in despatches for gallantry. The Wesleyans must be congratulated upon the site they have secured for their new Church House, although in the opinion of some people the price they are paying for the Royal Aquarium at West- minster is rather high. However that is a matter which concerns only the Connexion, and that body has a reputation for business acumen. Westminster, always, thanks to the venerable Abbey, associated with the religious life of the nation, is becoming</p>
        <p>THE ROYAL AQUARIUM, WESTMINSTER</p>
        <p>THE SITE OP THE PROPOSED TIEW WESLEYAN CHURCH HOUSE.</p>
        <p>more and more the spiritual hub of England. The Church House of the Anglicans stands behind the Abbey, and not far away is the great new Roman Catholic Cathedral, DOW nearing completion. But in many respects the Wesleyan Church House, when it is built, will occupy a better position than either the Anglican Church House or the Roman Catholic Cathedral, while the site is ?much to be preferred to that of the Congre- gational Hall, that imposing building in Farringdon Street not far from Ludgate Circus. The Royal Aquarium, which we suppose will shortly dis- appear along with the Imperial Theatre forming part of the same block of buildings, was opened twenty-six years agb by the Duke of Edinburgh, and has had a chequered career. Since that time much that has been done within its walls has made history of a modest kind. The National Liberal Club was born within its precincts in 1843, Lord Granville being in the chair, and Mr. Gladstone the principal speaker. Three thousand people dined there at that gathering. The Brigade of Gnards was here entertained on their return from the Egyptian War in 1882 by the Duke of Cambridge and the Duke of Connaught. Liberal Unionists held their inaugural dinner in St. Stephen's Hall when the party was formed in 1886, and the Duke of Argyll presided. And if one speaks of artists and enteminers who have appeared upon its boards it is difficult to know where to end. It is hardly likely that the new Church House will occupy more than half the site (over 100,000 square feet). At present, according to Mr. Perks, M.P., it is pro- posed to erect a monumental building in which there will be a hall to seat three thousand persons, a second hall to seat one thousand people, provision for a library, and a home for the various depart- ments of Methodism. This building would face the Abbey and cover an area of about 40,000 feet, leaving some 60,000 feet of land to be disposed of as might be thought proper.</p>
        <p>The marriage of Earl Beauchamp and Lady Lettioe Grosvenor which was solemnised at Eccles- ton Church, near Chester, on Saturday, was the most important and most brilliant wedding of the season. The floral decorations at the church were very beautiful, and the bride and her twelve brides- maids made a charming picture. Lady Lettice is noted for her picturesque taste in dress.and on Satur- day she was attired in a " Juliet " gown, an exact EARL BEALTCHAMP.\</p>
        <p>LADY LETTICE oltosvmort. TIE MOST BRILLIANT WEDDING OF THE SEASON. reproduction of the dress of a noble Italian lady of the Middle Ages. On the soft white satin, pieces cf fine silvered lace fell in a straight line from the shoulders, back and front. The high shoulder collar was also of the silver lace, and the quaint sleeves had cuffs coming half over the hands. A wreath of myrtle, jessamine, and orange flowers crowned her lovely brown hair, which was entirely covered with a long and beautiful lace veil. The officiating clergy were the Bishop of Worcester the Rev. Prebendary H. Montague Villiers, and the Rev. G. Robins, and many notable people were present. At the conclusion of the ceremony the guests drove to Eaton Hall, where the reception was held prior to the departure of the newly-wedded pair to the bridegroom's fine old Elizabethan mansion, Madresfield Court, Worcester. shire. Lord Beauchamp is only thirty, but he is already a man of affairs, and has filled such varied positions as Member of the School Board for London and Governor of New South Wales. He has also:been Mayor of Worcester, where he is very popular. The bride is four years younger than her husband, and is a sister of the Duke of West- in inate r andlthe Countess of Shaftesbury.</p>
        <p>Mites</p>
        <p>The Australians will go back to their owss country with light hearts and heavy purses after a. 'very satisfactory visit to the Old Country! By Ltheir victory at Manchester last Saturday they wo i n the rubber n the series of test matches. and with that the great object of their tour was accomplished. This is the actual state of the circumstances, but it must be owned that morally their victory is a very small one. Of the four games played two have been drawn one enormously in our favour, and two. • won, the last by the narrow margin of three runs, the most sensational finish on record. and it must be impartially admitted that, take them all round,</p>
        <p>LOCKWOOD.</p>
        <p>HEROES OF THE TEST MATCH.</p>
        <p>the test matches have been conspicuous for bad play on both sides. Hardly any of the leading players on either side have done themselves justice in these important matches. Darling, the Australian skipper, who did very well at Manchester, had been up till then the failure of the series. C. B. Fry and Ranjitsinjhi, our two greatest bats, have proved lamentable failures, and whether it was the wicket or the weightiness of the occasion, the per- formances all round have been very bad. Victor Trumper, who has been the success of the Australian tour, is the brilliant exception, and the victory of the visitors in the rubber is largely due to his fine play. In analysing the last match it will be seen that in the first innings of the Australians four men scored double figures. and in the second two, while on the English side three scored double figures in the firc„..and four in the second innings, Darling and Tyldesley alone exceeding single figures in both. This is, indeed, a sorry show for such a game! Trumper's century was a brilliant cxpo-itiou ; but Jackson's, coming as it did at a time of "rot," was even better, and with him Braund must again be honourably men- tioned. The bowling honours of the match fall to Lockwood, with 6 wickets for 48 runs in the first innings of the Australians, and 5 for 28 in the second, and it only makes the ordinary man wonder what MacLaren was doing in not putting him on earlier in the first innings I True, we have been beaten in the rubber, but still we feel, without any lack of good sportsmanship, that our beating has been neither satisfactory nor conclusive.</p>
        <p>The latest visitor expected in London is the Shah of Persia, who has for some weeks past been in France, taking the waters at Contrexe- vine, and enjoying himself in the gay capital of his favourite republic. Of all the Oriental potentites the Shah is one of the best known in the West, as this is not his first visit either to England or France, and he is noted for the very lavish fashion in which he throws his money about during his European tours.</p>
        <p>Pleasant as this is, it does not al-</p>
        <p>together __appeal</p>
        <p>to- his Ministers</p>
        <p>or his subjects, and the greatest objections were</p>
        <p>journey west- wards ; and in</p>
        <p>the end the result was more or less</p>
        <p>of a compromise</p>
        <p>between the Shah</p>
        <p>and his advisers,</p>
        <p>as it was decideci that he should</p>
        <p>Wl3O IS TO VISIT ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>visit Europe and</p>
        <p>take the Cure at</p>
        <p>Contrexeville, but should not represent Persia at the June Coronation, as he personally desired to do. The objection to this was not in any way un- friendly in motive, but entirely due to the extremely extravagant expenditure he indulges in upon the least provocation. He is an absolute ruler with the whole revenue of his, country at his disposal according to the constitu- tion of Persia but his Ministers nevertheless do not find it altogether easy to meet his large demands upon the national exchequer. This fault at home does not, however, render him unpopular abroad, and he was not only an amusing, but also a particularly profitable visitor to the last Paris Exhibition. Nevertheless, he received a great fright at the hands of a lunatic, who made a clumsy sort of attempt on his life,`but fortunately without serious result. This affair upset the Shah very much at the time, but did not have any deter- rent upon his plans for this summer. As usual' he will be the centre of considerable interest and .• curiosity in London.</p>
        <p>Glorious oodwocxl, as the popular Sussex race- gathering is always called after more than a century of success, is accounted by many the most delightful meeting of the whole year. While one of the great functions of Society, it entirely lacks the stiffness and formality of Ascot. and is, at the same time, free from the motley mob which crowds the Epsom carnival. Moreover, for natural sur- roundings and beauty it. is impossible to beat it any where in England.standinfr, as the course does, in the midst of the Duke of Richmond's grand park near Chichester, which is richly wooded with tall pines, which ward off the blazing midsummer sun and keep the lawns beautifully cool. Though within easy reach ot London. Chichester is just far enough from the metropolis to keep it free from the majority of the usual racecourse rabble, though, of course, a rough element naturally finus its way up from the purlieus of Port.4mouth. For the, most part, however, the spectators are drawn. from Brighton and the country round, as the local people always flock to the meeting Goodwood 'Park, while the great houses</p>
        <p>7.4.' • Aly\ : A ,•,\ " LIEUT. E. D. JOHNSON TIrE PRIZEMAN.</p>
