      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>SKETCHES AT GOODWOOD.</head>
        <p>SKETCHES AT GOODWOOD.</p>
        <p>in the neighbourhood invariably have parties for the week. At Goodwood Park the Duke of Richmond always entertains and generally is honoured by the presence of royal guests, and next to it in import- ance comes West Dean Park, where Mr and Mrs. Tames entertained the King for Goodwood week two years ago. This year, though naturally hie Majesty was compelled to be absent for the second year in succession, the attendance was as large as over, and the meeting was a conspicuous sucoess. In addition to the usual faces at Goodwood, there were a large number of the Indian and Colonial visitors for the Coronation—to say nothing of the inevitable American, who was out in large force. The racing itself was of very fair quaility on the whole, but the new conditions for the Cup were much oriticind.</p>
      </div>
