      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>instruction in all schools which receive aid from the rates and taxes be entirely under public con- trol. An hour in every day could then be set apart in which the various Churches could make their own arrangements for religious instruction.</p>
        <p>MANY and various are The Unionist Press the reasons which the and the North Leeds Election. Unionist papers give for the Leeds defeat. The " Yorkshire Post "attributes the disaster to the Education Bill, and that journal, published in Leeds, is in the best position to know how the contest was fought. But the London organs of the party, with a delightful unconsciousness of what they are doing, proceed to fill up the Govern- ment's cup of bitterness. The " Times " is unkind enough to say that a reaction has set in, and it warns the Govern- ment not to assume that local or personal reasons account for the amazing transfer oc support. The " Telegraph" holds that with the ending of the war the normal action of the pen- dulum has been rastored. If that be true many Conservative M.P.'s should lose their seats at the next general election. The "Morning Post "is frank in acknowledging', that there does exist throughout the constituencies " a considerable amount of dissatisfaction with the Government." The" Globe" regards the result as to some extent due to the spread of dissatisfaction at the wan. of vigour in the Ministerial policy. We are glad that these declarations come from the Govern ment's own organs for in these circumstances they cannot be said to be prejudiced. In truth the judg- ment of Leeds is a cumulative one, and although the Liberal rally was mainly on account of the obnoxious education proposals it is well to remem- ber that it has been stimulated by a number of other causes. The electorate, .as Mr. BURNS remarked, has been disillusioned as to the war bogey. We mention this first because people are inclined to overlook that persistent misrepresenta- tions caused persons who were Liberal in all their sentiments to vote Tory at the last general elec- tion lest the return of a Liberal should seem to indicate divided counsels to our enemies. I Although the Liberal party in the House of Commons voted the war supplies ungrudgingly they were vilified on Tory platforms as traitors ready to open our gates to the enemy. Such calumnies cannot be circulated any longer. The scales have fallen from the eyes of the electors, and they are appraising the Government at its real value. They are not satisfied with a Cabinet in which our commerce is represented by an amiable scholar, Mr. GERALD BALFOUR, utterly out of touch with the realities of life. They are not enamoured of a system under which education is controlled by a fainetant like the DuKE of DEVONSHIRE, and a flaneur like Sir JOHN GORST ; and under which the Navy, the guarantee for our existence as a Great Power, has for its chief an amiable peer without distinction or force of character. Elements like these are enough to chill the faith of the country ; and when there is super-added to them the feeling of strong resent- ment against the calculated injustice of the Education Bill, it is no wonder that the nation gives the Ministry notice to quit.</p>
        <p>Though theiShip Canal is not altogether a boon and a blessing,it brings to our shores, oecasiOnally, things other than smells. Once we had a visit of torpedo-boat destroyers and all was excitement. On Wednesday there came up the waterway two boat-loads of picked Indian troops over for the Coronation, and excitement ran high again. The banks were lined for miles by thousands of enthu- siastic spectators who gave the dusky visitors a right hearty welcome. Undoubtedly, the appear- ance of the troops was a revelation to many who imagined that Indians from India must be merely black fellows—certainly very brave—but of little account except as fightiLg men. The scores of handsome, intelligent faces which peered up at the crowds from the decks of the steamers were a complete disillusionment. Each man looked more like a prince than a "common soldier," some of the tall turbaned warriors being particularly fine fellows. In such a brief glance as was afforded in passing the different races could not be readily singled out, but at Latchford Locks there was a• better opportunity. Here one noticed the quietly- uniformed but business-like little Goorkhas, whose reputation as fighters; is second to none, Bengal Lancers, Hindoo Infantry, Afridis, and Afghans, almost all looking "as pleased as Punch." The cordiality of the crowd was heartily reciprocated by the men from the East, who cheered and counter-cheered in true British fashion as they slowly sailed on their way to Manchester.</p>
        <p>England has, after all,lost the rubber, the match at Old Trafford resulting in favour of the Austra- lians by three runs—the narrowest margin ever recorded in a test match between England and Australia. A game more remarkable for its vicis- situdes and fluctuations of fortune we do not re- member. From first to last interest and excite- ment never flagged, while the issue was in doubt to the very end. There is no need to recapitulate the chief incidents of the game. They are fresh in the memory of all, but it may not be unprofit- able to discuss the why and wherefore of the Australian triumph. That we have as good cricketers here as there are in Australia, the most pessimistic of us can hardly doubt. Man for man the English side was the stronger of the two in this last test match, but as a team and a combination our visitors and conquerors are some way ahead of us. There seems to be a greater application of intelligence to the game on the part of the Aus- tralians. They are not content to play cricket merely as it is played according to rule and custom. Each man has a distinct individuality on the field and the way all combine to make the circum- stances fit the case, as it were, is a source of strength which the Englishmen cannot tap. As a fielding side they are immeasurably superior and it is all due to this intelligent application of the brain to the work the hands and feet have to do. In batting and bowling we can hold our own, but the field-work of the Australians is an art which will have to be mastered ere we can rely on even I evident superior strength to win us the laurels.</p>
        <p>A writer on one of the evening papers, who is both of a statistical and of an observant mind, has some interesting things to say on the express trains of the year, reviewing what each railway has to boast of, or to be criticised for. He finds little novel to record in the railway race to Scot- land. The companies are doing good work, but are making no sensational bids individually for the notice of those in a hurry to get to the moors. The longest run on a Scotch express he finds is that from Wigan to Willesden by the West Coast ' route, a distance of 188 k miles being performed in 3hrs. 41rain., an average rate of speed of 51.1 miles an hour. There is, however, on the East Coast route a run which, if only 156 miles in length—that from King's Cross to Doncas- ter—is taken at • a speed of 511 miles, the run occupying altogether 3hrs. lmin. Yet a faster booked speed is that of the North-Eastern train from Darlington to York. The run is only one of 441 miles, but it is done in 43 minutes, which gives the speed of 611 miles per hour. This is said to be the fastest booked speed in the United King- dom, as well as the fastest ever scheduled in any British time-table. It has a close competitor, however, in a Midland train, which, running from Appleby to Carlisle, covers the distance of 30:1 miles in 30 minutes, a speed of 61.5 miles per hour. Even in the journey between London and the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>EXAMINER FRI Y AUGUST 1, 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRI Y AUGUST 1, 1902</p>
        <p>big Midland towns there an some improvements shewn this year,the longer journeys being acceler- ated in several cases by from five minutes to a quarter of an hour. This is all satisfactory enough in its way, but we wish we could be quite sure that the achievement of sensational speeds by certain trains was being accompanied in all cases by a sufficient effortto secure absolute punctuality by all. •</p>
        <p>Lord Kitchener's final despatch reviewing the' conduct of the officers in the war is excellent read- ing after our many bitter experiences in the cam- paign. It makes us feel that the General who will have most to do with any war in which the present generation of Britishers may be engaged is pro- vided with a splendid list of qualified "officers upon 1 whom he can rely. It is notorious that when the war was at its height, and cssualities and disease were deciminating the ranks, a great many young gentlemen were moved into the Army with a hasty military education. The Army coaches had to deplore that for the time being their occupation was practically gone. The examinations were made so easy that the students needed very little assist- ance. That state of things,of course will not go on. It is the intention to give the Army the best that theory and practice can afford it; but the theory can be added any time, and the practice in actual warfare is rare. We prided ourselves that our little wars in various parts of the globe had taught us all we wanted to know; but we found we were mistaken, and, as a matter of fact, some of these engagements with raw tribes may tend rather to disturb the balance of judgment in encouraging movements not sound against a stronger foe. Lord Kitchener has found out for himself the distin- guishing qualities of those who were most success- ful in serving him. He has also been careful to note the good points of others against whom the war went hardly. Colonel Clements, for example, he describes as possessing fiae soldierly qualities which his misfortunes served to accentuate rather than obscure. Observations like these do honour to a commander as well as to their subject. •</p>
        <p>Another satisfactory balance sheet was laid before the shareholders of Parr's Bank, Limited,at the half-yearly meeting held at Warrington last Thursday. Notwithstanding a difference of nearly one per cent. in the Bank of England rate, the profits exceed those of a year ago by a few thou- sand pounds. A dividend of 19 per cent. was once more declared and a balance of over one hun- dred thousand pounds was carried to the next account. The bank may be said to have reached the high-water mark of bank profits and the Chairman seemed disposed to think that the tendency in the future would be for banking pre- fits to grow smaller, relatively to the money employed. Sir John Harwood took a more optimistic view and considered that the volume of business would increase to such an extent as to balance any diminished rate of profit. A passing reference itas made by the Chairman to the proposed reduc- won in the rate of interest allowed to dspositors in Savings Banks. For many years it has been a sore point with bankers that the Government has paid .a higher rate of interest to depositors in Savings Banks than private bankers could afford to pay. The payment of 24 per cent to deposi- tors in Savings Banks has involved a loss which the taxpayer has had to recoup. It is now pro- posed to reduce the rate of interest by half-a- crown per hundred pounds, or as the bankers pre- fer to call it per cent. The reduction, in Mr Parr's opinion, is "a miserably inadequate one," but he comforted himself with the reflection that bankers must be content with small mercies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD INQUIRY AT RUNCORN. ---- The Isolation Hospital.</head>
        <p>LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD INQUIRY AT RUNCORN. ---- The Isolation Hospital.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday morning a local Government Board inquiry was opened to consider the application of the Runcorn District Council to borrow £l,OOO to purchase a site for an Isolation Hospital.—Mr Spruce, a member ns the Council, was present with the officials and Dr Vascher (county medical officer of health) also attended. Mr Ashton made a statement as to the population and the assessable value of the district, and said the site proposed to be purchased W 39 a most central one. Mr Vascher said that the County Council for some number of years had been pressing the District Ceuncil on the matter but it was not until the Isolation Hospital Act 1893 had been amended that they were able to do more ,than plan. They were now able to pay *of the cost for the structural expenses. The j lint hoTteal Bard on the Narthwich Union had purchased a site for a similar purpose and the Bucklo 77 Union was alsa mov- ing in the matter. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr Spruce thought the vibration of the railway which passed close by the site would prove a draw back. Mr Vascher replied he did not apprehend any diffi- cuDy in that respect. The Inspector observed that the site in its proximity to tbe railway was identctl with many other similar sites throughout the country. The Inspector afterwards visited the site.</p>
      </div>
