      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>SHOCKING WIDNES HOME.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING WIDNES HOME.</p>
        <p>At Widnes'S—e-ssre-a;;;laM;s-1.%;3-(1 Rose Ann Mitchell living in Water-street, were charged with neglect- ing their five children in a manner likely to cause them unneceosary suffering and injury to health. Mr W 8 Knowles prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.O C. Inspector Hackett said that from Febru- ary things had gone on from bad to worse at the prisoners' house. It was in a filthy state, and the children were alive with vermin. The household effects were pawned,and the 30s per week given to the woman she spent in drink. To sum up the situation in which they lived it was nothing else but rags and filth.—Police-sergeant Ratcliffe also gave evidence.—The case against the man was dis- missed, but the woman wee sent to gaol for six months. SEVERAL LADIES SECOND-RANDIRICYCLES for Wis.—GARLAND, 51, Bridge-st, Warrington.—Advt. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC its Tam HINGE OF TONICS.-63 SANKEY-STRERT Warrington.—(AdVt). •</p>
