      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SIR J. T. BRUNNER IN THE SPEAKER'S CHAIR. Con6ilerable amusement was caused by Sir J T Brunner taking a sea', in the Speaker's chair at the House of Commons about 1-30 on Monday morning He was cheered by the Irish members. PENAL SERVITUDE FOR OLD OFFENDERS.</p>
        <p>At Liverpool Assizes on Saturday, Patrick M'Creese (35), and Patrick Swift (28), labourers, both old offenders, for having at Widnes, on the 12th July, received and stolen respectively a pair of boots, a coat, and a vest, the property t Thomas Wood, were sentenced to four years' penal servi- tude each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>~--,... BODY RE-COVERED FROM THE SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>~--,... BODY RE-COVERED FROM THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>The body of 1--n-t-;tive of Flint, who had been missing from the ketch Ariel, lying at Weston Point, since July 22nd, was recovered from the Ship Canal on Monday mornina it was seen floating on the water by William dam, dock gate- man, of Weston Cottages, who brought it to the side and gave information to the police. The body was removed to the Weaver Hotel. At the subse- quent inquest an open verdict was returned. FARM FIRE NEAR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Shortly ei-f;;--Mid-u-cih-C—on—Sti-n-dai-a:fira was dis- covered to have broken out in the stables, shippons, and cart-house on the farm of kir John Tomlinson, Sea-lane, Halton. The contents of the buildings, including six tons of hay, were destroyed. Informa- tion was sent to Runcorn and tee fire brigade turned but were too late to be of any material assistance. The damage is estimated at £BB. The hay is supposed to have been set alight by spon- taneous combustion. The buildings and contents were fully insured. MASONIC PICNIC. The annual masonic excursion in connection with the Ellesmere Lodge No. 758 took place on Wed- nesday, when a goodly number of members and friends journeyed to Rhyl. The headquarters for the day were "The Westminster Hotel!' After par- taking of dinner the party were conveyed in brakes through Unmet Park, the seat of the Lord Lieucenant of Flintshire. After tea the party visited many of the surrounding places of interest. The home journey was made in good time, Runcorn being reached by 9-15. The arrangements were carried out by Mr T H Anne,t, secretary, who con- tributed materially to the success of the trip. SHOCKING- DEATH IT WIDNES DOCKS, At Widnes on Saturday, Mr S Brighouse held an inquest on the body of Thomas Comiskey, aged thirty-four, of Cromwell-street. The coroner stated that deceased was unloading copper ore from the flat Kidd on Wednesday. After the word was given to hoist the first tub, one bolt connect- ing two chains to the jib broke, and the jib falling struck Comiskey, causing instant death. William Rends', fitter, said that the presswe on the chain ' caused by the tub getting fast under the combings was responsible for the breakage. Mr Orr, his Majesty's inspector, contended that the pressure on the belt was sery little, and it, should have been the last thing to break. He would suggest that bolts of cranes should be made of good metal,turned and driven into the socket firmly and evenly.—A verdict of" Accidental death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>PARISII CHURCH CHOIR PICNIC.</head>
        <p>PARISII CHURCH CHOIR PICNIC.</p>
        <p>The janior members of the choir of the Parish Church had their annual picnic on Saturday. The lads themselves are privileged to suggest the place where it shall be to, and Southport, with its many I attractions, was named and decided upon. Con- sequently, on Saturday morning between twenty and thirty bright, cloudless faces belonging to the aforesaid lads were set towards that part of the compass where Southport lies. The brightness of the choristers waa in marked contrast to the skies, which looked sullen and ominous. Southport was wet and cheerless, the donkeys on the sands had lost much of their vigour, and the boats at Church- town Hardens were .damp in the extreme. Visions of mustard and water and hot drinks loomed largely, but up to the time of writing the drenching has had no ill effects. Nothing damped the spirits of the bop, who enjoyed themselves to their hearts content, and on the return journey being accom- plished voted that the outing had been splendid, and cheered loudly for those who had looked to the arrangements. lie party was accompanied by the Rev H B Firth, Mr F P Boraston (organist and choirmaster), and Mr John Littler (churchwarden). Mr W E Dudley had made the arrangements for the journey, catering. &amp;c., and everything passed off without the slightest hitch. MEETING OF THE SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>- -- The half-yearly meeting of the Cheshire Grand Division of the Sons of Temperance was held on Saturday afternoon at Halton in the Village Hall. There was a large attendance of representatives from Winsford, Crewe, Northwich, Halton, Weston, and Runcorn. Mr John Sanderson, of York, MW, grand trustee,presided. In the course of his address he emphasized the importance of their work, and madelan earnest and eloquent appeal to the young men present to engage in temperance work. He referred to his own personal experience in the early days of the movement and alluded to the opposition they had to meet in these days. A letter wag read from Mr Samuel Dodd,G W P.resigning his position owing to having left the district. Mr Alfred Price, of Crewe, was appointed to the office. The various reports were read by the officers. Mr Thomas J Holt, grand scribe, read a report in which he dealt with the condition of the order. He urged the secretaries to be more prompt in sending their re- turns. Including the cadets there are 901 members. The total funds amount to .£1,066 Ss 4i,l!an increase in the half-year of MS 5s Sd. The treasurer, Mr Thomas Heesom, presented his report, which was considered very satisfactory. Mr John Brown, G patron of cadets, urged the members to renewed efforts in this department of their work.—lt wa decided that the next Grand Division meeting should be held at Crewe early in next year.—A pleasing feature of the meeting was a vieit of the prominent officers of the Earlestown Grand Division who gave encouraging addresses. Messrs James Johnson and Alfred Noden. the auditors, gave their reports, which were accepted. An excellent tea was provided in the Wesleyan Schoolroom and was most appreciated by the members. A vote of thanks was passed to the ladies on the motion of Mr Thomas Heesom, supported by Mr W Bisbrown and J Pearson. This was considered to be one of the best meetings ever held in the district. aka DEATH OF A SAILOR—INQUEST. On Tuesday evening Mr R Dobson, coroner held an inquest at the Weaver Hotel, Weston Point, relative to the death of Francis Kenny, of Flint, a seaman on board the ketch "Ariel " of Chester, who was found drowned in the ship canal on Monday morning. James Robertson, of Park View Terrace, Dundee,eeaman on the echeoner Sarah Ann Widdup, of Fleetwood, said on Tuesday evening the 22nd July he was in company with the deceased from 6.30 p.m to 9.20 p.m. They were in the public house opposite the post office for a good while. They had four pints of beer each. They then went to the Watermen's Arms. They had two pints of beer each. Deceased "began to get arguing with the people there." A young woman—he thought it was the daughter of the licensee, asked witness to take him aboard.—The Coroner: Was he drunk? Well, you known he was wanting to fight.—l suppose it is rather difficult to tell when a man is drunk? Yes it is. —Witness (continuing) said he got the deceased into Post Office lane when he wanted to go to 'Runcorn and said he (witness) should not stop him. Deceased struck him on the chest and squared up to fight. He never touched deceased. Deceased .was very nimble on his feet but the drink seemed to get into his head: Witness was steady and remembered all the circumstances —By Superintendent Ennion: Deceased was a perfect stranger to him. Deceased was drunk but he thought he was abte to get aboard his vessel.—Harold iiill,a youth, of Guernsey, cook on the Sarah Ann Widdup, stated that the last wit- ness -asked him to try and get deceased on his ship. On reaching the Church,deceased asked" Do you belong to the Widdup." he replied," Yes." Deceased then said "I want nothing to do with the Widdep " and knocked witness down. Deceased fell down but got up and ran beck towards Weston Point. Deceased had had a lot of drink but could walk straight —William Ham Weaver Cottages, Weston Point, dock gateman, testified to finding the bo-ly in the Ship Canal on Monday morning.—P.C. Row- lands said there were no marks of violence on the body. Re found in the pockets of the clothing a knife, a pipe and Is 4d in monev.—The jury returned an opn verdict of" Found dead."</p>
