      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>LBY A CORRESPONDENT].</p>
        <p>The The!wall nuisance is not yet lost to sight though many think it is. As a matter of fact the nuisance is still in existence. Mr Naylor evidently contemplates some fresh move for he has asked to be supplied with the bacteriological analyais made by Dr Adams of the water on Mr Naylor's estate. There may be some more or less startling develope- mente ere lung.</p>
        <p>The excessive vaccination chargi,s of the doctors still agitate the minds of our worthy Guardians. On Tuesday such a glaring enormity was unearthed by Mr Linaker that the result was a series of ex- plosions not unlike a " rip-rap " firework. In answer to a question the clerk pointed out that a doctor outside radius could charge is 61 for each person vaccinaTed in a house at a given rime. Loul were the murmurs of oisgust from the mem- bers at this pronouncement. Mr Linaker (scath- ingly); it is monstrous! The chairman, Mr Stokes (surprisedly); It is ridiculous!! Mr Nlclntyre (horrified); Scandalous!! ! Further explosions were smothered in the general expressions of dis- approval.</p>
        <p>The Sun lay schools presented Mr Taylor on Tues- day with a gold medal indicative of the esteem in which he is held by the various bodies. The pre- sentation was made by Mr Simistsr in the Technical Institute and there were present representatives from all the religious organisations of the town. Mr Taylor welldeservas this memento of the Coronation festivities and it will doubtless 133 long prized by him and his family.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT . COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT . COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The focznig4tly meuting ol the :store authority was held on Tuesda7 t Dutt:nr Workhouie. There were present :--11r. Knowles (on airman), Mesirs. S Wood- ward (vica•chairrnan), 0 Poylancf!..E Clarke,J Xillntyre, T Dariingtm,T EUi r. J M)seley-Wiiiiatna W ti Bradford, T Wright, H J Woodward, G Pilling, P Gleave. R .13dlington, Admiral J P Jones PArry, T Antwiss, J W Hlugh, (acting clerk), W Diggle and J Svintoo. (sarvegors) and J F.rrington (Ligon- tor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>AQUEDUCT AT MOORE,</head>
        <p>THE AQUEDUCT AT MOORE,</p>
        <p>Mr E Mackie writing from the London and Nortl- Western Railway Offices, London with reference to the sewage tank over the railway at Moore said there was no doubt that the use by th cansl of the aqueduct over the railway at More for tile parpose of carrying the sewer i 3 a distioa gain to the Coancil. If the aqueduct had not been there the sewer would have had to be laid across the railway at considerable cost to the Council ani he thought the Council would upon consideration find that the terms of the draft agreement which were before them were not at all of an unreasonable nature. r After con,iderable discussion it is as resolved that MPRITS GINAre, Royllnce, Mawdsley, Darlington, and ' Piling form a committee to consider the tnattor and re- port to the Council.</p>
        <p>A VACANCY AT lIELSBY. Mr S Woodward moved that in consequence of Mr James Taylor, of Efelsby, having vacated his seat on the Council by b3lng absent f3r more than six months the office of Rural District councillor recently held by him be declared vacant.</p>
        <p>Mr ',maker seconded and the propolition w&amp; pacsad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>THELIVALL NUISANCE.</head>
        <p>THE THELIVALL NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr R A Naylor wrote stating that; ttyl medical officer hado,submitted to the Council some time ago a bacterio- logical report on the drinking water on his premises. He asked that he be furnished with this rep irt. The Clerk said that he had written to Mr Naylor stat- ing that the medical offic-r was at present away but that on his return the report would bo forwarded to him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>FIRE AT DITTON OIL WORKS.</head>
        <p>FIRE AT DITTON OIL WORKS.</p>
        <p>Admirable Work by the Fire Brigade.</p>
        <p>The Ditton Oil Works, also known as Bibby's Oil Works. which are situate upon Bale Marsh,neer Widnes. were the scene of a disastrous fire on Friday evening. It seems that about six o'clock a leakage occurred in one of the pipes running to or from the still and the escaping gas or vapour spa.dily became ignited, the flames being from the first fierm and vehement, since they were well fed owinz to the extremely infirm-sable nature of oil. The employes engaged in the work at once set to work with sterling determination to hold the fire in check and to save as much of the property as possible a massage being in the meantime sent down to the Widnes Fire Brigade. The works fire apparatas was brought into use, but the men found themselves hampered severely owing to a slack pressure from the mains. The fire speedily worked its way to the roof, and then attached itself to an ad- joining works, that of Messrs Maloney and Sans, dse makers. The Widnes Brigade, when they received the summons, turned out in the smartest possible manner and were away in a very few minutrs. The steamer accomplished the journey, which lay over an awkward sod difficult road, in record time, and took only some 20 minutes. Upon arrival the firemen were faced by other difficulties,but with a splendid spirit they sett, work under the direction of Deputy-SuperintendentE F Franklin and Foreman Beesley and laid hose to the brook some 700 yards from the works from which they were able to pump a good supply of water. Their efforts were chiefly confined to keeping the fire to as small a compass as possible, and in this way they succeeded beyond all expectations. The police, who numbered some 12 con- stables, under Sergeant Ratcliffe. assisted by Sergeant Twentyman, also rendered yeoman service, and a very large amount of highly combustible stuff, consisting of oil, resinous material, Ste.. was removed be the workers out of the range of the flames and the flying and burn- ing debris from the roof. The damage was net wry great and was only just over £l,OOO. Undoubtedly the work done by the firemen saved several thousand pounds worth of property. The loss is covered by insur- ance. The flames were finally subdued about 10 30 p.m., and the Widnes Briagade left jivt after midnight</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>NEW roN DIVISION_ CONSERVATIVE</head>
        <p>NEW roN DIVISION_ CONSERVATIVE</p>
        <p>-A S-0-diAtib.riT</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the Newton Divison Cot- servative Association was held on Saturday afternoon at the Central offices, Newton-le-Willows. Mr C B F Borron occupied the chair in the &amp;faience of Lord Newton. He was supported by C mei R Pilkingtoo, M.P., Dr Handyside, Messrs R Ruffin, W Holland Owen (hon. solicitor), J Catherall, J Simpson. G Mather, T Bowe*, ,J Smith, H Worsley, J Ruck, H Wiswell, W A. Mavvdsley (registration agent), and others. Colonel Pilkington proposed tho following motion : "That this quarterly meeting of the Newton Division of South-West Lancashire Conservative Association reipectfully tenders to his Majesty's Government its congratulations on the termination of the war in South Africa and on the successful way iin which peace has been restored, and desires to express its appreciation of the courage and det.rmination displayed by the Im- perial and Colonial forces arid the bravery shown by the gallint soldiers who had laid down their lives for their Kirg and country, and also to those wto with true devotion ministered to the wants of the wounded and dying throughout the war." The Chairman, seconded. —The motion was then passed. Dr Handyside submitted the next resolution as follows : " That this, tne quarterly ine,eting of the Newton Division of S lath-West L.ncsishin Conservative Association desires to convey to the most Honourable the. Marquis of Salisbury, Ic.G.. its regret on his retire- ment from the high office which he has so long held with credit to himself and to the bersefi'i of the country and to place on record its deep appreciation of the great and distinguished services he has rendered to the Empire and to express the hope that he may long be spared to enjoy the leisure he seeks, and which he has so well earned." Mr R Barton, in seconding,said the K a iser had utt lired very flittering remarks about Lord Salisbury. His re- tirement would be regretted not only by every right- thinking man in the United Kingdom, but in every</p>
        <p>English speaking country in the world.—The motion was then passed._ . . .</p>
        <p>Mr W Elolland Owen,in prop)sing the next resolution, which was as follows :" That this, the quarterly meeting of the Newton Division of South-Wesc Lanca- shire Association, wishes to place on record its hearts, approval of the appointment of the Rt ElOl3 A J Balfour to the office of Prime Minister, and is convinced that his great ability and adminhltrativa powers will teed to rurther the interests of the Empire, " said he would like to associate himself with the remarks of a previous speaker that it was a good thing for the country there was somebody able to follow in the footsteps of the late Prime Minister. Mr Balfour's experience gained in the laudarship of the House of Commons would stand him in good stead for the office he bad now taken up.—(Hear, hear). Mr J Catterall seconded.</p>
        <p>Colonel Pilkington spoke in support of the motion, which was duly passed.</p>
        <p>Coming Events.</p>
        <p>Tee annual sports in connection with the War- rington Football Club will be held on the Football Ground, Wilderspool-road, on 'Monday next (Bank Holiday). An attractive programme has been arranged and prizes to the value of £7O will be competed for. A new event this year will be the 200 yards scholars' race (open to scholars under 15 years of age, attending elementary schools). The other events are :-100 yards handicap, 220 yards handicap, 440 yards handicap, BSO yards handicap, 1 mile handicap. 1 mile bicycle handicap, 2 miles bicycle handicap, 1 mile bicycle novice handicap, and 9 atone boxing competition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>ST. HELENS TRADERS' ASSOCIAT!ON. The Society Promulgated.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS TRADERS' ASSOCIAT!ON. The Society Promulgated.</p>
        <p>DEFENCE</p>
        <p>N3twithstanding the stir that has been created in St. Helens during the last three or four weeks,during which a diversity of statement has been made as t 3 what the traders or shopkeepers intended doing to counteract the great devalopmbot of co- )perative tr,dinit in the town aud district, only on Wednesday night did the oppo,i- tion take definite shape. when a meeting was held at the Fleece Hotel, under the presidency of Councillor John Green, J.P. one of the leading grocers of the town, at which a Traders' Defence Association was 'formed.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, at the outset, r,ad the rules that bad been drawn up, which stated that the objects of the association were to promote the welfare of all traders by impressing 03 memoers the desirabilit). of trading only with thole woo supported private enterprise, and to watch over the interests of the trade as affected by legialatioo, co-operative stores anti otter influences, and to supply a means of defence and other advantages which could be more readily obtained by united action, and t maintain and e :force members' just riehts in all questions affecting their trade interests generally. The subscription Was fllC'd at:a maximum of 103 for the first year, with the proviso that a levy not extleeding 53 should be made if necessary. Mc, chairman explained that 5s would constitute intmberchip,but no doubt they would receive many contributions from small shopk ?ep- ers as expressions of sympathy. The rules were unanimously ai:opted. The Cnairman then said they were met that night for the first time in real harness. The Press had used the word "boycott," but they did not call it " boycott." They called it self-defence or preservation of their existence. They had received a large number of letters from all parts of the country, and fir that encourage- ment they were very thankful. In the flw Cases in which he bad been able to reply he bad advised traders to go and:do likewise. The point he wished to put before the meeting was that it was a question of dividend. If the stores would discontinue the dividend and let value be the true test, they as traders would disband , their association at once. They could afford to , enter int competition and hold their own. He aqkncl, to whom did the huge building in Baliwiri.stre,t belong. ! Supposing an individual who had bs3a a member of the stores for ten years left the Co-operative Society, bow much would he get when he left the town ? That wasa question that ought to be considered. Every indiviOual member had helped to build the stores, and if they were leaving the town they ought to be able to take a few bricks with them. The question was also one for the manufacturers of the town, and for the property ownere. The value of business premises had gone down very considerably, and if they went on as they ha:cl been for some years past, it would be a very serious matter indeed for the property owners. It was also a ratepayers' question, Take the land which the Corporation had cleared in Bridge-street, for example. NOW t oat the old property had been pulled down, the Corporation could no- get anyone to buy the land. If there had been no c .-operative stores, that property would have been invaluable. rks things stood, there were plenty of empty shops, and more would be empty if they went on as at present. He was glad to think that the action they %iv, re taking might stem the I tide, as they knew it Was already doing. The working man had been a great deal mentioned in reference to that movement, but in times of strikes who were the friends of the working men if not the private traders? It was a question for the tradesmen also, and he maintained that the tradesmen were under no obligation to employ assistants whose money was spent at the stores ,It Was a matter of life and death. Taey had put on their armour and meant to fight to the bitter end. They ' could live without the co operative stores, but the co- operative stores could not do without the private trad- r.</p>
        <p>_ (Yher tradesmen present spoke in support of the , movement, and Mr Bailey, for the shop assistants, said they had decided to support the tradesmen. Mr Guthrie said it ought to be explained that they had no hostility to trades unionism and trades societies with which they were in foil sympathy: They ought to mako that perfoctly clear. Oa the motion of Mr W 11 Pullins, seconded by Mr G Stringfellow, a large a-il represcntative committee was formed, with power to appoint:an executive committee. The Chairman, in a concluding speeeh, said they did not want to be aggressive. They were carrying on their opposition t the stores in a gent!emanly and basines like way, and he was quite sure all the traders would ; fall in with that view. Their object was self-preserva- tion, and they must rind wrould take rens-mat:de methods and means to gain a ltyelihood and eaisrence. Mr Murphy had asked his question in the House of Commons but he did not tee much satisfaction. H (Mr Green) never expected he would, hecgue they were not going to do anything, but what the stores had done ever since they stirtNl busioess in the town. The traders had a perfect right to say to pecpte who were supplied by them ; with funds that they ought in all reason to spend that money with private traders. A QUESTION IN PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>'ln the FlOll5O of Commons on Wednesday Mr Murphy I asked the Home Szcretary whcther his attention had been drawn to a meeting held last week at St Helens, when it was decided to form a combination to boycott people dealing with the looal co-operative society ; whether a viligance committee was started, and a list of workers and others having connection with the Co- operative Society exhibited ; and whether the Home Department had given any inatractions to magistrates to investigate the conduct of the shopkeepers engaged in these proceedings. Mr Ritchie : I have ne information on the subject other than that which has appeared in the newspapers, but in any ease I have no power whatever to give magistiates any instructions such a i those suggested in the question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT</p>
        <p>EARLESTO NN.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORREsPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>The inclement state of tha weather in this dis- trict on Saturday effectually put a stop to all cricket matches. Earlestown had made a special effort in connection with their fixture with War- rington,with the view of augmenting their finances which at the present time are not in a very flourishing condition. A fair crowd turned nu and although Warrington took the field, they had to retire to the pavilion when they had only com- piled 18 run. This was a great disappaintment Co the spectators who had expected to witness some good play.</p>
        <p>There is some talk of an attempt to extend the Co-operative boycott to Earlestown. A meeting of Earlestown tradesmen was held on Tuesday even- ing in the Town Hall, and a deputation attended from St Helens Although the meeting was really called to consider what soould be done in connec- tion with the Coronation holidays, thegreatest part of the time was taken up in listening to the harangues of the St Helens deputation, who came ostensibly to induce the Earlestown shop-keepers to initiate a crusade against the Co-operative I Society on the same lines as those adopted at St Helens. The matter was well discussed, and I although some few were in favour of the idea, it was impossible to obtain a unanimous opinion,and consequently no steps were taken.</p>
        <p>The Earlestown tradesmen were well advised to keep their hands clear of any action which might compromise them with the townspeople. The local Co-operative Society is much too strong a combina- tion for the tradesmen of Earlestown to tackle, and if they are wise they will refuse to interfere with it. No doubt such a fight as that which is taking place in St. Helens would be interesting, but with a combination of working men centralised as they are in Eirlestown, such a contest could not bs con- tinued three weeks without the tradeveople suffer- ing almost beyond recovery. The local Society is ' not by any means antagonistic nor pugnacious, and my own opinion is they would hail with delight a contest such as I have described. The Society is strong and well combined,whereas the tradespeople lack even the elements of combination or unanimity,</p>
        <p>There have lately been a few cases of scarlet fever in-Earlestown,and one of a particularly malig- nant character proved fatal. I am not at all sur- prised at fevers larking in our midst, when one considers the evil smells which proceed from the drains, and to which I have already drawn atten tion. Sanitary matters are not looked after as rigidly as :;hey ought to be, and it is absurd to sup- pose that one sanitary inspector can efficiently work the whole of such an extensive district as that controlled by the Newton Council. There will certainly be a rude awakening some day.</p>
        <p>Warrington Ragged School and Mission. The annual distribution of prizes to the children ttending the Sunday school took place on Wednes- day evening. Mrs John Crosfield distributed the prizes (about 150) and that lady was accompanied on the platform by Mrs Henry Roberts, Miss Rim- mer, Miss C Rimmer, and Miss G Crosfleld. Mr Skelland addressed the children and Mrs Roberts also spoke a few sympathetic words to them. Mrs Willis, Mrs John Yonge, and Mrs Robert Garnett sent letters, regretting that absence from home pre- vented them from being present, and expressed sympathy with the work of the mission.</p>
        <p>TREY WEAR WELL is what all customers say about AL, TAYLOR'S good boots for everybody; 45, Horse- market-street, iVarrintou.—Adet.</p>
      </div>
