      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>PURIOTIS MOTORING.—A DRIVER FINED AT D AMESBURY. At Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday Lefore Messrs W Lona. (presiding), H Nield, and Jos. Knowles. Herbert Ratcliffe, of Kendal. was charged with furiously driving a motor car along the Knutsfordwold, Grappenhall.—P C Williams stated that on July 18th, defendant, who was accompanied by his father. drove a motor ear along the Knutsford-road at a speed of at least twenty miles an hour. Witness could scarcely see the car for dust. Near Bradshaw- lane end witness saw something fall from the car and called to he defendant, who stopned about 2Ge yards down the road. Defendant said the pump wheel had fle wn off. The speed the ear was travelling at was most dangerous. Defen- dant was going In the direction of Knutsford. The same afternoon witness received two complaints about furious moles' car driving on the same rued.— Defendant said his pump wheel flew off. He could not possibly have attained the speed mentioned, because the batteries were almost run down. The consequence was the engine missed fire. He had his ten-mile-an-hour speed on, but was not going more than nine miles an hour when the constable saw him. If he had put a bigger speed en, it would have stopped the engine entirely. He came from near Preston on the date in questl,n.—Mr Greenall (magistrates' clerk) said defendant must have ridden over fifty miles, and on his own showing he did it in three hours.—Defniciant maintained that be conld only lanve driven the ear at ten miles an hour —Mr Greenall That won't do. I know a bit about motor cars. (Laughter).—Ths Chairman said the offence was a most dangerous one, and if motorists thonght no one bad a right to the road except themselves they would have to be taught differently. They, however. would deal leniently with defendant as that was his first appear- ance. He would be fined be and cost (13s fld in all).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>MINOR OASES. --- Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES. --- Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>PRlDAY..—Befare .the Deputy-Sisyor n te chair).. Coma-</p>
        <p>eillore Pierpnint and Smethurst. and Mr Thornton. THEFT OF RIEEDN FROM A GRATE.—Annie Lee, 5. Smith- street, was summoned for stealing% number of pieees of ,is ribbon value 2s Gai from a grave in; he Cemetery on the 18th inst.—Mr Stanley Wilson, deputy Town Clerk. prosecuted and said the defendant was seen it the Cemetery on the afternoon of the date named. Her movements being sus- picious the Registrar gave instructions to the ga-denars to keep on eye on her. At4,n Emmanuel Ratcliff a saw her in the act of stealing the ribbons from a grave. When defendant aaw him approaching she made off. He overtook her and charged her with the offence and she admitted it. The defendant was of rather weak intellect, and the Committee did not wish to press the case unduly. They brought it More as a warning to others Since the last prolecntion, about eighteen months ago. complaints of these miserable petty thefts had not been anything like so frequent.—The Bench dacidedlto record a conviction against the defendant and bind her over to come up for judgment when called upon DOG FIGHTS AND THEIR SEQUELS. —Rotert Watson, 67 Wellington-street. was summoned for ill-treating a dog on June 27th.—Evidence was given to the effect that defend- ant's dog commenced to fight with a dog belonging to a man namedlDitchfield nDefendant got hold of Ditchfteld's dogaand began to "choke it off." Ditchfield told him to loose the dog and defendant thereupon seized Ditchfield by the throat and pinned him against the wall. Ditchtield got away and went for some water and when he returned he found the dog lying exhausted and bleeding. A witness stated that defendant Inflicted the injury in separating the dogs.—Defendant was lined in the costs (155).—George Foster, 29 Wellington-street, was summoned for ill-treating two dogs by inciting them to tight.—A Mrs Bond stated that she waS going down Lord Belson-street with her dog when defendant came up with his dog. He exclaimed " NOW than, at him I "and the dogs commenced fighting. Both dogs were severely injured.— Defendant, who was represented by Mr Browne, called evidence to the effect that Mrs Bond incited her dog to light, —The case was dismissed. VIOLENT ASSAULT IN WAREFIELD-STREET.—Thomas Fox, 11. Howard-street, was summoned for assaulting William Looker. 166. Wellfield-street on July 12th.—Mr Unsworth appeared for the complainant and Mr Browne represented the defendant,—Mr Uneworth said the case was rather serious, as the assault appeared to have been premeditated. Complainant was an insurance agent and defendant was a platelayer. On the date in question complainant was walk- ing down Wakefield-street with his heads in his pockets When complainant reached the bottom of the street he sud- denly received a blow in the eye, and could not for the moment imagine what bad happened. The force of the blow eansed him to fall down on one knee and on looking up he saw the defendant, who butted him in the mouth with .r his head. Defendant also hurt him in the ribs. Complainant eventually got hold of defendant and kept him at a-m's length until some neigh- bours separated them.—Complainant's collar was tern from hie neak by the defendant, and his coat and waistcoat were also damaged.—Complainant and two witnesses gave evid- ence corroborating this statement. It was stated that there w s a wall at the corner of the street and defendant crouched behind it until complainant came up, and then committed the assault.—APer hearing the evidence Mr Browne said he would advise defendant to say hews, sorry for what he had done. Defendant's version of the affair was that complainant had been slandering him. and on the occasion in question he (defendant) asked him what he had been saying about him. and complainant lifted up his stick to strike him. He (defendant) then struck complainant. In face of the evid- ence adduced for the the corn plainant,he (Mr Browne) would not call his witnesses.—Complainant denied that any con- versation took place or that he had slandered defendant.— Defenant was fined 20s and costs, and an advocate's fee was allowed (23 Os lid in all.) PERSISTENT CRUELTY TO • WlFE.—Alfred Stan- way, 65, Buttermarket-street, was summoned for persistent cruelty to his Wife, Elizabeth Stanway, 48. Oxford-street.— Mr Steel represented the complainant and Mr Browne defen- ded.—Mr Steel said defendant was a man of ungovernable temper, and complainant had had to put up with a great deal of violence from him. At various times she had had to leave him. Various acts of cruelty were alleged. Defendant denied the allegations. After a long hearing the Bench granted a separation order with lfa per week alimony. WIFE DESERTION —Samuel Billion. 21, Scott-street. was ummoned for deserting his wife, Elizabeth Ellison, 24, 8 incoln Street.—A separation order with to per week alimony as granted.</p>
        <p>MONDlY.—Berore Mr F Monks (In the chair), Councillor Smethurst, Messrs Stroh%ll, Hayward, Wright and</p>
        <p>Thorsitc;n.</p>
        <p>A VERY GREAT IMPROP-iII—E;7:—IrI the adjourned case of George Peplow and Elizabeth Peplow, Tanners'-lane, who were before the Court a fortnight ago for neglentlng their children, Mr Browne, on behalf of the NSPC C, stated that there had been a very great improvement since the adjournment. He asked for theease to be adjourned for a further period of a month. The magistrates acceded to the</p>
        <p>request. Tag WHITECROSS COMPANY FINFD.—The Whitecross Com- pany, Ltd., were summoned for having on July 6th employed three boys under eighteen years of age, contrary to the Factory Act—Mr Browne represented the Company and pleaded guilty.—Major Roe, Chief Inspector of Factories for the district, stated that the boys were engaged on the Sunday in question in winding wire on bobbins.— For the defence, Mr Browne said the boys were sof paid by the Arm directly, but by sub-contrac- tors, but, of source. the nrm were responsible if anything wrong occurred. The date in question was the week after Coronation week, and the workmen were anxious to make up for lost time, as there were a great many orders to be executed. The foreman in the employ of the firm gave per- mission for the men to work but not for the boys to be em- ployed. The boys worked unknown to the Arm and were found by the Inspector. The tirm had no wish Whatever to do anything contrary to the Act—The magistrates were of the opinion that the offence had been committed with the consent of the foreman, and this committed the firm. A fine of 20s and costs in each case Would be Imposed. Tatiresnay.—Before Mr John Fairclough (In the chair Alderman gutton, Messrs Charles White, and J Rymer 111Youn g. THEFT OF LEAD,—James 11111, Queen-street, was charged with stealing from the •' Royal Court "hotel, Itylands-street sometime between Monday morning and Wednesday evening one cwt of lead value 12e, the property of B W Collin,builder The Chief Constable said air Collin was engaged, in doieg some alterations to the "Royal Court" hotel. On Wednesday night, shortly before 11 o'c ock, P C Lewis was on duty in I Winwick-street and saw the prinoner wheeling a pony cart containing a bag which contained something heavy. The conatable stoppf (i him and asked what the bag contained</p>
        <p>Prisoner told him that it belonged to him (prisoner.) Lewis was not satisfied and examined the bag and found it con- tained one cwt of lead which had evidentlybeen taken from the roof of some building. Prisoner was brought to the Police Station and on the way admitted that he was a labourer employed on the alterations to the hotel, and bad stolen the lead from the roof. A man named Lee. a hawker, of 15. Bowes-street, was walking in front of the prisoner when P.C. Lewis came upon the scene. The cart belonged to lee, and, although he said he knew nothing whatever about the theft, he admitted thatihe had agreed to buy the lead fro. the prisoner for Sc and had lent his cart for the purpose of having it transp Tted to his premises.— Lee was called before the magistrates, and stated that the prisoner came to his house, and asked him if he would buy some lead. Witness asked him if it was his own and the prisoner replied "Yes." Witness then went to prisoaer's house for the lead and they were going back with it in the cart when P C Lewis stopped them. Prisoner was sent to gaol for 14 day with hard labour.—The Chair non said that in his opinion Lee ought to be in the dock as well. If there were no reaeirers of this kind of goods there would be no thieves</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Daresbury Petty Sessions</head>
        <p>Daresbury Petty Sessions</p>
        <p>TuEsni.Y.-7Belore Messrs William Long (presiding),</p>
        <p>Henry Nleld and Joseph Knowles.— WARRIYGTON MEN FINED FOB 111,vanaTme Homan. Thomas Gavin, 7. Hill-street, Warrington. was summoned for cruelty to a horse by working it whilst in aa unfit state at Seven Oaks, on June 21st, and George Buckley,Knntsford- road. Warrington. was summoned for causing the same to be worked, P.C. Gilpin said he way on ditty in Warrington- road and saw defendant fir ving a pair-horse lorry. One of the horses seemed to be lame and apparently in great pain. He examined it and found a large sore near the collar about the size of a halfpenny niece, which was discharging. It was swollen and covered with blood, He ordered the man to take the horse out and not to work it. Another officer deposal to also seeing a small sore on the shin and to warning the defendant about another horse. Defendant Buckley said he did not know the horse was sore when it was sent ont.lf the man had reported it to him he could have taken another horse in its place. The Chairman said as it was the first offence the owner would be fined We and the carter 56.—John Musket% and Thomas Muster. 19, Tunnel- road. Barnton. were charged with a similar offence at Grappenhall on July Ist. P.O Williams said The horse was lame and practically walked on three legs. Its foot was hot. It was the off fore leg which was injured. The father of the defendants said the horse was fit for work when it went out at two o'clock. He had put the horse out to grass. A fine of 31 Siwas inflicted. STuITIr THROWING.—James Walker, George Grine and William Singleton. of Stockton Heath, and George Jamee,of Wilderspool. were summoned for throwing stones to the danger of passengers at Lumh Brook on July 6th. -P.O. Tart said the boys, with several others, were throwing stones at an advertisement plate fine) to the bridge. Several persons were passing at the time and had narrow escapes of being struck.—Fined 1. each.</p>
        <p>LADY FINED FOR FURIOUS RLDINO.—A young lady named Mabel Sewall, residing at the Mulberry Tree Inn, Stoskton Heath, was summoned for furiously riding a horse in London-road. on July 4th—PC Tart said losi was standing in the doorway of his house at about 8.45 p.m. on the day in question, and saw the defendant riding a pony at a fnri- ous speed. He called upon her to atop, bus she appeared to take very little notice. The animal went at quite a fast pace for ahead 250 yards.—Miss Sewall said tbe horse had not been taken out for about three days and was fresh. When it was being hrourht out a boy smacked it on the legs which esnsed it to go fast, and she could not stop it quick enough The pony was kert for pleasure and needed more exercise.— A fine of Is was inflicted. A SUSPICIOUS PERSON.—Henry Cafferty, of no settled abode was charged with frequenting at Daresbary on Jilly 12th with intent to commit a felony. —lt was stated that prisoner was seen to twice enter a tent on the field in which the parish fete was being held, and to handle some clothes. He was taken into custody.—He had been before the magis- trates at ether towns under different names, and on this occasion was sent to gaol for three months. MrscELLasrEods CASES —The following were fined for not having lighted lamps attached to their vehicle. or hicyeles : Alfred Smart. Tarporley ; Peter Mitchell. Edward-street, Warrington; William Roughsedge. Stockton Heath ; Joseph Paylor, Crowley; William Wilkinson. Keokwick ; Richard Willings, Mersey-street, Warrington ; and John Haspull Barnton. Thomas King was fined 2s fid for drunkenness, and David Cassidy was mulcted in a similar manner for using bad language.—Henry Gamble. Warrington, was stunmonee for allowing his dog to be at large without a name upon the collar Defen- dant, who said he only had the dog given to him the day it got lest, was discharged —William Wlch, Stockton Heath, was fined is for allowing his chimney to be on fire. It was stated that the proceedings were taken at the instance of the Runcorn Rural Council as a wfrniag to others. THOUGHT HE LOOKED BIG.—A boy named Frederick Sayers, Wilderspool-road, was charged with making use of obscene language.—Defendant said he was woraing among men, and bethought it made him "look big" to swear.—On promising not to offend again he was fined 2s 6d- Tgatiena SHY AUTIfORITY.—Mr Alfred Cooke was granted temporary authority to sell at the Old London Bridge Inn, in place' of his deceased mother.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Meters F 3 .Norman (in the chair). and )3</p>
        <p>Im!son.</p>
        <p>TIFEPTS or Coat.—Two women named Mary Hushon and Mary Wood, with two girls named Mary Jane Gregory. aged 12, and Julia Hushon, aged 11, were charged with stealing and receiving 51 pound• of coal on the 23r4 July belonging to the Ship Canal Compatsv.—Mr Preston, Manchester. prosecutel.—P 0 Davies deposed that on the date mentioned he saw the fonr defendants coming from the direction of the Old Quay Works. Hushon was carrying s bag and Woods had also a bundle He followed them and found on a piece of waste ground (be bag produced half full of coil. From inquiries he agfprehended the two girls and in consequence of what was told him by the girls he apprehended the women. He afterwards found that the bag belonged to the woman , Hnshon —The women pleaded not guilty and the children gulltr—The former were flaed 105, or in default 7 days, and , the children were treated under the First Off enders' Act and bound over. _ _</p>
        <p>ALLEGED SAVAGE ASS•IILT BY A CONSTABLE —Henry Moore, Egerton-street, was summoned at the instance of Job Brown for an assault alleged to have been committed on the 21st June.—Mr Knowles appeared for the almplalnant and Mr Lake for the defendant.- The evidence for the complain- ant showed that on the date mentioned the complainant with his sweetheart was sitting on the wall faaing the Ship Canal when it was noticed that the defendant was watching them from the embankment, Brown want up the embankment to see the reason why he was being watahed when Moore knocked him down the enatankrnent, without the slightest provocation. Defendant also klekedithe complainant on the heal and again struck him. Complainant then kioked de- feneant on being attacked again. Defendant then left him but, afterwards followed him and kicked him from behind. It was also alleged that the defendant used bad language toward Brown and threatened to throw him into the can al. Moore eventualy took the complainant before Mr Meadowcroft, dis- trict manager of the Ship Canal Company, who took his name and address. Re could flat do Hs work in consequence of the assault.—Mary Williamson corroborated the complain- ant's evidence.—The evidence for the defendant, a sworn in constable of the Manchester Ship Canal Company, showed that the defendant heard sounds from the lower portion of the embankment and went to see what was the matter. The complainant at once rushed up to the top and struck defend- ant afterwards kicking him violently in the stomach A struggle ensued and the only blows struck hr the defendant were in self defence.—Moore had been " off " work over a month in consequence,—Mr John Mearlowcroft, district manager of the Ship Canal Company. corroborated a portion of the defendant's evidentse.—The ease was dismissed.</p>
        <p>- VIBLEItPING OUT.—Herbert If ibbit was charged with sleep- ing in an outhouse without visible means of subsistence on Sunday night —P 0. Bowyer deposed that ho found the prisoner fast asleep and he refused to go out. There were numerous complaints about people sleeping out in the neigh- bourhood —Prisoner was sane to Knutsford for seven days.</p>
        <p>OBSCP:NE LANGUAGE.—Aaron Ankara. Cooper-street, was fined les or in default? days for using obscene language.— Joseph Bushell was fined 10s for the same offence. NEIGHBoITRLY SQUAlBLE.B.—Sarahlckson was summoned by Mary Barnes tor an alleg d assault at Weston—lt app.ared from the evidence that both women were neighbours and had quarrelled over their children,which resulted eventu- ally In blows being struck by both parties.—The summons was dismissed.</p>
        <p>BEGGING.—Thoman O'Rield was sent to Knutsford for 7 days for the above offence.—P2o. Rowlands proved the case. DerrsK.—Sfichael Coffey. on the evidence of Superinten- dent Bnnloi. was fine I 135 or 7 days in default for being drunk on the Sunday previous.</p>
      </div>
