      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>EDUCATION DIFFICULTY.</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION DIFFICULTY.</p>
        <p>Rev F E Powell's Views.</p>
        <p>" The bother about education " is the subject of a short article by the Rev F E Powell, in this moot-Oa issue of St James's, Latchford, parish magazine. In it the rev gentleman eay3 —Soma at our readers may be glad to know a few of the arouments Which are used by th 3 fricrods of definito reliei rus teaching in all altate-supported schools. 'these autnber about 21,000 altogt ther. They shoold ramember that these echoola are divided into two great classes—so called Voluntary schools and (since 1870) Board Schools. They should remember, tan, that while there ar3 only 4,000 Baud Schoo's, there are 16,991 Vsluntary Schools. These voluntary schools have not been built by the State as the Board Schools have been, but by private subscription and enterprise. They supply precisely the same education as the Board Schools—in some cases the equipment is better, in other; oases worse. But the secular educa- tion, which ought to be niade as good as possible in all schools, has to satisfy the Government Board of Educa- tion, else no public money would be forthcoming to pay for it. The chief difficulty at present before the country is that of the control and maneoement of each particular school. The 4000 Board Schools are managed , by popularly elected bodies for the purpose. The 14,409 Church 8 rhords are managed by Church managers. the 1045 Roman Catholic Schools by Roman Catholics, the 458 Wesleyan Schools by Wesleyans, and 1079 British and other Non-Church Schools by Congregationalists, Baptists and so forth as the cue may be. Each board of management appoints the teachers and decides the question of religious teething in the schools, Some people say, let us nat ke the School Board system univt real. But to do this would mean the expenditure of 28 millions of money at the least in order ti buy up or replace the schools which private enterprise has erected. Besides, people in very great numbers prefer these Voluntary Schools, and they are sufficiently strong to prevent their being done away with. We must always remember that they were first in the field hp a long way, Board Schools only dating from 1870 when they were intro- duced to eupplemeet and not ro supersede the others Religious teaching in Beard Schools can be distinctive of no particular body of Caristlane. It is what is known as Undenominatienal. But thia favours those who call tlaemselyrs with pride unsectarian. They get their religion given to their children without any trouble,paid rut of rates and taxes. The Jews are also favoured in this way. We should not complain of this. But if on. sectarians and Jews may have their religion taught in , State schools why may not Churchmen, Wesleyans, Roman Catholics Sze, have their relieion taught in their own schools, and in Board Schools as well to the s.; children of these who desire it? Non- cede/mists have a distinct grievance in that there are about 8,000 parishes chiefly in oountry districts where the only religious teaching they can have for their children is that provided by the Church. But Church- men have also a distinct grievance in the 4,000 Board schools where the only religious teaching they can have for their children is unsectarianism. Of course tho con- science clause which protects a child from receiving such religious teaching, exists in both cases. But if religious teaching be given at all, a parent should be able not only to protect his child from religious instruction .of which he disapproves, but also to roeure for his child the instruction which he desire. The present system is sturoirl, iregical and tmjust, 'rhe Education Bill now before Parliament--M this particular grievance—goes a little way, hut only very little way toward removing it. What we desire is that (1) that religious teaching should be given in accordance with the creed of the parents concerned ; (2) that this religious teaching should he given by those teachers who believe it themselves ; (3) that no alteration be made in the management of these schools which would jqopardlie these two principles ; (4) but that compatible with this provision, the control should be made as popular and as general as possible.</p>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Society. Local devotees of the camera will be interested to learn that the Warrineon Photographic Society has been resuscitated.A meeting of these interested in the objects of the society was held in the Druids' Hall on Tuesday evening, the Rev T Bishop presiding over an encouraging attendance. Mr W J Twiss (secretary pro tern) said the object of the meeting was to revive interest in the old society and to enrol new members. They wished to stimu- late the interest and energies of amateur photographers in Warrington, and he thought there was plenty of material in the town 'to carry on a flourishing society. The Warrington Society had twO rooms (a lecture room and a dark room) at the Old Academy which they could place at the disposal of the Society, and asked for an offer of rent for the use of them. The average membership in past years had been about forty, but members tell off owing to the Society having to change quarters so frequently. Mr Miller said numerous amateur photographers had expressed their willingness to join a Society.—lt was decided that the annual subscription should be 5s per year, and that .E 7 per year be offered for the use of the rooms at the Old Academy.—The following officials were elected : Mr Roger Parr president (subject to his accept- ance); Messrs J Fairhurst, E J Dail, and E V Walker and Dr Smith, vice-presidents ; Mr Kirk- land, treasurer; Messrs W J Twiss and B F Bishop, secretaries ; Mr P Dalton, auditor; Mr Armstrong lanternist; committee, Messrs W Winstanley, H Miller, J Harding, D S Birrell, G Clark and W PAmore. The rules of the old society were adopted with one or two alterations.—The chairman was Iheartily thanked for presiding, and in returning thanks wished the Society every success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>AUGUST 1 1902</head>
        <p>AUGUST 1 1902</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Ellesmere-street Wesleyan Methodist Sunday School.</p>
        <p>The anniversary services were htld in the Misslon Hall on t+unday and Monday. Mr Mvsee Welsby, from the "Joyful News" Mission, Reehdale, gave three niE- courses on Sunday, and a splendid ad•-iress on Monday evening entitled "An hour with some good old preachers and others." F r rather over an hour he kept his audi- ence in wrapt attention. He told several humorous incidents of his experiences with various people. The attendances at all the services were very gratifyiog, more especially on Sunday evening when the hall was so crowded that the vestry behind was filled, and the entrance door completely blo.clrel. The anthem on Sunday morning was " Peale, the Lord." In the after- neon " Laurel Bank" was given, and in the evening " Praise ye the Lord." O.? Mond ty evening the choir gave" Count your blessinge." The choir was led on Sunday by Mr Plumpton, and on Monday evening by Mr Llewellyn. Mr Andersen officiated at the harmonium and Miss Patty Taylor accompanied the children in their hymns on the piano. During the Sunday evening ser- vice, Mr Levi Richardeen sang with much feeliue a 8311 entitled " Lavinz all to Jeeue," b inz accompleied on the piano by Miss Anitie Taylor. Th - ccelectione and donetiene for the day did not quite reach the amount raised last year, but the anniversary W3O very success- ful. With donations expected during the week the amount may possibly exceed last year's sum. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Chapel.</head>
        <p>Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Chapel.</p>
        <p>Visit of Mr. R Cameron, M.P.</p>
        <p>Sankey Bridges Wesleyans were. on Sunday last, favoured with the servic-s of Mr R Cameron, M.P. for the annual choir sermons. Mr Cameron's reputation as a preacher and lecturer was sufficient to attract large congregations, and the servic.s passed off with complete success. At the morning service Mr Cameron based his observations on the words " Now faith is the substance of things hoped for, ths evidence of things not seen," and preaclaed a powerful sermon. In the afternoon the choir rendered a service of song entitled " The two harvests" in a very creditable manner. Mr J Woods presided, and Mrs Dodds read the connective readings. Mr E G Rickeis presided at the organ and Mr D R Dunn conducted. At the even- ing set vice Mr Cameron took for his text the words " Except a corn of wheat fall into the ground and die, it abiCletb alone ; but if it die it brinv.tb forth hitvh fruit." The choir rendered the anthem "What is man?" and Mr S Flossedl contributed a solo. The collections for the day amountsd to V re 31. LECTURE ON "THOUS CARLYLE." On Monday evening Mr Cameron &amp;slivered his popular lecture on " The life of Thomas Carlyle." There was a large attendance,Mr R 11 Poscstt,J.P.,of Runcorn, presiding.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said it was a great joy to him to be called to corns cad be of any service to them that even- eg and he trueted that th It meeting might be for their mutual joy and lasting benefit. He liked whenever he go e the oppertuaity ie a Methodist chapel to say that at any rate he was proud to be a Methodist. (A pelause.)Ele believed ia the real all type of Methodist, weich they were keine to-day. He believed that in every service they held they should expect and have faith in God to give them fruit in those services- The one thing, he believed, which had held Methodism back from becoming the greatest factor in the evangelisation of this country was the fact that they themeelvee, as they went to their services Sunday by Sunday, did not look to God for the presence of the Holy Spirit to mani- fest itself in every service. lentil they got back to that expectancy of their Methodist fathers, who cz- peeted that God would be with them ia every service and would manifest is presence :they would not have anything like the glories which their fathers saw in their ministry. He expressed the hope that the zeal aud en- thasiesm of his hearers would be kindled and that they would attempt greater thiogs for God than they had done in the past. (Applause.) Mr. CAMERON, ALP., who had a very cordial recep- tion, referred, at the outset, to the hostility with which 1 Carlyle's works were at first received, and then to the gradual hushing of the storm and the change of opinion. Thoughtful men began to study Carlyle and to write about him, and artists began to paint pictures with a new spirit, and new ideals. If he had done nothing but convert John Ruskin and inspire him for his great work, Carlyle would have done a great deal. Ruskin was, perhaps, the truest disciple that Carlyle ever had. Continuing, the lecturer asked his hearers to think of Carlyle as a man truly wise and truly great, in spite of the lopposition kindled against him, which he bore patiently and heroically. To-day, Parliament was ' different because Thomas Carlyle taught; the pulpit was different because he taught ; art was different because be taught, and social changes were largely due to the impetus which he gave to social reforms. All England, he thought, was better because Carlyle had writteu and lived. He (the speaker) thuught it was a I pity when he nest a young man who hod not read Carlyle. They could never have a graep of the problem of life, nor the inspiration to duty end sacrifice,to bear- ing oppesition,and being etrong,real, and without sham, until they had received the baptism which the books of Carlyle gave to them. The lecturer then went on to review the life of Carlyle, and referred at length to his home life, and his remarkable progress as a student ae, school and college, which ended in his seouring first elaes both in mathematics and classics. Carlyle's study and translation of German and French writers was touched upon, and the lecturer referred in ,detail to several of these works. Speaking of Carlyle's work on Oliver Cromwell, Mr Cameron said it crushed the lies which for over two hundred years bad gathered round Cromwell's name. In other works, Carlyle taught what the Poor Laws ought to be. Sines then, the Poor Laws had been mended and mended in the direc- tion he had pointed out. Be told us that we ought to have Free Libraries. Free libraries were now established everywhere. Be told us that national education was highly necessary if the people were not to sink in ignor- ance and brutality, and nereape bring about another revolution like the French revolution. We had now got national eveducation, He wrote the " History of the French Revolution"for one purpose,and thalweg:tie a warning to all rulers that if they sowed the wind they would reap the whirl wind. If they neglected the peer and overtaxed them more than they could bear, and the poor were hungry and ignorant what could they expect ? They might crush a man not be was a man still, and he would boar so much but at last he would turn upon them and rend them. That was Carlyle' philosophy of the French Revolution. In conclusion, the lecturer said 'Oat Carlyle was the most earnest man of the last century— &amp; man who hated shsms and lies, who wanted truth in history, and truth, siecerity, an I htunality in oar politics. lie wanted us to be real men, not lovers of pleasure, but men who feared God. (Applause.) The Rev. J. F. MATTHEWS proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman and lenturer. He hoped that as a ' result of the lecture they would all go borne deterrniued to fight znalously, nobly, heroically and continuously against ell sorts of shams in ecclesiastical, municipal, Becht' and political life, and lastly but not; least. ais Free Chnrchmen to fight to the death against the infamous Education Rill now before Parliament. Mr. W. limes seconded, and also tendered sincere thanks to Mr and Mrs Decide for their valuable assist- ance in cenneetion with the 'services.</p>
        <p>The vote was heartily aereed to. In responding, Mr Cameron paid a high compliment to the choir for their singing at the previous day's ser- vices. He expressed the hope that the lecture would stimulato young men and women to obtain the books he had referred to and read them, and if they gave a year to it ani formed an appetite for the high truths contain- ed therein they would be different men and women im a short time. The Doxology having been snag, the meeting was brought to a close with the pronouncing of the Benediction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Council Meeting Postponed.</head>
        <p>Council Meeting Postponed.</p>
        <p>Owing to the Bank Holiday on 'Monday, the monthly meeting of the Town Council has been postponed until the following Tuesday,August 12th. Local Oddfellowship. The monthly meeting of the LoN al Perseverance Lodge of the Warringi;on District of the National Independent Order of Oddfellows was held at the Crown and Sceptre Hotel. Buttermarket street, on Saturday. P G George Hands occupied the N G's chair,Bro Jervase:Wilkinson,the:vice-chair,and were supported by PPGM Robert Sewell, PP GM John Tyson, and P G Edward Crozier. A vote of condolence was passed with the widow and family of the late Bro John Emson, woo it was stated, was at one time secretary for a short period, and took a gtear, interest in the lodge, prior to having the position of Prov C 8, in toe Independent Order of Oddfellows, MU. He was always a good worker, and woald be sadly missed by all the friendly societies in the town. Bro J Wilkinson was elected noble grand,and Bro A Cliffe vice-grand, for the ensuing half. year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Warrington P.S.A, Society.</head>
        <p>Warrington P.S.A, Society.</p>
        <p>An opn meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Sunday, and was largely attended. The ladies had an opportunity of attend- ing and seeing the work that is being carried on by the society. Quartermaster-Sergeant Roberts, who presided, spoke of the evil of gambling. Mrs Hannan sang " The Better Land " and " Just for to-day," for which he was accorded a hearty recep- tion, Mr Hannan gave 'A Dream of Heaven" in excellent style, and Miss Lucy Evans rendered " A Dream of Childhood," which was much appre- ciated. Mr Wadsworth was the accompanist. This was one of the most successful services that have been held in the hall, and it was very gratifying to the members to see their numbers increasing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE ~, YORKSHIRE.</p>
        <p>The return match in the above annual engagement at :icon Valley on Saturday proved unsucc2ssf at for the Lancashire side, although they entered the meeting with 44 shots in hand—gained at Stretfoid last month. The green of the Victoria Cub, over which the contest was waged, afforded less opportunity for effective scoring by the casual visitor than those hitherto selected by York- shire for the inter-county event, After the president and vice-president's game, with which the meeting opened, had been disposed or, Latioashire were three points to the fore,but the next return placed them to the rear, ard Yorkshire continued to ecore in advanc^ until their mlnity extended t 79 on the day's play. Closely contested games formed the exception rather than the rule, anc.: odd-that re:tilts never appeared on the scorc- sheet. It is the fourth match won by Yorkshire bver qie home turf, and the second which they have gained by a majority en the aggregate. Details :</p>
