      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>CYCLE PARADE AND</head>
        <p>THE CYCLE PARADE AND</p>
        <p>CARNIVAL.</p>
        <p>Further Arran wements.</p>
        <p>The arrangements for the fancy dress eycle parade and c %newel to be held on August 23rd in aid of the Infirmary and the Nursieg Association are progressing saeisfactorily. The weekly meeting of the general committee at the Patte-Arms Hotel on Thursday evening last weekwas well attended. Mr D Davies presided. flee hoe. secretary (Mr E Pickavance jun), real a letter from the Town Clerk in which permission seas grs.nted to the Club to use a room at the Town Hall for the giving out of the collecting boxes. The Town Clerk added that the boxes used last year were at the club's disposal. Mr Pickavance intimated that the Mayoress was leav- ing town, and therefore could not take the chair at the ladies' meeting to be held on the following Pueeday. Mrs Hoary Roberts, also, would be unable to take the chair, o wing to a prior engagement. She had written wishing them every success. It was resolved that Mrs James Fairclough should be waited upon and asked to preside at the ladies' meeting. The hon secretary else announced that Mr Davies and himself had waited upon the Mayer and Mayoress, who had promised the club their ucqualifid support. The Mayoress epprived of the scheme whereby an autograph photo of herself would be presented to every lady collector who, on the date of the car- nival, collected more than £1 before the parade came ea. This would be a suitable memento of the occasion. The Workingmen's Committee at a meeting on the night previous, had also promised all the assistance in their power. Messrs Armitage and Rigby, at that meeting, agreed to provide at least 50 girls as °enactors, but probably the number from Cockhedge Milts whose services in this connection they could obtain was nearer 100. A discussion took place as to the necessity of training the girls to collect efficaciously, it being stated that on the occasion of the last parade a large proportion of the crowd was not reached by the collectors. It was re- solved to try and secure the services, as col- lectors, of girls, from 16 years upwards, employed at the various works. Messrs D Davies, J C Robinson, H H Evans, J H J Hat:noon, E Pickavance and C Leigh were appointed a deputation to wait upon the ladies' committee at their meeting on Tuesday. It was resolved that Messrs Watt, of Mat- cheeter should be invited to send down fancy costutnes .as before. It was unanimously agreed that the parade should assemble in the Park at 4.30 and start at 5, and that the route should be the same as in 1900. Mr Picka- vance reported that the whole ef the details, so far as the concert was concerned, were virtually settled and the entertainment was sire to be high class and enjoyable. In connection with the concert a ticket competition, with $l, view to raising extra money, was suggested by Mr Mien, and be and Messrs Wells, Litton, J Sampson and J Taylor were appointed a sub- committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Meeting of Ladies.</head>
        <p>Meeting of Ladies.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening a meeting of the Ladies' Com- mittee formed in connection with the fancy dress cycle parade and carnival, promoted by the Warrington Cycling Club,lwas held in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall. Thrre was a large attendance. Messrs D Davies (chairman), E Piekavance (bon. secretary), G Robinson, H Evans and C Leigh atttrided as a deputa- tion from the General Committee. .</p>
        <p>Mr DAVIES observed that owing to an engagement in the North Miss Parr was unable to b 3, present, but Miss White hal kindly consented to preside. The object of that meting was to point out the duties and the work they expec'ed from the young ladies who had generously come forward that night in such numbers. They hoped sincerely that as the parade approached the numbers would double and treble, because they required at least 400 collectors. They wished it to be clearly understood that unless they per. sistently went round with boxes in the crowd the parade would not be a financial SUCCO93, but they did not wish the collectors to go in batches. They must go singly. Boxes could be (drained on the Friday evening before the parade at the Town Hall between six od eight o'cicck and must be returned t 3 the same place. It Was further explained that an autograph photo- I graph of tt.e Mayoress was to be given to each lady collector who collected £1 before the parade. This was independent of the prizes that were to be offered. The book scheme is to ensure the promoters against loss should the weather prove unfavourable. It was desired that all collectors if possible should be attired in fancy costumes, prizes for which would b 3 given. At the close Mr DAVIES heartily thanked the ladies for their attendance, and remarked that if the gentlemen worked as hard as they did the parade would be an entire success.</p>
        <p>Miss White was also thanked for presiding.</p>
        <p>A Choir Prip to the Isle of Man. The choir of St. Peter's, Oughtrington, and their friends, had their annual trip on Saturday, Douglas being visited. Lymm was left late on Friday night, and the party arrived at Douglas early on Saturday morning after a calm passage. A start back was made by the afterno3n boat. and Lymrn was reached in time to enable all to have a good rest before the Sunday services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>WARRINGTON CONT RACTOR SUED FOR DAMAGES.</head>
        <p>A WARRINGTON CONT RACTOR SUED FOR DAMAGES.</p>
        <p>Gasp at Blackpool.</p>
        <p>In the Nisi Nit's Court—b—ef—ore—Mr Justice Wills and a Special Jury.—On Tuesday the case of Addiva and company, Limited, and others was heard.—Tuie Wig au action in which the plaintiffs, wine, spirit, and tea mer- chants, Churah-street, Blackpool,rsou,ht to recover frord the National Telephone Company and their contractor, J A Ewart, damages for injury caused to a house be- longing to rho plaintiffs by alleged negligence in exeraVa- ting for lkying a telephone cable along Churck-street Blackpeol. The Telephone Company pleaded that tbeY. were not liable because they employed a contractor, and the contractor said he was not liable because he did the work with sufficient care. Mr Shee, K. C,,, and &amp;II Sutton were counsel for the plaintiffs; Mr Pickford,lLC. and Mr Compton Smith for the Telephone CornplaP and Mr Overond Evans for the contractor. Ur Store, in opening,stated that the plaittiff's prersiaaii were at the corner or Church-stree, and on Apr,l last, without giving any noLice, and before thi msnsger at the shop had arrived, the defendwits started laving,,„ cable under the footpath along the gable of the plaintin: premiseF. The excavations made along the wall well' down to about 21t 6in in depth, or below the foil:03E00 of the plaintiff's premises, which w•s 12in down, ae,,d,, resting on the solid clay. Not only did the defendaraP expose the whole length of the gable wall, but they fait no precautions whatever to prop up or protect the gable end. It wou'cl be contended the when tha deftr dants had gone dowa one foot they ought to to have stopped,because it was obvious that to the foundation was • very dangerous thing to do. About three e'elic in th 3 afternoon one of the plate glass windows cracked, and, when spoken to, the fora' Imam of the work and they had not done anythin% t° I cause this. The wo:k said proceeded with and complels4' It was fours 1, when the plaintiffs wanted to lock up Oa premises at night, that the door would not shut, and had to be barricaded. The window cracked again, a% aaothr r also orackei, while the gable wall had sunk a 0" was gradually drawing the whole building that ays34 The plaintiffs' idea was that the gable wall must Coall,,e down and be rebuilt,which, with decorations, ,fcc, wool° *oat from £4OO to £450 ; £5O was claimed for comPalY Wien for disturbance of lensiness, and £lOO for contia; gencies likely to arise in the reconstruction of an 01'</p>
        <p>Suilding.--The manager of the shop admitted, in et examination' that there was an old crack in the g?</p>
        <p>end, but it was in no way detrimental to it; b—u-ilaing' and it was impossible to see through into the bedroonig• —o'her witnesses were called in corroboration. el Mr Ovorend Evans then ea led vritnesscs on behalf ; the contractor. Their etatements generally were Or the Corporation men took up the flags where toed trenches were to be dug for the underground cablesfori„ that the contractor's men had nothing to do with ` direction of the trenohes. The contractor's forecill; stated that the instructions for cutting the trey:lobe' cern' given by a Telephone Company's employ&amp; Itt °eyeing the men never saw the base of the plaint gable wall. Between the trench and the wall six incbt . _</p>
        <p>were left, and there was nothing to show that they' bild gone below the fooings or foundations of the walL--k,1:1 arehitect and surveyor stated that a subsidence taken place in the building before the 18th April. Pw did not think the gable wall required to be taken doorti and he eonsidered that excessivo sums had been down for repairs. His estimate was £4O, while that s' another witness was £4l, for effecting the repairs sees: sary and allowing something for contingencies. Anotbeil witness, in crov-examination stated that there bs been a subsidence in the building acme time ago, cause it had been " cut and carved to pieces " iss..Tc; The jury found for the plaintiffs. awarding Lo"' damages. The contractor agreed to indemnify the TM' phone Company in damages and costs payable to t4t3: plaintiffs on condition that the Telephone Cmmany Ps' heir own coats.</p>
        <p>Death of Mr D. Spruce of DaresburY• ;(1 We regret to announce the death of Mr Day', Spruce, which took place at his residence at NI; bury on Thursday of last week, at the age of years. Deceased had been in failing health PS some time. Mr Spruce had been of a quiet 8,1/fai retiring disposition,and had gained the respect 0, who knewihim.The funeral took place on Saturd,l at the Parish Church. As a mark of respect blinds in the village were drawn while the corte,,6d passed. The mourners were Messrs T 5, VV 11. 81;d A W Roby (nephews), Messrs James,Elenry, Alfr and Thomas Ryder, (nephews), Masters, Charle,st Albert,and James Ryder jun., Dr Jones (Liverpo°l: Mr U Rogers,Mr I Rogere(Prescot),and Mr C JOet, The service was conducted by the Vicar, Rey;de Clifton Smith, and among those at the graoEt„ were Mr TS Burgess, Mr J Cooper, hr C W Pbij Mr T Ellison, and Mr A Booth. A large nuolbi3" ° floral tribute,' were placed over the grave. Lord Newton's Complaint against the Government. In the linage of Lords on Monday, Lord NewtOi called attention to an amendment to the LocolA Government Provisional Order Bill which, he sal; involved an important principle, and asked for s, explanation.—The Duke of Devonshire said ths.: the noble lord in charge of the Bill was not prese7, and he was afraid he could not give an eitPisli.o tion.—Lord Newton responded that it was __ 40</p>
        <p>extremely slovenly way Of conducting busines9 etre the part of the Government. Ile shou/d more,t4., adjournikent of the House.—The Earl of Wavo- grave explained that the noble lord in question Ws, ill and unable to ba present.—Lord Newton sugge,4 ted that some other member of the Governlo:o with more leisure at his disposal should have he`of deputed to explain the amendment.—The Dukeit. Devonshire said an explanation would he faryrd coming on Tuesday.—On this understanding D° Newton withdrew his motion.</p>
