      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>PARR'S BANK, LIMITED. The Chairman and Savings Bank Interest.</head>
        <p>PARR'S BANK, LIMITED. The Chairman and Savings Bank Interest.</p>
        <p>There was a representative attendance at the half- ' Yearl meeting of Parr's Bank,Limited, held at the bank' premises, Winwick-st reet,Warrington, at noon Oft , Thursday last week. Mr Cecil F Parr(chairman of . the company), presided, and there were also pre- terit Mr Alfred Crewdson, Mr Thomas H Dixon, Mr danhu Dun, Mr N ffarrington Eckersley, Sir John J tlarwood, Mr Alfred Hewlett, Mr Robert Neill, the 4011 Arthur Stanley,M P, Mr Peter Stubs, Mr Alfred torrison Turner, Mr Joel Wainwright.Mr Alexander esines, Messrs J Charltan Parr, John Coy, Joseph b 4 ' tickett, Wallace Broadbent, Peter Hayes, William "rrock C Baker, E C Cooper, Rowland Y f, °Ling, Cuarles White, William Flint, Francis ""Pees, William Atherton, Robert Wright, John Pane, A G Gray, William Owen, Dr Bowers, Alfred ;3,11AV. with R W Whalley (general manager), and " nyatt (secretary), SPEECH BY THE CHAIRMAN. Mr CECIL F PARR in moving the adoption of the e- r , port and balance sheet, which appeared in the : ;]xaminer " last week, said once more the direc- tors bad to report that the position and prospects ,?f the business continued to be in every way satis- Metory. (dear, hear) The amalgamation of Pares's Leicestershire Banking Company had been suc- cessfully carried out, He could assure them that full investigation had convinced them of the thoroughly good character of the business. (Ap- plause.) As an old established county institution, With an inception much like Parr's Bank, beginning as it did as a private bank before the commence- neat uf the last century—Pares's Bank had always held a position second to none in standing and res- 12ectability in its own district. Its name had been gs closely acsociated for many years with the names Ot til Pares and lieygate, as Parr's was with s names of Lyon, Greenall or Parr. 1 It seemed 3 natural fate that two banks so nearly . Identical in name should finally be joined in one. ; tl.B far as the first three letters went there was no nge. (Laughter.) He considered therefore,that they were entitled as of right at the outset to three . !Moths of the confidence of their customers and , their shareholders; and he hoped and believed that t fl;BY would very shortly earn the remaining fourth. ..."IPplause.) The death of the late Chairman, Mr Ile b Nate, was a very severe loss, not only to the 114111 E with which his family had been so long con- . ected but to both the town and county of Leices- ilershire as well. As chairman, Mr Ileygate would been the director selected to join the board of directorse. The caoiee had now fallen on his suc- kenoa in the chair Mr Hussey Packe, also a well :own public man throughout Leicestershire. He nas chairman both of the County Council and of iliarter Sessions, and was altogether most worthily Of Its to represent the united bank in that part ._ Its sphere of operations. The shareholders were rked that day to confirm his appointment. Equally Illaortant for the future wellbeing of the incor- t°rated bank, was the fact that the other directors eftcl all consented to retain their office, and would Lootintie to give their services in the 11duct of its affairs. It was the day- b -day supervision that kept a banking 7,tlksiness in a strong and healthy condition. pveY had, therefore, every reason to hope that .. a.or'e Bank,under its scarcely changed name would ntain the high character which it had cora- Inded in the past. (Applause.) They had Zatltly to deplore the loss of their late colleague .a r Ross. His sterling good sense and life-long fMking experience gave great value to his counsel . at their board. Mr Ross was also a director of the 'entandard Bank of South Africa, a bank which for • ''na' years had been connected with their own by Intimate financial relations, It was, therefore, r liatural and fitting that they should seek for his ' successor at the same board, and they had found that successor in the person of the Hon Sir Charles Win. Fremantle, 1i,c.8. Sir Charles was a man of great and varied experience. He was a Suez Canal ' director, and for many years he worked as a public servant as Deputy Master of the Mint and in other eaDacities under Government. The third name hich was presented for confirmation needed no latroduction or recommendation to a Lancashire alldience. All their shareholders in the North would !f4ree that the bank had been fortunate in securing the services of the Hon Arthur Stanley, mem- nr for the Ormskirk Division of Lancashire. i'.ltaself a Lancashire lad. (Applause.) At the 11ue when, last January in London, he (the Chair- bean) was paying a very inadequate tribute to Mr . Ws exceptional services to the bank on his tetirement from the post of general manager, he 11111 list say he could not help feeling that it would 4 4VO been in a sense more fitting if he could have hrtici what he did say here—here in the town where Ilfirst began to work for the bank. His words, Itititurally, would have found a still deeper echo in be hearts of all his old Lancashire friends, amongst lltnom he lived and worked for 27 years, namely . Ona 1865 to 1892. (Applause). He did not pro- ," ijca)e to repeat his words that day, hut would ask ' v en 3 to join with him in wishing Mr Dun many iv!ars of life and health, both for his own sake and it'llt of the bank for which he had done so much, (nAcl of which be still remained a director. "IPPIa ) He hoped and believed some future 0, , use • e"alrrnan might at some far distant date pay an i tlially cordial and equally well-deserved tribute aft) Mr Dun's successor, Mr Whalley. (Hear, hear.) Whalley would find Mr Dun both an easy man Sip e a difficult man to follow. He stepped into the Zc'rninand of a bank in a sound condition, conducted good staff under a well-tried system, a system ti,ltti which be himself was entirely familiar. On "6 other hand, he would find it hard to emulate the i tact, the patience, and the discrimination which fbtt., Mr Dun conduced so much to the well- fealliß of tbe institution. (Applause). He i4ue Tcbairman) however, had no doubt that Mr e halley would prove fully equal to the task. The i!Rures on the balance sheet were swelled all round Mthe inclusion of the figures of Pares's Bank, !:4eicestersbire, but the proportions of those in- creased figures were very much the same. He had !,old them more than once, and he was not afraid to tell them again, that the proportion of cash and Short money, and cash at the Bank of England was very nearly one-third of the total of their liabilities to the publicom that they were in a pleasant position By the addition to the capital of 12,250 shares the capital subscribed was raised to .E8,542,500,and the Paid up capital to •c 1,708,500 the reserve fund also being raised to a similar figure. They were placing , aS usual, £20,000 to their premises account,. The increase this year in this iteni was largely due, but not altogether to their new Manchester office,which Vas rapidly approaching completion. The cost of this new building when finished would certainly b 5 heavy,but it was situated in such a central part the city that they would have no difficulty in letting;a, off at very remunerative rents, the consider- .") portion of the building not required for the 'duct of their own business. They had already numerous applications from would-be tenants th 1 they calculated that with the amount La.?, would thus receive in rents,and the price they IA"; Rot fir their present premises, they would slly sit at a very little higher cost than they he!,6 at present doing in their inadequately-Med 411iding in King-street. Reviewing the current Months, notwithstanding a difference of very helrlY one per cent, in the Bank of England rate je"gi'een this half veer and the correspond- vieg 84 months for 1901, the average being £3 3s 6d vpaagainer, £4 29 6/1 a year ago,the net profit return 40,1 larger by a few thousands this time than a year (Appinuqe.) This was the more gratifying. ..nouch as he noticed a statement in this week's e°llomist" where out of a total of 24 banks three-</p>
        <p>able coto 4,4(1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>PANCY MEETING YOU.</head>
        <p>PANCY MEETING YOU.</p>
        <p>lust in time to tell you that Holdroyd's Gravel Pills Cr Zitanteed and positive cure for Lumbazo, Backache, Wind Gout, Kidney Diseases, &amp;e. Try one box, le °llenaieta. Your money promptly returned if not satiated. ;141311"D' MEDICAL H ILL, eleelcheaton, Yorks. "'a 12 stumps (1)</p>
        <p>fourths of the number showed a less profit for this current six months. Though he did not wish to pose as a pessimist, he much feared that the tend- ency would be for banking profits to grow smaller, relatively of course to the money employed, as the years passed on. He was very glad to see that the Parliamentary Committee on Savings Bank Funds had reported in favour of a reduction in the rate of interest allowed to depositors. It was true that the reduction pro- posed, even if larried out, was a miserably inade- quate one if only per cent, and a rate of 2 3-8 per cent, as it then would be, would still compete most unfairly with hankers, and certainly it would still result in a loss—though a modified one—to the tax- , payer, for if trained bankers who knew their busi- ness could not afford at all times to give 2 3-8 on deposits—as they knew they could not—it was quite certain that a Government department which might only invest in a2i security would not be able to do it without lose. However, he supposed they must be satisfied, or,at 'all events content with small mercies if only to the ext entlf per cent. To sum up, apart from their latest amalgamation, their business continued to show a gradual but a satis- factory and steady increase. Their deposits, irrespective of the Leicestershire figures, were larger, the number of accounts (again leaving out their new purchase), was greater by over 2,000 than it was at the beginning of the half-year, which was a very satisfactory condition of things. (Applause). Sir Jones J HARWOOD in seconding, observed that there was one thought which passed through his mind while Mr Parr was speaking, and that was the remark he made that he did not wish to be a pessimist, but that the profits in future would be smaller. He the speaker) hoped, however, the meeting would takc notice of the fact that if the volume of business increased com- mensurately with the diminished profits one would balance :the other. They must not take too serious a view of the fact that the profits of banks would be smaller. He bad great faith in the development of the business and he believed it would be in excess of what they contemplated. Looking at all the circumstances he was prepared to say the bank was in a sound.prosperous and healthy condition. The report was satisfactery,and the manner of conducting the business was also satis- factory, Mr Dun was giving more zeal to the affairs of the bank than he had seen him give for many years, and he said to Mr Neill that morning that it was a pity Mr Dun did not retire long since, for he seemed more zealous now than when he was in harness.. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>, TOO CHAIRMAN : Far be it from ma to throw cold water on Sir John Harwood's optimism. I hope his expectations will be realised, but don't run away with the idea that we are ever going to give more dividend. (Laughter.) The resolution wag carried unanimously. ELECTION OF THREE DIRECTORS.</p>
        <p>The errAtastAx moved the election of the Hon. Arthur Stanley, M.P., Mr Hussey Packe, tratd the Hon. Sir Charles William Fremantle, B.C. 8., as directors. Mr JOHN STONE seconded, and the resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>SPEECH BY THE MAYOR.</head>
        <p>SPEECH BY THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR of Warrington (Mr J Charlton Parr) moved a vote of thanks to the directors for their services during the past half-year. He felt sure, he said, he should be voicing the wishes of the shareholders here and else- where by emphasizing it most sincerely, and recording their grateful thanks to these gentlemen for the care and attention and good management they had displaced in the conduct of the business as evidenced by the excellent balance sheet they had been able to produce. (Hear, hear.) They wished also to extend a cordial wel- come to those directors who had honoured them with their presence that day, and to assure them that they highly appreciated the compliment. They also felt that it was a graceful tribute to the birth- place of the bank, and also useful in this way—that it was an evidence of their interest and consideration for their country constituents and aharehelders, for, after all, though the head office was in London, and the important business might all be centred there, the country and the provinces supplied to a great extent the sinews of war in the shape of deposits and reliable, funds. tie therefore thought it was a wise policy on the part of the directors to have the half- yearly meeting out of Londcn,and thus show considera- tion and regard to the interests and feelings of their country supporters and shareholders, on whom they were so largely dependent for the confidence and sup- port essential to the successful conduct of this immense business. (Hear, bear and applause). Mr WILLIAM OwEsi, as a Warrington man, said he fully appreciated the remarks which had fallen from the lips of the Mayor of Warrington, and he dad much pleasure in seconding the motion, which was arried. THE SEAFF TELINIKED.</p>
        <p>In moving a resolution of thanks to the staff for their services, the Hon A STANLEY said he looked upon it as particularly fortunate that they should have found in their present general manager such an able successor to Mr Dun. Even in this case they were in a happier position than banks sometimes found themselves in. They sometimes had to make a choice, and it was a case of " How happy could I be with either, were Vother dear charmer away "—(laughter)—but in this case they had both the dear ckarreers with them still, as Mr Dun re- mained on the board to give them his assistance,whieh, they were told, was now of a more zealous character ; that was a double-handed compliment. (Laughter). Mr CHARLES WRITE, in seconding the vote of thanks, observe(' that the shareholders of that neighbourhood would have great pleasure in receiving the general manager, who was well-known to them, as was his family before him. If anyone could have been preferred as successor to Mr Dun, Mr R W Whalley was the one. (Hear, hear.) The motion was carried.</p>
        <p>Mr WHALLEY acknowledged the compliment on be- half of the staff and remarked that he thought he might fairly say that all did their work well, and what was more, they lead the interests of the bank at heatt (Hear, hear.) With regard to the too-flattering remarks respect- ing himself, he could only repeat what he bad said be- fore, Wit it was his intention as far as possible to con- tinue the same wise policy which had been pursued so ably and successfully by his predecessor. His task was made lighter in this respect by the help of a very able assistant general manager. Mr Stewart. They had also at hand other promising lieutenants. (Rear, hear) THE CHAIRMAN'S SERVICES ACKNOWLEDGED. Ur A BAINES, in moving a vote of thanks to the chair. man, observed that he had not had the pleasure ol attending a meeting before,but Mr Parr had given them an exceedingly interesting statement with regard to the position of the bank at the present time, and he thought their heartiest thanks were due to him for presiding over the meeting. As one of the directors of Fares's bank he wished to acknowledge the kind way in which Mr Parr had spoken of their businese in Leicestershire. The figures quoted in connection with their business bad been before them and Mr Parr had told them that the bank had been one of. the most successful banks in the Midland Counties. Certainly if not the oldest it was one of the oldest, and though there might have been on the part of some a sentiment of regret that the old name of Pares should be swallowed up, they were struck by a remark of the Chairman in London that it was the fate of country banks either to eat or be eaten. And it was their fate to be either eaten or amalgamated with a first class bank. He acknowl.elged the courtesy and the evtreme kindnese which the directors of Pares's Bank, had received from the directors and managers of Parr's Bank during the time of the negotiations, while they made investigations in the same way that the directors of Parr's Bank made their investigations. They were received with absolute courteey and kindness, and all information necessary tor the amalgamation of the bank was put before them. It Was only due to the directors to state this. They felt they had joined one of the best banks in the country, being continued in a r and they looked forward to their Leicestershire business upnerteruyelienoensclaistitoeore niltanwdouwlhdiebhe carried en on the sampeo country made it so successful. They looked forward to continued prosperity in Leicestenhire. (Hear, bear.) Mr JOEL WAINWEiGHT said he esteemed it a privilege to second this resolution, and though he occupied enly a secondary position he could assure them no one could feel more impressed than he had been by what he, had seen and heard. He was a comparative stringer to Warrineton, but he knew that the name of Parr was hardly ever mentioned in tbe town or in the county without respect and affection. He had been in the habit of attending bank meetings for half a century, and he was bound to say that he never heard a more careful or more interesting statement from a Chairman, and if Mr Parr would not think it impertinent he would add never had he heard a speech delivered with such care and modesty. (Applause.) the re- itortßank for re- solution. o.lno.isNuCeoliitzibshe:nd tao cbuestaolml allowed e do ftopslumpsp, more than 50 years. He was sorry they were being swallowed up by Parr's, but he did not think it would be found that the Leicesterehire besineee would be such tahcalotairtgewaosultdo lbeecsae. nmtehaends o vif dividend. H augmenting trusted re ther it. (Hear, hear). The resolution was heartily agreed to.</p>
      </div>
