      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN POLICE APPOINTMENT.</p>
        <p>The position vacated by the transferment of Inspector Morgan to Lytham has been filled by the appointment of Inspector Foster, of the Lonsdale, South Division,who has been stationed at Carnforth, and is promoted from sergeant. Inspector Foster will shortly take up his duties in the district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>FATAL: COLLIERY ACCIDENT AT GOLBORNE.</head>
        <p>FATAL: COLLIERY ACCIDENT AT GOLBORNE.</p>
        <p>Peter Speakman, aged 17, a lasher-on, of 117, Revell-street, Platt Bridge, was following his em- ployment in the 6ft mine, Main's Pit ot the Barn- furlong collieries, near Golborne, on Monday, when he was caught between two tubs and fatally in- jured. He was at once conveyed in the ambulance to the Wigan Infirmary, where he expired on arrival. A NElrrox HUSSAR INJURED IN CAMP.</p>
        <p>Trooper William Keay, aged twenty two, a mem- ber of the C Troop (Newton-le-Willows) of the Lancashire Hussars Imperial Yeomanry met with an accident in the camp at Southport on Monday. It appears that while he was forming into column on the shore during exercises he was kicked by a horse and sustained a compound fracture of the leg. He was removed to the Infirmary for treat- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>BURTONWOOD FCHOOL BOARD,</head>
        <p>BURTONWOOD FCHOOL BOARD,</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held on Thursday last. Present—Messrs W Fairclough (chairman), R For- shaw (vice-chairman), W Southern, T Forster, Rev A M Mitchell, and W Webster (clerk) It was resolved that as the day now fixed for the Corona- tion falls within the holidays there be nothing done to celebrate the event, but that the question of an additional holiday in accordance with the wish expressed by the King be considered liter on. The preliminary arrangements for the coming session's evening school were considered, and the Clerk was directed to send each member a copy of the school- master's recommendations before the next meeting, The treasurer's book showed receipt of £77 18e 10d on the overseers. Goon TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The Hope of Earlestown Lodge 1644 held their usual weekly meetiug at their new quarters,the Co- operative Hall, Earle-street,on Monday night. One new member was initiated. The following officers were elected for the next quarter :—C T, Bro Hatton; VT, Sister Rowley ; Sec, Bro Greening; Assistant Sec, Bro Newman; Fin. Sec Sister Hatton; Treas. Bro J Warburton; Chaplain, Sister Naylor; Mar, Bro Dodd; D Mar, Sister Barlow ; Bro Peers; Scri, Bro Anderton ; PC T, Bro Cutler ; Pianist, Sis E Woods ; Assist Pianist, Bro Roberts; E S and Reg, Bro Clarke. Anyone wish- ing to join the order can do so any Monday night at above Hall.</p>
        <p>Sr. PETREL'S CHURCH DAY AND SUNDAY SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The annual field day and treat to the schOlars attending the above schools took place on Wednes- day. The weather opened rather threatening, but fortunately it kept fine. The children to the num- ber of about 400 assembled at tho school at 2.30 and headed by the Farm School band marched in procession through the principal streets of Newton to the residence of Mrs Smith, Brookside where a couple of hymns were sung. The children after- wards proceeded to the church where a brief ser- vice was conducted by the Rev J Ryder. Tea was served in the schools, he wants of the children be- ing atteuded to by the teachers. After tea the scholars made their way to a field kindly lent by Mr W Longton and here they enjoyed themselves io various games, prizes being awarded for racing, skipping-rope competitions, &amp;c., whilst the band played for dancing until dusk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>MIS-810N CHOIR- PICNIC.</head>
        <p>MIS-810N CHOIR- PICNIC.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the choir of the Brunswick-road Mission, together with several teachers of the Sun- day school, and friends, had their annual picnic. The party numbering about 40 left Earlestown at 8.45. in a reserved carriage for Chester where they arrived about 10 o'clock. The time between then and noon was spent in a visit to the Cathedral and rambles about the ancient City Walls. Dinner was partaken of at Williamson's Restaurant, and after- wards the party sailed up the Dee in the direction of Eccleston Ferry, returning to tea at the Res- taurant, which was served at six o'clock. The homeward journey was commenced at 8,15, and Earlestown was reached at 9.30. The araangements were admirably carried out by Mr R Macklin secretary DISTRICT COUNCIL OF'FICIALS' JAUNT.</p>
        <p>The officials of the Newton Urban District Coun- cil had their second annual picnic on Saturday, their destination this year being Llandudno. Leav- ing Newton-le-Willows at 8.50, they landed in Liverpool shortly before 930, and left the Landing- stage at 11 o'clock by the St. Tudno for Llandudno. The weather was of anything but an enjoyable character, rain falling almost during the whole of the passage, and although the sea was somewhat choppy none of the party suffered from mal de mer. Dinner was partaken of on the boat and being well served was thoroughly enjoy ed. Llandudno was reached at 1.45, and as it was still raining, the time had to be spent under cover as best they could. 1 he homeward voyage was commenced at 5.15, and tea was served immediately on starting. Liverpool was reached at 8 o'clock, and the party returned to Earlestown at various times, some remaining in Liverpool until a late hour. Mr E Cleworth had charge of the arrangements. WOODWORKERS PICNIC FROM EARLESTOWN. On Saturday afternoon the workmen employed in tha wood machine shop of the Viaduct Works had an outing to Gateacre, but the pleasure was considerably marred by the inclemency of the weather. Leaving Earlestown at half-past 12 in a char-a-bane, the party numbering about 40,reached Gateacre shortly after three o'clock, and put up at the Bridge Inn situated in tbe heart of the country. A bowling handicap for prizes was indulged in, the winners being D Sherlock (1), T Davies (2) and J Rice (3). A well served knife and fork tea was afterwards done full justice to, and several members enjoyed a rural walk about the district. A start for home was made after 8 o'clock, Earlestown be- ing reached at 11 30. ASSAULT ON THE COLLINS GREEN STATION</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>MASTER.</head>
        <p>MASTER.</p>
        <p>Mr William Dawson, the esteemed stationmaster at Collins Green, was subjected to a dastardly assault on Saturday night, by which he suffered considerable personal injury. On the arrival of the 10.33 p.m. train trom St. Helens Mr Dawson stood at the gate taking tickets, when a man of rough appearance came up to him. Finding that he would not be allowed to pass without producing a ticket it is stated that he struck the stationmaster in the face. Mr Dawson fell to the ground and was picked up. On examination it was found that he was suffering from a broken jaw, whilst three or four teeth bad been broken. His assailant managed to make his escape and was not seen again in the dis- trict.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>-EARLESTMYN WESLEYAN CHOIR SERMONS.</head>
        <p>-EARLESTMYN WESLEYAN CHOIR SERMONS.</p>
        <p>The annual sermons in aid of the choir connected with the Earlestown Wesleyan Church were preach- ed on Sunday. The Rev William Burgess was selected as the preacher, and the thoughtful words which he uttered will not be forgotten for some time by those who were privileged to hear him. Mr Burgess is a returned missionary, having spent 30 years in India, and he is now stationed at Finsbury Park, London. At the Wesleyan Conference, how- ever, he was appointed to the Italian Wesleyan Mission, and he will shortly take up his residence at Rome. In the morning the anthem "0 sing unto the Lord," was rendered with great taste by the choir. whilst at the evening service the musical portion was a special treat. The two anthems "The wilderness shall blossom as the rose " and " Praise the Lord "were extremely well rendered by the choir, whilst a pleasing effect was produced by the quartette " God is a spirit." Miss Lillie Barr gave a tuneful rendering of " The Gift," whilst "The Better Land "was capably rendered by Miss Olive Roberts. Mr G W Kidd presided with his ...Puel efficiency at the organ. The collections amounted to ever ten guineas. 30,000 BreLstOlNTZPbyBWLohll77le6rloll;oerird-etirgeBl Manchester. The most successful Eye Specialist in , England. He has cured after Hospitals, Doctors, and oculists have railer Book poet free on mentioning this paper.—A.dvt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIOA T AUGUST 1, 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIOA T AUGUST 1, 1902</p>
        <p>THE NEW WATER WORKS:</p>
        <p>The sinking operations in connection with the new well at the Newton District Council Water Works are progressing favourably. A depth of about 128 feet has been reached and when completed it is anticipated that an excellent supply of water, even to the higher reaches of the town, will be obtained. AUGUST BANE HOLIDAY IT EARLESTOWN._</p>
        <p>_ The tradespeople of EarleAown at a meeting held in the Town Hall on Tuesday evening decided to observe the August Bank Holiday on Monday by closing their premises. The various works are ig- noring the holiday, but they will close for three days from Thursday evening next. If the weather is fine there is likely to be a very large exodus of holiday makers, especially from the Viaduct Works in connection with the quarter fare privilege.</p>
        <p>CYCLING ACCIDENT AT EARI:ESTOW—N. On Tuesday morning two young fellews hailing From Gerrard's Bridge, St Helens were riding along Crow-lane Earlestown about seven o'clock when they had rather a serious experience. One of them through the collapse of his machine was thrown headlong into the road and received very serious injuries to his head and body. He was rendered unconscious. Assistance was obtained and he was carried to Dr Latham's surgery where he remained three hours insensible. Later on he was removed home and on Wednesday was reported to be in a dangerous state.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>WIDNES HOTEL MYSTERY.</head>
        <p>A WIDNES HOTEL MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>Inquest and Verdict.</p>
        <p>At the Accident Hospital, Widnes, on Wednesday morning, Mr Coroner Brighouse held an inquest on the body ot an unknown man, of respectable appearance, who was found dead in bed at the Central Hotel on Sun- day. Mr BEnnet, chemist, Victoria-road, stated that the man called at his shop on Saturday night. Witness did not recognizs him for a minute or so, but liter called him to mind al a man named Lithom, who hid b ;en in his employ. He appeared quite reserved, and told him he had been to America. Deceased asked !for a "pick- me-up," which was supplied. He got no morphia. The police reported that every endeavour to get the rody identified bad been made. Miss Forster, daughter of the landlord of the hotel, said deceased came to the hotel at a quarter to ten on Saturday night and asked for a bed. As he had no luggage with him, she asked for a deposit, which was paid. He retired to bed at eleven o'clock. Nothing unusual was seen about him, and he Was sober when he went to bed. _ _</p>
        <p>Florence Derbyshire spoke to showing deceased to his room. He said he would be " lying in ' for a while in the morning. Mrs. Forster, wife of the landlord, said that at noon on Sunday she knocked at the room door, and received no answer. She went in the room afterwards,and found the man asleep. She shook him, but could not rouse him. From the look on his face, she went down for her husband and sent for the police. Police-constable Rutter stated that he was called in about 320 on Sunday afternoon. He found deceased n bed, undressed, in a crouched position. The glass croduced was lying on the dressing-table, and upon further search a small packet containing a fine white powder. was found glistening in the sun..</p>
        <p>Dr M'Leonan said he was called in and found the man dead. Tha body was taken to the mortuary, and a post-mortem examination made. The membranes of the brain were congested. The left lung was healthy, but the other was affected by plurisy.The heart was healthy In his opinion, death was due to some narcotic poison. The liquid found in the stomach was red in colour, and was quite oonsistent with morphia poisoning. Deceased must have suffered a great deal of pain. He might have taken the morphia to produce sleep. The deceased, being a chemist's assistant, must have been cognisant of the proper quantity to take. The jury returned an open verdict.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Newton Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Newton Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY—Before Colane' Wood and Mr Collingwood. CRUELTY TO A HORSE AT WARRElGTolf.—Joseph Shaw was charged with cruelty to a horse at Wlnwick, near' Warring- ton, on the 13th July. Shaw. who is a travelling gip sy, failed to appear. The evidence of the prosecution Was to the effect that the defendant's horse, which was dragging a heavy van, was suffering from lamlnitis and was very lame. A fine of 23s and costs was Imposed. NEWTON REFORMATORY LAD'S ESCAPADE—Thomas Cowell, of Liverpool Farm School lad, was charged fwith stealing. on July 23rd, a pony and trap and 8s 6d in money,the property John Way 326, Warcrhve-road. Newton thee Wiy• well proved giving the lad Is 6d In money and sending him to Walker's Brewery for malt grains.—William Forster, a carrier, of 44, Bismark-street, Liverpool, said the defendant came to his stable ori the 23rd instant. He stabled his horse here for the night, and paid witness Is. Defendant at so paid. witness ls on the nest night, and remarked that •4 the load of furniture for which he had come was not ready." He next saw him in the custody of the pollee.—Police-constable Clarke stated that when he charged the defendant he replied, •• I spent the money, the pony and cart are all right,"—The defendant now admitted taking the money—Mr Digweed said that Cowell was a simple sort of lad, and he thought this job would not suit him. He had still a few months to stay in the school.—The charge of stealing the pony and trap was withdrawn, and the bench took a lenient view of the case, committing the defendant to prisdn far one day, and ordered him back to school.</p>
        <p>BAD LANGUAGE AT NEWTlN.—Williani Moody, Thomas Thompson and George Brooks, were charged with contraven- ing the bye-laws of the London and North Western Railway Company by using obscene and abusive language at Newton- le-Willows Station on the 6th of June. Moody did not appear. The others pleaded guilty,—Mr Benne, solicitor, stated that the defendants were passengers from Wigan way, their disorderly conduct led the guard to speak to them. Whilst on the platform at Newton-le-Willows, where they had to change,theyused most abominable language in theipre sence of women and children;and when remonstrated with by a paseenger,they threatened to assault him. Three passengers had voluntarily attended court to give evidence; they were so disgusted at the conduct of the defendants.—The bench fined the defendants.—The bench fined the defendants 20s and costs,and the chairman remarked that they were obliged to the company for bringing the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>TILE TOWN CLERIC OF ST. ElEtris.</p>
        <p>Mr Bailey was the recipient on Saturday of a pleasing token from the staff of his office on his bidding them farewell, the present being a pair of silver candlesticks. Hopes were expressed that Mrs Bailey's health would soon be completely restored as a result of living in Bournemouth. Mr Bailey declared that he would always have pleasing recollections of his service in St Helens SAD DROWNING FATALITY.—A BRAVE BOY.</p>
        <p>Thomas Featherstone, aged ten,of 3, Barton-street, St Helens, and Thomas Stanchion, a companion, were dis- porting themselves on a raft on a pool of water near Sibby's Copper Works, on Tuesday afternoon, when Featherstone slipped off the raft, and unfortunately pulled Stanchioa into the water as well. The only per- son abmt at the time was a boy of eleven, named Herbert Pierce, who with great gallantry walked into the water until be reached Stanchion, and, although the water at the spot was nearly up to his shoulders, by great good fortune Pierce managed to get out of the pool, taking Stanchion safelv to the side. By this time Featherstone had sunk,and ttrugh Pierce at once ran for Police-constable Hayes,from Pilkington't works,Featber- ton e was dead when pulled cut. - FATALITY AT BOLD COLLIERY. _ .</p>
        <p>Joseph Jones, collier, aged thirty-three, of 5, Assam- street, Sutton, St. Helens, met with a shocking death on Wednesday at Bold Colliery. He finished work in the afternoon at a point a considerable distance frAm the pit bottom; and was seen making his way towards the shaft. He was then in a m)tion brow in which two full waggons were being hauled: Afterwards be was found lying dead on the second box, with his head very badly injured. It appeared that he had hem riding on the boxes, and had been crushed by the low roof. ST. HELENS STOKER'S SHOCKING DEATH. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse, county coroner, held the inquest all Peasley Cross, St Helens, on Thursday, on Frederick Ward, of Fleet-lane, a stoker at the Ashton' s Green Colliery, who died on Tuesday morning from injuries received on Saturday. The deceased was attending to one of the boiler fires, when be knocked over a paraffin lamp with his rake, and the burning oil set him an fire. —The jury returned a veraict of "Accidental death," and recommended that colza oil be used in place of paraffin, which they considered far too inflammable for such purposes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>CORONATION.</head>
        <p>THE CORONATION.</p>
        <p>Continued from Pa gp 3. RUNCORN URBAN COUNCIL CORONATION R X PENSMS,</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Runcorn Urban Celinell was held on Wednesday. Mr R fi Posnett in the chair. There were &amp;No preen t M.sars J D Riahrown, E Gatley, W Handley, E Imison, T JoneS, .1 Pritchard, J J Shaw,and wiswall with Mr F A Lake (law clerk), Mr Marathale (secretary), and Mr J Wilding (.urveyor).--&amp; resolution was passed that orders be made on the treasurer for sums amounting to £2O in rpaprct of exoenscs incurred in connection with the coronation of Ms Majesty King Edward VII.</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GrAnx BOOTS AID tSHOES. UNDER THE CLOCK', BRIDOE-STREET.—Advt. H M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC 18 THE KM OF TONICS —63, SANICEY-STERET, Warrington.—(Advt.) SEVERAL SECOND-HAND BICYCLES for se cheap. GARLAND, Ironmonger. Bridge-et, Warringtott.—Advt. ASK for CUNNINGHAH'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—A 4. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR GOOD BOOTS. A TWO-FEET TREAT step inside a pair of ALT TAYLOR'S easy fitting boote.—Ailvt. BEST BOOTS roa ALL.—UNDRI THE CLOCK.—Advt</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE.</head>
        <p>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE.</p>
        <p>ITS MARVELLOUS EFFECT UPON THE STOMACH LIVER, KIDNEYS &amp; BOWELS. . . . . .</p>
        <p>SEAWEED possesses a natural strengthening, healing and purifying power, far greater than other known remedies. This is now conceded by important authori- ties. It was first introduced by Mr. Veno as one of the ' ingredients of Veno's Seaweed Tonic, since which time Veno's Seaweed Tonic has been used in hospitals and by doctors themselves because of its superior curative pro- perties. Its most,brilliant effect is produced in stomach, liver and kidney diseases, and tie extraordinary cures iz performs, even in the worst cases, is writive proof of its efficacy. It is a god-send to sufferers from indiges- tion, wind, headache, general weakness, kidney trouble, weak and painful back, torpid liver, female troubles, poorness of blood and HABITUAL CONSTIPATION. Ask for VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC. Price le. lid. and 28. 9d. at chemists and medicine vendors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>7Lttter6 tot. the Ebttor.</head>
        <p>7Lttter6 tot. the Ebttor.</p>
        <p>Sectarianism and its Failures.</p>
        <p>Sir,—As the battle of the sects is still raging round , the Government Education Bill a few observations on :the failures of Sectarianism itself may not be without ' interest at the present time if I can ehow that the sects, after all, are but poor exponents of the faith once de- livered to the saints, and hardly worth contending for. Sectarianism has had a long and somewhat chequered career in this country, and may be roughly defined as Christianity according to Rome, Canterbury, and Dissent in general, rather than after the teaching and example of its divine founder. / And yet, notwithstanding these divisions, each sect claims to be a Church of Christ with a duly qualified , and ordained ministry. Serious and unmistakable objections at once apdear to prevent our endorsement of this large, and important claim, and foremost amongst these are the divisions to which I have adverted. Instead of exhibit- ing the unity and brotherly love which the Lord desired should ever be the distinguishing features of his follow- ers in all ages, Sectarianism everywhere produces jneaasl obueseyn, omf tue tnu pa al idniesdt r atins td asnmd bitterzed at t s pa encdt at chlee wofo riltda r) e the s' partisans persecuting and killing one another in His name. Then as regerde the ministry, the discrepancy between the Lord's method of appointment and that of the &amp;otitis truly astounding. In a few humble and un- learned toilers on the Sea of Galilee, Jesus, who knew what was in man, found the instruments best fitted for his purpose. And these he gathered around him, and afterwards sent them forth without purse or scrip to preach the Gospel. But the ministers of sectarianism are generally drawn from the wealthy and idle classes and trained, not in the scheol of Christ, but in theological seminaries where they are grounded in the tenets of the respective sects. And when they leave these institutions they are " reverend" gentlemen, prepared to preach the gospel for salaries, little or big, according to the pecuniary circumstances of their flocke. And again, of another true sien of the true:church!the sects appear to be entirely lacking. "If theyjhave persecuted me, they will also persecute you," said Jesus to His disciples. But where is persecution for Christ's sake now to be found ! The modern church and the wcrld seem to leave so much in common that their I,!Ationt3 Ire generally quite friendly and agreeable, and hence the martyr's sufferings and the martyr's crown have long since become things of the past. Thus in three most important particulars the sectarian institu- tions are very far removed indeed from the Lord's idea of a Christian church. It now remains for me to point out two remarkable instances of failure in regard to their preaching. No Cbrietian man will deny that the doctrine of peace and goodwill among men lies at tne very root of the religion of Pelle. So important was this doctrine considered that its first announcement was made by angels from heaven, and it was the burden of the teach- ing of the Lord and his disciples. To prove that this vital, this gracious doctrine is not taught and pressed in our sectarian assemblies as it ought to be I need only refer to the late war in South Africa. Perhape never at any former period of English history *as this doctrine more shamefully treated than it was during that war. Ira a land full of churches and chapels and religious teachers of all kinds the spirit of war was absolutely triumphant. Its cruel voice reverberated through the Houses of Parliament; it raged and revelled in the streets, insulted and abused the friends of peace,and raised its head in our pulpits. And now, when the strife is ended and thousands of widows fatherless children, and maimed and battered remnants of the battlefields are reaping some of its awful fruits, the lads in our day and Sunday Schools are being trained in the use of weapons that they may be ready for the wars of the future. Surely the true, the logical infer- ence from all this is that sectarianism must either con- nive at the killing of men, or offer an opposition so weak and spiritless as to be of no practical value. That our conduct towards all our fellow creatures should be the same as we should rightly expect at their ' hands is another beautiful principle of Christianity. Compliance with this just law would soon change the whole lice of society for the better. Under its influence Christian wars would be impossible, and men in all con- ditions of life would treat each:otherjwith justice and kind ness. But alas," The golden rule," like toe rule of peace is only partially observed. In the churches of the sects where above all other places, we ehnuld expect to see it loyally carried out it is found that the rich men who put gold and cheques on "the plate "are highly esteemed and exalted, while the poor are more or less despised for their poverty. Even among the ministers of religion themselves there are some very striking anomalies in regard to this rule. The lower grades of eculesiastics who bear the burden and heat of the day must live as well as they can on very small stipends ; while their superiors, who certainly are more ornamental than useful, are often extravagantly paid. And when one turns to the industrial and commercial portions of the religious world the same partiality and selfishness are painfully apparent. How often have many of the humble producers and manipulators of the country's wealth to appeal to their rich employers for even " a living wage " ! And the carefully prepared statistics of Messrs Booth and Rowntree go far to show that such pitiful appeals too often 'all upon deaf ears and unsym- pathetic hearts. From the figures alluded to it appears that nearly a third of the inhabitants of this rich, Christian country are in a chronic state of poverty and hunger through lack of means to procure the bare necee- series of life. Surely it is no breach of charity to sty that all this injustice and misery are condemnatory of sectarian preaching. For if " the golden rule " were faithfully taught and insisted upon as a Divine com- mand, without fear or favour of men, the epectscle of boundless wealth and boundless poverty standing side by side would no longer he a discredit to the country as it is now. swill a remedy for the state of things which I have Po imperfectly described ever be found in eectarianiern. The pressing need of the age is less of that piebald article and more of the Christianity of Christ for the two thines are not synonymous. In sectarianism we have Christianity so blended and obscured by the creeds and ceremonies of men as to be sometimes almost un- recognizable. But Christianity itself is the love of Chriet and a few simple, heavenly principles implanted in the hearts of men, whereby they are enabled to do justly, love mercy, and walk humbly before God. Yours truly, lONA. July, 1902.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Help the Bufferlng:l</head>
        <p>Help the Bufferlng:l</p>
        <p>• Sir,—No higher or nobler impulse can possibly touch our hearts than the alleviation of human suffering in whatever form,and with the object of assisting the War- rington Infirmary and District Nursing Ameociation more fully be carry on their merciful work among the people the promoters of the forthcoming cycle parade:and carni- val moat earnestly appeal to everybody within the in- fluence of these institutions for their assistance in every manner possible with the view of insuring the most complete success of the work in hand. The highly.' satisfactory result of a similar effort for the same object in tt.e autumn of 1900 when the very handsome sum of £365 was banded over to the Infirmary encourages the cyclists of Warring- ton, kc, cricket clubs, harriers' clubs, football clubs, and other kindred organizations (who are responsible for thia movoment) in the hope that a still greater success may be achieved on this OCCtitiioa. Pages might be written,if necessary, in support of the claims of the Infirmary and Nursing Association to public assistance and sympathy; they are unquestionably the two institutions touching closest the whole community, and it is with every con- fidence that this appeal Is made to all classes in the town and surrounding districts by the PARADE AND CARNIVAL COWS' TTEE. (D. DAVIES, Chairman General Committee). Warrington, July 30th, 1902. Donations will be thankfully received and acknowledged by— MR. CHARLES LEIGH (Hon Treasurer), Bewsey Chambers, Warrington. MR. EDWARD PICKAVANCE, jun. (Hoe. General Secretary), V, Bridge-street, Warrington. MR. HAMPSON, Secretarv,the Infirmary,Warrington.</p>
      </div>
