      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>DEATH OF LORD GERARD.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF LORD GERARD.</p>
        <p>Lord Gerard. who had been ill for some weeks at his Kentish seat, Eastwell Park, Ashford. died on Wednes- day morning. In Lancashire the news was received with much regret, and the bell of the St. Oswald Roman Catholic Church, Ashton-in-Makerfield, was tolled. Among the inhabitants of the township his lordship was exceedingly popular. Lord Gerard, who has died at the age of 52, having been born on the 21st June, 1851, was the premier Roman Catholics baronet in the kingdom. The Gerard family has played a leading part in the history of Lan- cashire, and has suffered much in the persecutions of past times for its faith. The deceased peer was the second baron, the peerage having been conferred by Lord Beaconsfield in 1876, in pursuance of his policy of Catholic conciliation. The Gerards are one of the most ancient families in England, and can lay claim to long descent, tracing lineage right back to Otho, a Saxon lord of King Alfred's time. The baronetage goes hack to 1611, having been one of the first created. Lord Gerard was educated at the Oscott Roman Catholic College, and chose the army for a profession, being at the time of his (teeth a retired lieutenant of the 2nd Life Guards. In 1887 Lord Gerard married Mary Milner, the sister of Lord Durham and Mr Henry Milner. The heir to the estates is the Hon Frederick J Gerard, who is nineteen years of age. Lord Gerard succeeded his father in 1887, ten years after his marriage. Besides the son who now succeeds:the father there is a daughter, the Hon Ethel Gerard, who like her mother, is well- known in society. The wife of Lord Rosebery, the late Lady Rosebery,was godmother to the Hon Ethel Gerard, and with the customary generosity of the Rothschild family dowered her godchild with much largesse. It will be remembered that something like a year or more ago Society was much perturbed by Miss Gerard's jewels being stolen from her room while she was yachting at Cowes. Lord Gerard had two seats, Eastwell Park, Ashford, Kent, where he has died, and Gars wood Hall, Garswood, the ancestral home of the Gerard family.</p>
        <p>WITH ROLLER AT THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>When Lord Gerard retired from the army he did not give up military service by any means. He was the head and Colonel commanding the Lancashire Hussars, a fine body of Yeomanry originally drawn from the Gerard estatAs, and locally styled " Lord Gerard's men." When the late Siuth African war broke out he was one of the first to offer himself for active service, and he went out to the front with Sir Redvers Buller, serving as Aide-de-camp on Buller' personal staff, throughout the Natal campaign. He had the experience of having his Loran shot in the neck at Colman, hut, for- tunately, the rider escaped unhurt, and be had the honour of being mentioned in dispatches from the front for his services.</p>
        <p>On returning home be was accorded a public welcome by his native township, and, more than this, he had the honour of receiving the medal for distinguished services at the hands of the Bing. Lord Gerard's name was placed first on the list of staff officers in Lord Robert's final recommendations for distinguished services during the South African campaign. The King conferred upon Lord Gerard also the Order of the D.R.O. The Garswood estates are rich in coal seams, and the royalty from the black diamonds swell the coffers of the Gerard family to such an extent that the fortunate heir must be rich almost beyond the dreams of avarice. The deceased was chairman of the Newton Conserva- tive Association, and at one time played a prominent part in the division. He also took a deep interest in agriculture, and often presided at the meetings of the Makerfield Ploughing Society,one of the leading societies of it kind in the country.</p>
        <p>DROPPING THE _II FITZ."</p>
        <p>The family, as already stated, is one of the oldest in England, and has played, from century to century, a conspicuous part in public life. It was prominent in the Crusades of the 12th and 13th centuries, and still re- tains the memorial of its service in Palestine. In those days the family name was 'titzgerald. In the 13th Cen- tury the name was changed to its present form, " Fitz" was taken out, and Gerard, instead of Gerald, was sub- stituted. It was about this time that the family settled at Ince Hall, near Wigan. Sir Thomas Gerard attended Parliament in 1388 as oee of the knights from the shire of Lancashire, and he was High Sheriff of the country.</p>
        <p>CHAMPIINS OF QUEEN ALM.</p>
        <p>The adherence of the family to the faith of their fathers was very marked during the reigns of Edward VI. and of Elizabeth. It was this adherence which probably impelled Sir Thomas Gerard in 1750 to consider bow to effect the escape of Mary Queen of Scots, who was held prisoner at Chatsworth.</p>
        <p>The plot was discovered and Sir Thomas and his con- federates, Sir T Stanley and Lord Dudley, were lodged in the Tower of London. Sir Thomas Gerard wrote to the Privy Council that Queen Elizabeth had spared greater offenders, and be hoped she would not deal sharply with him. Elizabeth, however, took a serious view of the matter and Sir Thomas was fined so heavily that he was compelled to part with his manor Byrn-hill or Brindle. When the son of Mary Queen of Scots aseended the English Throne the Gerards reaped some rewards of their fidelity. They were amongst the first creations of baronets who were instituted to establish an English colony in the province of Ulster, in Ireland : and this was not the only Instance of the King's appreciation of the sympathy of the Gerards for his unfortunate mother</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>IN THE CIVIL WAR.</head>
        <p>IN THE CIVIL WAR.</p>
        <p>During the disastrous dispute between Charles I. and his Parliament. many of the Catholic families in this district took active interest in the Royalist cause. Richard Gerard was a distinguished cmlonel, and conse- quently shared the fortunes of his master. He himself raised and led an army of foot, and after many vicissitudes of fortune he was appointed cup bearer to the Dowager Queen...</p>
        <p>"- Charles ~ in his futile efforts to oust the Puritans, staved one night at" The Brynne " as the guest of Sir William Gerard. This was in August, 1650. During the stirring days of 1715 and 1745 the Gerards kept aloof from the madding strife of parties, and it was during this period that the family built the present splendid residence at Garswood, then called the New Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO MR SAMUEL</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR SAMUEL</p>
        <p>TAYLOR, J.P.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening at the Technical Institute, Run- corn, there was a largP gathering of Sunday School representatives for the purpose of presenting Mr S Taylor (Chairman of the Urban District Council) with a gold medal to commemorate his connection with the schools during the recent Coronation celebrations. Mr John Littler occupied the chair and he W 49 supported by the Rev J Osborne, M.A. Messrs F J Norman, S. fiPeßett, T Beck, T Simister, the representative 3 of the' Sunday Schools and others.</p>
        <p>Mr Littler in opening the proceedings paid * high tribute to Mr Taylor's public work in Runcorn and con- cluded:a pithy speech with expressions of esteem and regard.</p>
        <p>Mr. Beck, in explaining the object of their meeting, dwelt on the differentl kinds of presentations and the motives which promoted them. In Mr. Taylor'e case it was the highest esteem for his personal work which prompted the presentation. Everyone heartily wished Mr Taylor would have a long life to enjoy the esteem and good feeling of his fellow townsmen. They did not give the medal for its intrinsic value hut os a memento. Nothing wee more,remarkable than the unanimity with which they had:decided on the making of a presentation to him. He hoped in the days to come the medal would be a sources of pleasure to him. (Applause.) '0 Mr Simistere in making the presentation said he was pleased at the great honour which had been !Von- ferred upon him. It was an extremely interesting duty he had to perform. He was an old Sunday Schooll worker, and he considered Sunday schools were becoming more and more important. The rising youth required sympathy, and the Christian worker enthusiasm. Mr Taylor had always been intensely sympathetic. He knew many eases of trouble which Mr Taylor had privately relieved. They, as 1 schools, had never yet gone to Mr Taylor for financial aid and been refused. Be thanked God they had such a man in Runcorn. (Applause.) When the matter of the presentation was mooted there was only one opinion concerning it. The medal was not as valuable as they wished it to be. But it was not their intention to pay Mr Taylor but to give him some evidence of their appre- ciation of his kindness to them. The medal bore the inscription :—" Presented to Samuel Taylor, Eq., by the Sunday schools of Runcorn es a token of respect and in appreciation of his generosity on the eccasion of the Coronation of Edward VII. A .1902," On the ob- verse side were the faces of the King and Queen. In times of difficulty it was nice to have some memento indicative of the good will which helped a man to take heart again. He hoped Mr Taylor would still go forward to glorify God and benefit his fellow men. He had great pleasure in the name of tho,e3 he represented in present- ing Mr Taylor with the medal. (Apnlause.) Mr. Taylor in replying said he hardly knew how to express himself, but he thanked them all for their extreme kindness. They could not put the value on it that he would. (Applause.) The medal would be before his mind right through life. His people, sons, grand- sons and others, would appreciate it quite as much as he did. It always gave him ext•rerne pleasure to help any good Cause and to help those who could not help themselves. Everyone from the highest to the lowest , could do something towards helping those 1 around them. They had suffering humanity and many great causes which needed assistance ' Life was very short at the longest,and a kindly word of advice to those who needed it would always help them forward. He was pleased to think there were those who counted it a pleasure to work in the Sunday School. Others were fitted for public work. Al! could do some- thing. Be thanked them most sincerely for their kind- ness and he felt be could not express himself as he would like to do. (Applause.) Mr Beckett afterwards addreseed the meeting and a vote of thanks to the Chairman brought the meeting to a close.</p>
        <p>GREAT LIBERAL VICTORY AT</p>
        <p>LEEDS.</p>
        <p>Return of Mr Barran,</p>
        <p>The polling in North L,eds to fill the vacancy caused by the elevation of Mr W L Jackson to the peerage took place on Tuesday. The candidates were Mr Rowland Barran (Liberal) and Sir Arthur Lawson (Conservative). The resultlwas officially declared on Tuesday night as follows :</p>
        <p>Barran (L) 7,539 Lawson (C) -- Liberal majority Previous elections have resulted as follow :- 1885. 1892. Wl, Jackson (C.) ... 4,494 W L Jackson (C.)... 5,790 A W Rucker (L.) 4,237 T R Leuty (G. L.) 4,776</p>
        <p>Majority 257 Majority 1,014 1886. 1895. W L Jackson (C) A 0 Rutsoo (G L) 3,682 11 S Baines (R)</p>
        <p>Majority 619 Ddajority 1,503 1900. W L Jackson (C) 7,152 J C Hamilton (L) 4,955</p>
        <p>Majority 2,517</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above authority was held on Tuesday at Dutton Workhouse. 12dr J Stokes was in the chair and there were also preeent Messrs S Taylor, T Woods, J Douglas. the members of the Run- corn District Council with Mr W E Hough, (assistant clerk). J Canna' (master), T Cooke, T Riley, W DePrez (relieving officers).</p>
        <p>COST OF PAUPER LUNATICS. Mr A Hamby, clerk to Chester Asylum Committee. wrote informing the Board that the committee had fixed the rate of maintenance of pauper lunatics in the asylum fer the quarter ending September 30th next at 7s 7d per head per week. The Clerk reported that the amount was the same as last. quarter.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : That is very satisfactory. THE COST OF VACCINATION. "MONSTROUS," "RIDICULOUS" " SCANDALOUS." -. • .</p>
        <p>The following resolutions which had been passed at a conference convened by the St Giles, Camberwell, Board of Guardians were forwarded by the clerk, Mr Charles Stevens, who asked for the views of the Runcorn Board on the suggestions contained therein :—That in the opinion of this conference the fees for primary and re— vaxination performed at home should not exceed 2s per case. and 19 61 if at the surgery of the public vaccinator. That the present minimum sum of 9f and 31 should be a fixed rate in respect of the remuneration of vaccination officers.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : Something requires to be done in the matter.</p>
        <p>Mr Lioaker asked whether a doctor were allowed if he travelled outside the two miles radius to charge 7s 6d in each case if he vaccinated more than one inmate. The Clerk : Most deridedly under the contract. Mr Linaker : Then it is monstrous ! The Chairman : ft is ridiculous ! Mr Mclntyre: Scandalous !</p>
        <p>To matter was referred, after further discussion, to the Parliammtary Committee, on the proposition of Messrs Wright and Woodward.</p>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>It was reported that the following sums had been paid for out relief; Runcorn £B5 ; Budworth, £29 ; Frodsham £25.</p>
        <p>Passed on the proposition of Mr Wright. House COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the House Committee a vote of thanks was accorded to Mrs Holden, of Antrobus Vicarage for having the Friendly Society girls to tea on the 22nd July. A similar vote was passed to the Rev. Lyon Bennett for flowers at the flower service at the Iron church at Duttoo. VISIT OF THE MCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD INSPECTOR. Mr Moseley Williams chairman of the House Com- mittee reported that there were 147 inmates in the house compared with 140 last year. Mr Dansey, Inspector to the Local Government Board, had visited the house and made certain suggestions which would receive due attention.</p>
        <p>The report was approval on the motion of the Chair- man and Mr. 8 Taylor.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL POOR LAW CONFERENCE. It was resolved that Messrs Roylance and Wood and Messrs Mclntyre and Darlington as alternatives with the clerk be appointed as representatives to the North- Western Poor Law Conference at Preston on September 26th and 27th on the proposition of Messrs. Wright and Wcod ward.</p>
        <p>STRANGE POISONING CAGE AT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-08-01_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>A Woman's Fatal use of Drugs. Inquest and Verdict.</p>
        <p>The adjourned inquest into the circumstances of the death of Jane Grine (28), wife of a farm labourer at Halton Lodge, near Runcorn was resumed on Saturday, by Mr R Dolamon,coroner,and a jury of 18. The woman died on the 10th lust, three-quarters of an hour after swallowing, a drug, a portion of which, and also the contents of the stomach of the deceased, bad been sent to the county analyst for analyais. The duty of the jury, explained the coroner, was to determine for what purpose the deceased had taken the drug. Superintend- ent Ennion represented the police, and Mr Samuel H. Johnson, wholesale druggist, Whitecimpel, Liverpool, also watched the proceedings. Ernest Bond, 30, Russian-drive, Liverpool, said be was a legally-qualified chemist in the employ of Messrs J'FI and 9 Johnson, druggists, Whitechapel, Liverpool. On Thursday. the 10th inst., the firm received two letters from Halton, each ordering a quarter of an ounce of absinthe. He forwarded a quarter of an ounce of oil of absinthe to each address. Oil of absinthe was not a scheduled poison and was not regarded as a poison. A dose was one or two drops. There would be 120 drops in a quarter of an ounce. He did not indicate the dose upon the bittle. As far as his experience went absinthe was not a drug largely wad in England. The dru7, placed upon wool or any absorbent, would keep away moths and other insects. He did not know this practically, but from books.</p>
        <p>James Powell, farm labourer, Halton Lodge Cottages, reposed that on the 10th instant he was called home from the 6eld4 and found deceased lying in the yard of the house. He brough her some'brandy. Elizabeth Powell, wife of the previous witness, said the deceased obtained some oil of absinthe from John- son's, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>_ The Coroner : Wby did she want it P—She thought she was pregnant, and she wanted to stop it. Witness, continuing, said the drug canoe by the even- ing post, and shortly afterwards deceased came into her house and said she had taken the stuff. A few minutes later she found the deceased lying speechless in the yard. A juryman was questioning witness as to bow she and deceased obtained their knowledge ef the drug, but the coroner ruled that the questions could not be put, P.C. Upton described visiting the house. Deceased was lying on the kitchen floor dead. Mrs Powell said deceased had taken some oil of absinthe. Fie questioned Mr Grice.and he said he knew nothing about it. Wit- new' found in deceased's dress pocket a piece of paper with oil of absinthe and Messrs Johnson's address upon it. Mr Grice afterwards found the small bottle (pro- dueed) in a potato field. _ _ _ . _ _</p>
        <p>Dr Carter Bell, analyst to the county of Chester, ssid he had analysed the contents of two small bottles (Pro- duced). One bottle contained 3 grains of absinthe ; the other contained 21 grains. He had alsoanalysed the contents of the stomach of the deceased, and found 103 grains of oil of absinthe. This was more than sufficient to cause death.</p>
        <p>The Coroner Is it not a drug frequently met with ? No ; very seldom. _</p>
        <p>, Is it a poison P—l look upon it as a poison. 4111 You %re not a mYdical Ilman ?—No. I believe oil of absinthe is frequently used as an abortive. Questioned by Mr. Jobnson witness said be believed the oil was Men used for curing worms in children.</p>
        <p>Dr. Carruthers, recalled, stated that after hearing this evidence he was of opinion deceased was poisoned by oil of absinthe.</p>
        <p>The Coroner, in summing up, said there appeared to be a difference of opinion as to whether oil of absinthe was a poison or not, but there was no doubt that,taken in more than very minute doses, it would cause death. In his opinion it was very reprehensible to supply a drug like this without a direction as to the dose, and especi- ally to a person whose letter, he doubted not, disclosed that she was an illiterate person. Deceased administered the drug to herself when alone, and if she did so with the intention of producing abor- tion she committed self. murder. Applications were frequently made to medical men and druggists to procure abortion without any ides of the criminality of the eV'. That it was wicked and criminal could not be too strongly emphasised. If any person had administered the drug to the deceased with intent to procure abortion, that person would have been guilty of murder, but that point did not arise in this case. If the deceased intended to take the drug medic- inally for a proper and legitimate purpose, then she died by misadventure from an overdose. The jury returned a verdict that death was caused through deceased taking an overdose of oil of absinthe to cause abortion, and recommended that such adanger- OuS drug should be scheduled as a poison. The Coroner The logical consequence is that she feloniously killed herself.</p>
        <p>The Foreman (Mr Walker) : We are satisfied it was taken by mistake. The woman aid not know the quantity.</p>
