        <p>A Child's Singular Death. An inquiry was conducted at the Police Station on Thursday evening into the circumstances attend- ing the death of Thomas Henry B Mellor, aged five years, son of George H B Mellor, painter,lB, Napier" street.—Deceased's father stated that at aboat five. o'clock on Monday evening, the 14th of July. deceased come borne and complained that abd named William Mora.. 1.. a throws a stone at air and hit him on the forehead. Witness Saw that 131.3 right temple was slightly swollen, but did not think it would be of much consequence. Deceased did not appear to be any the worse for it until the following 'Wednesday, when his forehead became very much swollen. He was taken to the Infirmary where the doctor lanced his temple and allowed him to go home. On Friday he became worse and witness called in Dr Peacocke who found that ery. sipelas bad set in. The doctor attended aeceased until his death, which took place on Monday last. Deceased, since he said that Morgan hit him with a stone, said it was done by a little boy in the school yard—Dr Peacocke said death was due to erysipelas which could have been set up in the manner stated by the deceased.—Detective Barlow (Coroner's officer), said be had made inquiries at the school but could obtain no information as to how deceased came by his injury. Tb 3 boy Morgan said he knew nothing about it—The jury returned a verdict that deceased died from erysipelas but there was no evidence te show bow it was set ap.</p>
