        <p>THE Werrington and Mi F-Cheshire Examiner, pub , lished every Saturday morning. , -rHE Runcorn Examine-, published! Friday after- noon. -1- noon. It is the only Weekly Paper in the district. and offers the best and cheapest facilities to advertisers. THE Widnes Examiner, published Friday -a- after- noon. Widneecontains the largest number of Chemical Works of any town in the kingdom, and s rapidly becoming , 51 important centre of industry. THE Earlestown and Newton Examiner, published every Friday aternoon. THE St. Selena Examiner, published every Friday afternoon. THE EXAMINEE SERIES PREPAID ADVERTISEMENTS APARTMENTS WANTED HOUSES WANTED $ 4:PIETMENTB TO LET HOUSES To BE SOLD , &amp;RTICLES LOST MISCELLANEOUS WANTS ART/CLEB FOUND MISCELLANEOUS SALES One Three Six Words. Week. Weeks. Weeks. 18 ............... Os. 6d. ... is. 3d. ... Zs. Od, 17 16 45 54 JOHN NV ALKER AND CO. . . ----.--------------------- lIIE EXAMINER. AUGUTT lit. 1902 "._-_____ _--------------------'_.___-_.---- IT is hardly auspicious Carried by Closure that Mr. BALFOUR should celebrate his accession to . the Premiership with an attempt to carry the 3 most obnoxious clause of the Education Bill by force. A party which has wan its majority by a tricky appeal to the electorate on a wholly differ- ent issue owes a certain responsibility to the country. Five electors in every nine were unfor- tunately of opinion during the spurious excitement of October, 1900, that Ar. CHAMBERLAIN should „ be allowed to finish the war he had begun. t. Iliad their opinion been asked on the question i whether Mr. Balfour should be permitted to , destroy S'.:hool Boards, and endow sectarian education from the rates, the verdict might have been very different. Even as things are, it is doubtful if votes could Iv given on the merits of the question, without regard to the exigencies of party, whether Mr. Beteoue 1 . could command a majority in the House of Commons. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, absorbed though he is in Colonial affairs, has never renounced ' the views he held when he founded the Bir- reingham School Board, with Dr. DALE as his ally. No less than eighty Unionist members have signed a memorial against the control clauses of the Bill; and, though they seem to lack the Courage to vote as they think, their views are ,voiced from time to time by men like Sir MICHAEL ' I'OST ER and Mr TeirroN. If the question were really settled by a frank appeal to the division Lobbies, Mr BILFOTJR Could only hope to carry his point by the votes of the eighty Irish Catholics. . There never was less excuse for Mr BALF OUR'S resort to the closure, and his attempt to dispose of over one hundred amendments without debate in a couple of all-night sii tings. The mischief is that an appeal to force in Parliament inevitably Provokes a reaction in the country. A religious dispute was never yet settled by a majority vote. THERE are many large principles at stake in the debates on this vital seventh clause. There is first the old constitutional maxim that public Illotley must be under public control. The whole Working expenses of the Church Schools, from the teachers' salaries downwards, are now to be paid from the taxes and the rates. In return, the com- munity is only allowed to appoint one-third of the members of the boards of management. Then, again, there is the democratic principle. The rate- payers nowhere exercise a direct control. The responsible body (the County Council) does not administer the rates which it levies, and the various committees which do exercise a sort of control are composed of nominees. But it is not on these abstract questions, grave though they are, that the really angry objection to the Lill turns. It involves a concrete injustice to !. individuals. There are 8,000 parishes in England, as Mr. Leovn-GeoueE remined the House of Corn- tams, in which the Church School is the only school. To this school Dissenters are compelled to send their children, andto its upkeep they are compelled to contribute. But if its managers °lose to use their majority of four in six to main- tain an exclusively clerical atmosphere by recruiting the teaching staff entirely from High 4-laglicans, the locality has no means of redress elhaeover. It is an utterly intolerable situation. 'Why the Church which only finds the building I%'hile the locality pays the actual expenses of teaching should be allowed to appoint a two- thirds majority of the managers is by no means vious. To reverse the proportions, as the 'llsior• of Hereford suggests, would only be equit- able. But the easiest and the fairest solution is that whith the Australian Colonies have adopted. was a Unionist, Professor Sir MICHAEL FOES- :ts who made this suggestion in the House of , °Mmons, but it would probably commend itsel 40 tolerant men of all parties. Let the secular</p>
