        <p>DEATH OF MR. JOHN EVISON• A Prominent Friendly Society THE FUNERAL The funeral of the late Mr Emsnn took place on Thursday afternoon,smid many manifestations of regret The first part of the burial service was conducted in St Paul's Church, at which Church Mr Emson was a member of the congregation. The Rev W Braceoamp conducted the service, and delivered a sympathetic address. Mr Amos presided at the organ and the choir was in attendance and led the singing of the 39th Psalm and the by mn " A few more years shall rol'." At the close, the procession was re-formed and proceeded to the Cemetery, where the interment took place. The chief mourners were Messrs Fred,James,:and Edward Emson (sons), Mr Albert Mitchell, Derby (son-in-law), Mr William Caldwell (brother-in-law,) Mr W Sill (brother- in-law), Mr Herbert Emson (nephew), Messrs Bibby, D Whitham, Joseph Crosby, Henry Longehaw, H Bloom- field, D Houghton, and G Maybury (the two last-named representing the Friendly Societies (Council. )The Gilbert Greenall Lodge of Freemasons was represented by Bros James Parkinsou,W.M.,J R Griffiths,P.M., Joseph Shill- cock, P.M., Adam Webster P.M. CG McDowell, 8.D., J Hedgcock, A Jolley, W J Shaw, F Dell, 11 Shackleton ' and T J Hulse, and the Rev B Percival (chaplain). Bros F Howard, W.M , and H F Greenough, P.M., repre- sented the Lodge of Lights, and Bro F Bennie, IS.. the Makerfleld Lodge. The Independent Order of Odd- fellows (Manchester Unity), was represented by Bros T S Steel, Prov. G.M., E It Clare. Prov. D.G,M., S Wads- worth, D.S., A Webster' F J Whittle, T Rigby, F Webster and M Knowles, P.P. G.1.112e, F Taylor, T J Gornall, E Powell and H Taylor, P.G.'s, and T Brad- , shim. P.V. Mr Philip Garnett was also present to show a last mark of respect. Wreaths were sent by the following :—Widow and family, Jennie and Albert, Fred and Ada, Mr and Mrs Whitharn, Mu. Mawron and Stanley, Mrs Culm and Annie. Mr Crosby and family, Mrs Clare and family, Mr J W Drown, an old friend David, Mr and Mrs Chrimes, Mr and Mrs Teece and family, sewing girls and Mrs Wrigley (Messrs R Garnett and Son's),Admiral NfiEloo Lodge,fellow officers in the Manchester Unity of Oddfellows, the Friendly Societies' Council, Mr and Mrs Boyle, Mrs Gittins and family, and Mrs Loy. The funeral arrangements were in the bands of Messrs Mounfield and Sons and Mr H F Greenough supplied the carriages.</p>
